const RCHANGES="# Rudolf OS changes\n\nEach version's first line after \"Summary:\" is what Settings shows before an update is installed; the list is what\n\"What's new\" and the notification after an update show. The newest version comes first.\n\n## 1.6.2 (in progress)\nSummary: In progress.\n- Changed: on the native desktop Task Manager opens a small page of its own (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 38: built from the desktop\n  page with only what Task Manager uses, everything else read only if ever needed): about 11 MB less in Chromium's\n  measurements, in its own web process; it looks and works as before. Settings is measured and left as it is.\n- Changed: the plan (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 39): native apps for Rudolf OS too, not only WendigOS: librudolf-ui and a native\n  Task Manager first (1.6.2), then Settings and the Office apps, each screenshot-compared with its web version before it\n  replaces it on the native desktop.\n- Changed: the plan (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 38): smaller pages for the native desktop's app windows, measured first (on the\n  host, nearly all of a window's memory is the whole desktop page's script): Task Manager first, then simple apps, then\n  Settings panel by panel; Oracle's Chromium memory as its own item.\n- New: a VPN for the whole computer (Settings \u203a Network \u203a VPN). Add your VPN provider's or your home router's WireGuard file\n  (an administrator's password), then connect and disconnect there or from the taskbar's network pop-up, which shows its\n  state. While it is on, every app's traffic and its name lookups go through it, the local network is still reached\n  directly, and nothing goes round it by IPv6. Its key is kept where only the system reads it. The same on Native,\n  Chromium and Lite.\n- New: who may turn each VPN on and off, an administrator's choice: Everyone (the default), Administrators only, or Always\n  on (it comes back at every start and nobody can turn it off). Accounts with parental controls cannot turn a VPN off,\n  and turning off a VPN another account turned on says so first (\"Ann's VPN is on for everyone on this computer\").\n- New: a VPN's kill switch (an administrator's, off at first): while a VPN with it is meant to be on and its tunnel is\n  down, nothing goes out but the VPN's own server, until it is back or turned off.\n- New: Settings \u203a Network \u203a VPN says that Oracle's Tor windows then run inside the VPN.\n- New: OpenVPN VPNs: add a provider's .ovpn file the same way (with its username and password when it asks for them).\n  Rudolf OS runs OpenVPN only while that VPN is on; files that would run scripts or plugins are refused.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop the taskbar's pinned apps and workspace buttons kept their light grey edge in dark mode, where\n  Chromium mode draws a dim one, and a pinned app had no line under it while its window was open (grey, or the accent colour\n  while that window is the active one). Both now look as in Chromium mode, on every monitor's taskbar. The \"Several monitors\"\n  check compares both looks, light and dark, whatever the hour it runs at.\n- Kernel: the SMB client for network shares (Windows computers and NAS boxes, 1.6.2 Part 35) is built in as a signed module, loaded\n  only when a share is opened. It speaks SMB 3 (and SMB 2.1 when asked for a share), never the old SMB 1 or 2.0, and Rudolf OS\n  serves no share.\n- New (for File Explorer's network shares, Part 35): Rudolf OS can open a shared folder of a Windows computer or a NAS box for the\n  account signed in. Its files are that account's alone (other accounts cannot see it), the server is asked to encrypt where it\n  can, the password is kept in the account's keyring only if asked, and every share closes at sign-out. A wrong password, a\n  missing share or a server that speaks only the old SMB 1 is said plainly.\n- New: Network in File Explorer. It lists the Windows computers and NAS boxes found on your home network (with the firewall on)\n  and opens their shared folders as folders of your own: browse, open, copy to and from, drag onto the desktop. You can also type a\n  share's address (\\\\server\\share or smb://server/share). Your recent shares are listed and reconnect only when you open them,\n  never at start-up, so a server that is off never slows the start. Disconnect closes a share. The same on Native, Chromium and Lite.\n- New: scanners (step 1 of 4: driverless scanning, without the Scan app yet). Rudolf OS finds the scanners and\n  printer-scanners on the home network that speak eSCL (AirScan) or WSD, as most made since about 2015 do, with the\n  firewall on, and USB ones that speak eSCL through IPP over USB; it scans with sane-airscan (pages in colour, grey or black\n  and white, an area, the resolution, the document feeder's pages) and saves PDF, PNG or JPEG. Scanning runs as the\n  account's own user. 495 KB more on the disk; nothing runs until a scan.\n- New: Scan, an app of Rudolf's own for those scanners (step 2 of 4), in the Start menu, in File Explorer (Scan into this\n  folder), in the Print dialog and in Settings \u203a Devices: the scanners found, a preview, the area drawn on it or a paper\n  size, colour, grey or black and white, the resolution, the glass or the document feeder; pages added one scan after\n  another, put in order or taken out, then saved as one PDF, or each page as PNG or JPEG, in Documents or where you choose.\n  A paper jam, the cover open, an empty feeder, a busy scanner or none found are said plainly. Scanning runs as your\n  account's own user, which also writes the file. The same on Native, Chromium and Lite.\n- New: older USB scanners (step 3 of 4): eight of SANE's own drivers for scanners that need one, chosen by what is\n  common: Canon PIXMA and CanoScan (pixma, genesys, plustek, canon_lide70), Epson (epson2, epsonds), Samsung, Xerox and\n  Dell all-in-ones (xerox_mfp) and Fujitsu document scanners (fujitsu). They work only over USB, and the scanner is given\n  to your account's own user only while it scans. 2.4 MB more on the disk.\n- New: Linux apps such as Simple Scan use the network scanners Rudolf OS found (step 4 of 4): after Scan has looked\n  for scanners, their addresses are given read-only to the Debian sandbox's own scanning settings. USB scanners stay\n  with Rudolf's Scan app: Linux apps get no USB devices.\n- Fixed: with the taskbar at the left or the right, Quick settings, the notifications and the calendar open beside their\n  buttons (they opened in the screen's bottom-right corner, over a taskbar at the right); a right-click menu no longer lies over\n  the taskbar; and the desktop's icons move at once when the taskbar grows instead of sitting under it. The same on Native.\n- Kernel: the system's images can be packed with zstd (squashfs reads zstd as well as xz, on x86-64 and ARM64). The images\n  themselves are unchanged in this version; a later one moves them to zstd once every updated computer can read them.\n- Updates: an update whose system images its own kernel could not read is refused with a message saying so (when it is\n  checked, and again once downloaded), as are the recovery system and Make a stick; nothing on the computer is changed.\n- Updates: when an update packs the system's images in a new way (as the move to zstd does), Settings \u203a Update says before\n  downloading that they come whole this once, and how much (\"Download (about 530 MB)\"), on every connection; later updates\n  download only the changes again. The download no longer tries the changes first for such an image.\n- New: Desert Camp, a third moving background (Display Properties \u203a Dynamic Backgrounds): an open desert with clusters of\n  tents round their campfires. At sunrise the sun comes up as a shimmering mirage on the horizon and the campfires smoke; at\n  night the tents glow from inside, the fires flicker, the stars twinkle under a nebula and shooting stars streak by. The same\n  on Native, Chromium and Lite.\n- Fixed: Nintendo 64 and Dreamcast games in Emu never started. Starting one stopped Rudolf OS's system service (it restarts\n  by itself) before the emulator opened. Now Rosalie's Mupen GUI and Flycast open with the game, full screen.\n- Fixed: Nintendo 64 games had no controls. Rosalie's Mupen GUI connects no controller until its settings have been saved\n  once, and Emu opens it without its menus. Player 1 is now set up the first time a game starts: the first controller, or\n  the keyboard (the arrow keys are the stick, X is A, C is B, Z is Z, Return is Start). A setup you saved in its settings\n  (Ctrl+I) is kept.\n- Fixed: Nintendo 64 games end with F12, and the emulator then closes. Emu's note said Esc, which did nothing there, and\n  ending the game left an empty window.\n- Fixed: When Emu closed a Nintendo 64 or Dreamcast game to start another, the emulator kept running. The same was true of\n  Sober (Roblox) when Rudolf OS closed it. They now end completely.\n- Changed: the plan (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Parts 33 to 35): a VPN for the whole computer (WireGuard, then OpenVPN; an\n  administrator adds them, everyone may turn them on and off unless told otherwise; an optional kill switch), scanners\n  (driverless first, with a Scan app) and network shares (Windows and NAS shares in File Explorer).\n- Fixed: Bring your files now opens an old Windows disk's BitLocker for real. It was checked in a virtual machine on Rudolf OS's\n  own kernel with BitLocker volumes made by Windows: AES-XTS (Windows 10 and 11), AES-CBC, and AES-CBC with the older Elephant\n  diffuser. Each opened read-only with its recovery key, which is stored nowhere. Two fixes: opening no longer waits for ever for\n  the system's device manager, and a mistyped recovery key now says \"That key does not open it.\" instead of \"BitLocker could not\n  be opened here\".\n- Changed: a new installation now starts with the Native desktop (the owner's decision, 2026-11-22). Chromium and Lite stay\n  in Settings \u203a System health \u203a Desktop engine; if Native does not start, Chromium is used. A computer updated from 1.6.1\n  keeps the desktop it had.\n- Fixed: the title bars check (std-titlebars-check.yml) passes: Chrome is started without --enable-automation, whose bar on\n  the runner moved the desktop page 56 px down so the test's presses missed (38/38 and 22/22 now).\n- Changed: the plan (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 31): zstd compression for the system's images, measured against today's xz\n  in QEMU and on the owner's two computers before anything changes; the kernel learns to read zstd first.\n- Changed: a Library game's save record may now be up to 6 MB (it was 256 KB), so a game with a large world, such as Ember\n  Reach's shared galaxy, can keep it in a few records. Saves stay per package, game and account, mirrored and backed up as\n  before. The desktop tells a game its limit when it starts, so a game made for this works on older versions too.\n- New: Flatpak apps that Rudolf OS installs may now show their progress on their taskbar buttons. Each one gets a single extra\n  permission, set by Rudolf OS, to say its progress to the desktop, and nothing else. Apps installed earlier get it at the next\n  Update all. (Chromium-based browsers and most Electron apps today send their progress only on Ubuntu's old Unity desktop with\n  its library inside the app. Rudolf OS cannot provide either, so they still show none; Firefox already does. See\n  docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 25b.)\n- New: on the Chromium desktop, an app's own title bar and buttons work as on Native (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 29). Dragging an\n  app's own title bar or edges (Discord, Steam, VS Code, GTK apps with a header bar) moves and sizes it with Rudolf's frame\n  following, the same way and with the same limits as on Native; its own maximize button and a double-click on its bar maximize\n  and restore it, and its own minimize button minimizes it, as Rudolf's title bar does. The app's Move and Size from the keyboard\n  work too. Before, these did nothing on Chromium.\n- Changed: the Standard desktop is now called Chromium (it is the same desktop: the desktop page in Rudolf's Chromium), and\n  Settings \u203a System health \u203a Desktop engine lists the three as Native, Chromium, Lite. A computer on Standard stays on it and\n  shows it as Chromium; nothing saved changes. Help, System health, the self-test, the sign-in's messages and rudolf-fetch say\n  Chromium too.\n- Fixed: the Several monitors check failed on every run although its tests passed: monbeh_t.js named a picture\n  \"cmp.lock:cover2.diff.png\", and GitHub's upload of the pictures refuses a ':' in a file name. Nothing in Rudolf OS changed.\n- Fixed: the Lite engine check's apps' own title bars test (csd161_t.sh, 7) failed whenever it ran after 18:00 UTC: with no\n  theme chosen the native desktop turns dark at night, and the window's edge is then the dark theme's, as it should be.\n  The test now chooses the light theme. Nothing in Rudolf OS changed.\n- Safer: no script from another site ever runs in the desktop's page or an app window, which hold the key to the system's own\n  service. Lua (Command Prompt, BlockLua Builder, Blitzen) always uses Rudolf's own copy of its runtime, never one downloaded from\n  a website, and the page now has a security policy that refuses any other site's script. Flash content plays in a sealed-off frame\n  of its own: the Flash Player app, and Oracle's pages for Flash files.\n- Fixed: closing Steam's window left Steam running out of sight (it goes to a tray icon, and Rudolf OS has no tray), using memory\n  until it was force quit. When its last window is closed, and no game of it is running, Steam is now asked to quit as it quits\n  itself, and ended if it has not after 30 s; signing out also asks it to quit first (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 14, found on the\n  owner's Vivobook). Task Manager's Force quit is unchanged.\n- Faster: on the native desktop a Linux app such as Steam is started as soon as Rudolf knows it, instead of after every list of\n  apps (the Library's, the Linux apps', Debian's) one after another and the Start menu's pictures, which took about 10 seconds on a\n  slow laptop; the list of installed Linux apps is kept and given again at once until an app is installed, updated or removed; and\n  the desktop's icons and Start menu no longer wait minutes for Linux apps' pictures that cannot be had.\n- New: Linux apps' progress on their taskbar buttons (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 25b). A Linux app with a download or other work in\n  progress shows it on its taskbar button while its window is minimized (on Chromium: while another window or the desktop is in\n  front), with the same fill as Rudolf's own windows and the same Display Properties \u203a Taskbar \u203a Progress colour, on Chromium and\n  on the native desktop alike. Apps that say it with the Unity launcher signal (Chromium-based and Electron apps, and others) are\n  heard by your account's own portal on your account's own session bus; Firefox's own progress on its window (the XApp hint) is\n  read from the window itself. Only your own apps, and a Flatpak app only if it may talk to com.canonical.Unity and only for its\n  own desktop file. A percentage only: the creeping fill stays for Rudolf's own work.\n- Fixed: the Blitzen knows check's \"\u26a1 Why?\" test (bzwhy57_t.js, 4) failed at some times of day: when the search, which\n  reads the log lines with their times, found two pieces of one document's section, both had the same name, and the test\n  took the one kept for the one unticked. It now unticks a row by its place and looks for that row's text. Blitzen leaves\n  out what is unticked, as it did; nothing in Rudolf OS changed.\n- Fixed: Oracle's status line kept saying \"Goes To: <address>\" after the mouse left one of Oracle's own pages (its home\n  page, the reader) for the rest of the desktop or Oracle's own bar: Chromium does not tell such a page that the mouse\n  left it, so the desktop now ends it itself when the mouse arrives on its own page.\n- New: Rudolf's title bar on the Chromium desktop's windows from outside the page (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 29, the owner, 2026-11-21): Linux\n  apps, the encrypted Oracle and Oracle's Tor windows get the same title bar, buttons, edges and window menu as Rudolf's own windows\n  (and as Native's frames), in the title-bar style and colour chosen in Display Properties. Drag the title bar to move them, any edge\n  or corner to size them; maximize and restore, minimize (the taskbar button brings them back), close (the app is asked, as by its\n  own close button), Always on top and the workspaces from the window's menu. A click on the desktop or another window puts the app\n  behind it, its window keeping its place with the app's name (\"Click to show it\"). Full-screen videos and games keep the whole\n  screen with no frame; dialogs open in the middle of their window. Chromium-based windows (the encrypted Oracle, Tor windows,\n  Chromium-based browsers) leave their own frame off; apps that always draw their own (Steam, Discord) show both, as on Native.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop, Task Manager could come up as an empty window for several seconds (or not at all) while the desktop\n  was busy, for example while Steam was starting: its page waited for the desktop's hidden background page. Task Manager now has a\n  page of its own and comes up at once (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 14, found on the owner's Vivobook).\n- New: two-player games from the Rudolf OS Library on your home network (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 30; Ember Reach 0.5.2's\n  duels). Press Host on this network in the game, and Rudolf OS runs a small relay on this computer while you play,\n  and announces it on the network; on the other computer Find games lists it, and the two games meet through it.\n  Nothing goes over the internet. The relay runs as your account's own user (never as root), answers only the local\n  network, and the firewall opens its port to the home network only (never on a cellular connection) and only while a\n  game is hosted. It stops when the duel ends, or within a minute if no game connects.\n- New: Bring your files (docs/plan-1.6.2.md Part 27). Installing Rudolf OS on a disk that holds another system now looks at it\n  first, read-only: Windows (each person's Documents, Pictures, Music, Videos, Desktop and Downloads, OneDrive's folders too),\n  Linux (/home and its own folder names) and macOS (said: Rudolf OS cannot read a Mac's disk, and how to copy the files on the\n  Mac first), each folder with its size. Keep them (the default), choose which, or erase without keeping (said with the size\n  that is lost). Kept files are copied onto a USB drive and every one is read back and checked (SHA-256) before anything is\n  erased; a drive that fills up is followed by another; a copy that fails or is cancelled erases nothing, and the installer\n  itself refuses the disk until the copy (or the choice to erase) is made. A BitLocker disk asks for its recovery key (it is\n  opened read-only and the key is never kept); a Windows that was hibernated or not shut down fully is read read-only and\n  said. After the first sign-in Rudolf OS offers to bring them in (Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Bring in files from the\n  old computer): the old Documents into Documents and so on, into your own encrypted folder, names that clash kept side by\n  side, each file checked again and then by the antivirus. The copy on the drive is left as it is.\n\n## 1.6.1 (2026-11-22, release 2026112201)\nSummary: In progress.\n- Fixed: on Standard, with \"Keep my websites encrypted with my account\" on, Oracle would not open (\"Oracle needs\n  Rudolf's Chromium with its shell, which this computer does not have\"): the list it starts from was only made on Native.\n- New: File Explorer to the desktop (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 28, the owner, 2026-11-21): items dragged from a File Explorer window\n  (one, or several chosen with the selection box, Ctrl or Shift) and dropped on the desktop go into your Desktop folder, as a drop on\n  a File Explorer window would put them there: from C: moved (copied with Ctrl), from a USB drive, the Games folder or a folder of\n  this computer by File Explorer's checked transfer with its progress window (a read-only place's files copied), names that clash\n  named as File Explorer names them (NAME - COPY.EXT). The icons appear where they were dropped (the first in the cell under the\n  pointer, the others next to it) and keep that place. Dropped on a desktop folder's icon they go into that folder, on the Trash\n  into the Trash. After Cut or Copy in File Explorer (its menu, the toolbar, Ctrl+X/C), the desktop's right-click menu has Paste\n  (greyed while nothing is cut or copied), and Ctrl+V on the desktop pastes too: the icons where the menu was opened; Cut moves\n  and empties the clipboard, Copy copies and keeps it. A desktop folder's menu has Paste into folder, a desktop file's Cut and\n  Copy (one clipboard: Paste in File Explorer takes them). On Standard, Lite and Native alike: on Native the shell takes the\n  drop from the Explorer window, whose page does the transfer, and gives the icons their places; its menus' Paste, Cut, Copy and\n  Paste into folder are the page's. Fixed on Native: a click on the desktop gives it the keyboard, as on Standard (Ctrl+V,\n  Delete and F2 on the desktop went to the window in front before).\n- Security: Blitzen never shows or sends a private key, a token or one of this computer's secrets, whoever asks or\n  however (social engineering, a page, file, mail or log that tells it to, a key split up, spelled out, encoded or in a code\n  block). rudolfd hides them in what Blitzen is given and in what it writes, before any of it is shown, spoken, kept or\n  copied (\"[hidden: a private key]\"), refuses an action that would send one even after Allow, and keeps Developer mode\n  from reading key files. A password or a code is sent only after you allow it in the desktop's own prompt, which only\n  your own click can answer. A program Blitzen runs sees only its own folder, with no network. Each attempt is in System\n  health \u203a Security.\n- Security: code Blitzen writes can no longer act with the desktop's rights. JavaScript and Lua it writes run in a sandbox\n  with no access to the desktop's page, its storage, rudolfd or the network (before, a program \"checked by running it\" could read\n  the desktop's page and use the system's API: something Blitzen read, a web page or a file, could have made it write one). Lua\n  runs on a copy of your own files: a check throws the copy away; \u25b6 Run asks, in the desktop's own dialog, before keeping what it\n  changed, listing each file, and only in your own folder. An HTML answer's preview loads nothing from anywhere.\n- Security: in Blitzen's answers, only the pages its web search read and Rudolf OS's own documents open with a click; any other\n  address is shown whole and opens only after \"Open this address?\", which shows its site and what it would send.\n- Security: what \"Learn from what I do\" keeps (what you did, your notes for Blitzen, the setting itself) is kept only in your\n  account's own encrypted folder, only while you are signed in, never for another account; what 1.6.0 kept outside it is moved in.\n  Blitzen's model engine no longer can save a conversation to the disk.\n- Security: Developer mode: every command Blitzen proposes for your test computer is shown whole before it is sent, with what is\n  risky in it said in plain words; one command at a time; one carrying a password, token or key is never sent. A workflow to start\n  shows its file, and a pull request its whole change, before Allow.\n- New: a minimized window's taskbar button shows the work it is doing: the button fills from its left edge to its right, a few\n  pixels inside its edge and behind its picture and title, as a progress bar (a download, a page loading in Oracle, a copy or move\n  in File Explorer, a drive's copy, Extract here, an installation or update, printing, a text message being sent, and any progress\n  bar in the window). Work with no percentage creeps towards the end and completes when it is done; the fill fades away then, and\n  restoring the window hides it. Green as it comes: Display Properties \u203a Taskbar \u203a Progress colour has Blue, Orange, Purple, Red,\n  the accent colour and any colour. The same on Standard, Native and Lite.\n- Fixed: Find printers finds network printers with the firewall on (found on a Vivobook: it listed none). It now asks\n  the network as other computers do (multicast DNS from its own port, 5353), and the firewall lets in only those\n  answers, from the local network and never on a cellular connection; nothing else is opened. Printers that answer\n  only to the computer that asked are found too.\n- Changed: Settings' pages are built from one list of additions each, in the same order as before, instead of a\n  function wrapped up to 15 times. They look and work as they did; a section that fails no longer stops the sections\n  after it from being shown (the failure is written to the console).\n- For the phone build to come (2.0): the groundwork of its home screen's three panels: on a build marked as the mobile\n  build, the three workspaces are panels shown full screen, switched with a swipe to the left or right on the desktop or\n  a title bar, on Standard and Native alike. Nothing changes on computers.\n- Printing: a print queue for the whole computer. Linux apps print too (LibreOffice, GTK and Qt apps, lp in Debian and\n  Flatpak apps: through the queue, as on any Linux), and Settings \u203a Devices \u203a Print queue shows what is printing, with\n  Cancel for your own documents; another account's are listed without their names and cannot be cancelled by you.\n  Printers that take only pictures of the pages get them from the queue too. USB printers' devices belong to the queue\n  alone, and an IPP over USB printer can be reached only through it.\n- Native: an app's window loads the desktop page without 11 modules no app of its own uses (WordPro's additions, Notey for\n  the office apps, the Wi-Fi panel, the computer check, the SNES core's loader, the weather, Settings' search and a few\n  more); each is read the moment something of it is needed. Settings, Task Manager, Oracle, the App Store, Blitzen and\n  the games and office apps that use them keep the whole page, as Standard and Lite do. Measured: no difference in memory\n  that can be told from the noise (about 1 MB at most).\n- Native: each app's window uses about 10 MB less memory. A window no longer makes the pictures of the start menu, the\n  desktop's icons and the taskbar for itself (the native desktop draws those), nor the taskbar's pinned buttons or the\n  dynamic background's moving parts under the app, and it no longer gives the desktop its list of apps again when it opens\n  if the list is unchanged. On the test computer, five app windows and the hidden page: 483 MB instead of 531 MB.\n- Native: the window's menu of an app that draws its own title bar says whether it can use Rudolf's instead. For apps\n  whose own bar holds only the title and the three buttons, \"Title bar: Rudolf's\" cuts their own bar away and frames them\n  as any other window, remembered for each account and app; for others the menu says Rudolf's would hide their menus and\n  buttons.\n- Native: one title bar for apps that draw their own. Discord, Steam, Claude, VS Code and other Electron and Chromium apps,\n  and GTK 3 and 4 apps with a header bar, no longer get Rudolf's title bar above their own: their window keeps Rudolf's edge,\n  round corners and resize edges, and their own bar does the rest, as on Standard. Moving them by their bar, resizing them\n  from any edge or corner, their maximize, restore and minimize buttons, a double-click on their bar, the keyboard's Move\n  and Size from their window menu, and a right-click on a GTK header bar (the window's menu) all work, with the same limits\n  and transitions as Rudolf's own title bar. GTK's invisible shadow margins are cut away. Every other window keeps Rudolf's\n  title bar as before.\n- New (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 19): game mode does more, on Standard and Native alike. While a game is full screen the\n  processor is asked for its full speed (AMD's amd_pstate and Intel's intel_pstate; not with battery saver), and behind a\n  Linux game the desktop's own work waits (its processor time lowered, its animations stopped); notifications are held and\n  one says how many came after, as before. Everything goes back as it was when the game leaves full screen or ends, a crash\n  included. Games that ask for Feral's GameMode (gamemoderun, Steam's launch option, Flatpak games through the portal) get\n  the processor's speed, and nothing else. Settings \u203a System health \u203a Memory \u203a Game mode turns it all off and on.\n- Apps that can use the system's title bar are asked to, once: the first time Rudolf OS starts Chromium, Brave, Edge, Vivaldi,\n  Chrome or ungoogled Chromium from Flathub, Debian's Chromium, VS Code or VSCodium, it turns on their \"use the system title\n  bar\" setting, so they get Rudolf's title bar with nothing lost. Only when you have not chosen otherwise in the app; your\n  other settings are kept.\n- Native: idle windows give their memory back. Notepad or File Explorer minimized, or hidden behind another window, for a\n  while (Settings \u203a System \u203a Idle windows: on by default on computers with under 6 GB of memory, after 10 minutes) is closed\n  behind the scenes and comes back where it was when you show it: its files, tabs, folder, the scroll and the window's size\n  and place. Never with unsaved changes, a dialog, sound or video playing, a call, a download or a transfer; Oracle and Linux,\n  Debian, Flatpak and Wine apps are never touched. Task Manager shows such a window as \"Resting (no memory used)\".\n- New: Oracle blocks ads and trackers. A shield \ud83d\udee1 in the address box says how many were blocked on the page; its menu\n  turns blocking off (or on) for that site and can note a broken site in System health. Settings \u203a Privacy \u203a Ads and\n  trackers in Oracle: on (the default) or off, the sites it is off for, and sites of your own to block. Private and Tor\n  windows always block. It uses Rudolf OS's own list of well-known ad and tracking services, checked on this computer:\n  nothing about the pages you visit is sent anywhere. On Standard, Native and Lite.\n- The desktop page is 870 KB smaller: the Quake engine and the Galaxy Map's universe are files of their own, read only when\n  Quake or the Galaxy Map opens (every window of the native desktop, and Standard's page, parsed them before). Each window\n  of the native desktop uses about 5 MB less memory.\n- Native: the shell's hidden pages are one. The desktop's service page, the portal's page (a Linux or Flatpak app's\n  questions, file dialogs and links) and the start-up page (the notice after an update) were three pages each loading the\n  whole desktop; now one page does what each did. The quick settings' switches, Do not disturb and Clear all, pinning,\n  muting from the taskbar, the desktop's menus, renaming, dropping onto the Trash, files dropped from Linux apps and the\n  start menu's search are done by that page at once, instead of a page of the whole desktop loaded for each. Less memory\n  (on the test computer 110 MB instead of 170 MB with no window open, and no 100 MB peak for each switch).\n- Security: signing in and out with your files on the disk (the encrypted folder opened with your key, your Oracle\n  folder, Linux apps' folders, your account's own Linux user's session) and opening the encrypted folder of\n  Settings \u203a Security (and preparing the drive for it) are done by the drives helper as root, no longer\n  by rudolfd, which reads every request of the desktop: the key goes to it on a pipe, never as an argument, and the\n  account is checked again there (a removed account is refused). Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: the accounts' own Linux users (made and removed with the accounts, written into the system's user list,\n  their folders given to them) and Settings \u203a Privacy \u203a Saved passwords for apps are handled by a helper of their\n  own, no longer by rudolfd; it is asked only when an account comes or goes. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: restart, shut down, sleep, the power settings, brightness, Task Manager's \"Clear cache\" and the desktop's\n  priority, and setting the clock are done by a power helper of its own, no longer by rudolfd. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: Wi-Fi (scan, join, forget, the radios and bands, the country, the adapter), an address by DHCP or a fixed\n  one and the name server, the firewall, the hotspot and metered connections are handled by a network helper of its\n  own, no longer by rudolfd; a network's password reaches it on its standard input only. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: Bluetooth (turning it on and off, searching, pairing, connecting, forgetting, the pairing question) and\n  Thunderbolt and USB4 devices (approving, not approving, forgetting) are handled by a devices helper of its own, no\n  longer by rudolfd. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: Task Manager's End process and End process tree, and reading the kernel's messages (System health, the\n  crashes list), are handled by two small helpers of their own, no longer by rudolfd. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: the display's settings (the graphics path, the desktop's shell, resolution and frame rate, the keyboard's\n  layout, restarting the display or the desktop's browser) and sound (the volume, the microphone, the echo canceller,\n  the sound card) are handled by helpers of their own, no longer by rudolfd. Nothing changes on screen.\n- Security: rudolfd can be confined (off by default until a beta has run with it: /persist/etc/rudolf-confine, or\n  rudolf.confine= at the start-up, off, log or on). All of rudolfd loses the system calls and capabilities nothing it\n  starts needs; each request that starts no program is put in a narrow confinement of its own (only rudolfd's own\n  system calls, writing only in rudolfd's folders through Landlock, which the kernel now has, fewer capabilities);\n  each of rudolf-rootd's helpers gets only its own capabilities; and the API answers each window only what its code\n  asks for (the native shell, the page). Refusals are said in System health.\n- New: a ZIP larger than 1.5 MB in Downloads (kept on the disk) opens in Archive Manager, which lists what is inside it\n  (names, sizes, folders; the first 3,000 shown) without loading it, with Extract here; File Explorer's Open with offers\n  Archive Manager for it too. Extract here (there, or in File Explorer) unpacks it next to the ZIP with a progress window\n  (the file, n of N, the bytes, Cancel): a folder named after the ZIP, its sub-folders as in the ZIP, each file kept on the\n  disk and checked by the antivirus (one quarantined or blocked is said so). The ZIP is unpacked as the desktop user, never\n  as root; one with entries that point outside it (\"..\", an absolute path) is refused, and links in it are left out.\n- Changed: files kept on the disk can now be in sub-folders of Downloads, where they stay (before, they were moved back to\n  the top of Downloads); only those at the top are renamed on the disk when renamed. Deleting them, the Trash and\n  Downloads' own files work as before.\n- New: reader mode in Oracle. On a page with an article (a news story, a blog post, a recipe) a \ud83d\udcd6 button shows in the\n  address box; it, F9 (also inside a website) or \u2630 \u203a Reader mode shows the article alone: its title, author and text, its\n  pictures and links, in Rudolf's reading style, with A\u2212 A+, the width, the font (serif, sans, OpenDyslexic), light, sepia\n  or dark (dark with Rudolf's dark theme), \ud83d\udd0a Read aloud in Rudolf's own voice (the sentence being read highlighted) and\n  \ud83d\udda8 Print (the article alone). \u2715 Exit reader or F9 gives the page back as it was. Nothing of the website runs in reader\n  mode. Also for .HTM files on C:. On Standard, Native and Lite. It uses Mozilla's Readability (Apache License 2.0).\n- Fixed: the release build no longer stops when linux-firmware updates a file: it takes linux-firmware at the commit the firmware list was made from (the list's new \"R\" line), and a check runs that fetch on every change to the list.\n- Changed: Oracle \u203a \u2630 \u203a Reopen my tabs next time now brings back every Oracle window you had, the tabs asleep (\ud83d\udca4, loaded\n  when clicked) except the one in front, so Oracle starts quickly. After Oracle or the desktop ended unexpectedly, the next\n  Oracle window offers \"Restore your tabs?\" once, even with the setting off. Your tabs are kept in your own encrypted home\n  folder (locked when you sign out), no longer in the desktop's settings. Never for private or Tor windows, or Guest.\n- Changed: Native uses less memory: each window's page frees its caches and collects garbage sooner (a limit that\n  follows the computer's memory, checked every 5 s instead of 15), and the pages the desktop opens by itself at\n  start-up wait until the others have started, so their memory peaks no longer come all at once.\n- Fixed: Emu's \"Open a file\u2026\" refused a ZIP or disc over 512 MB (\"copy it in Files instead\"). A ZIP or a disc image\n  (.chd, .cdi, .gdi, .cue, .pbp, N64) chosen there is now added to the game library (a ZIP is unpacked) and the game\n  added starts; nothing that large is read into the page. A 7-Zip file is refused with a reason.\n- Changed: the most a \"choose a file\" window takes into an app at once follows the computer's memory: about a sixth\n  of it (no more than 40% of what is free at that moment, and never over 2 GB) instead of 512 MB for every computer. The\n  Vivobook (3.3 GB) keeps about 512 MB; 8 GB about 1.2 GB; 16 GB and more 2 GB. The message says why a file is too large.\n- New: File Explorer moves files by dragging them from one window to another, between Rudolf OS's own folders, a\n  plugged-in drive and the Games (Emu library) folder, either way: dropped on a window's list they go into the folder it\n  shows, dropped on a folder into that folder, and what will take them is highlighted. A drag moves; Ctrl+drag copies\n  (the pointer shows which). Cut, Copy and Paste in the right-click menu work the same between windows and drives. A\n  move between Rudolf OS and a drive is a copy that is checked first; the originals go only once everything arrived, and\n  one that is cancelled or fails leaves them where they were.\n- New: a selection box in File Explorer: press on an empty spot of the list and drag over the files to select them\n  (Ctrl or Shift adds to what is selected); then drag them, or right-click them. A drive's files can now be selected\n  with Shift+click too, and have a right-click menu (Cut, Copy, Paste, Delete, Copy into Rudolf OS, Properties).\n- New: a window shows a transfer's progress: the file, how many of how many, the bytes, the time left, and Cancel. It\n  closes by itself when done and stays to say what went wrong if something did.\n- Fixed: copying a large download kept on the disk (Copy and Paste in Downloads) made a 99-byte \"- COPY\" that did not\n  open. The copy is now a file of its own on the disk; copies made like that before are mended when Downloads is opened\n  (kept, never deleted).\n- Fixed: File Explorer's Properties window showed only an icon.\n- New: Oracle's status line (bottom left, where it says \"Done\") shows where a link goes while the mouse is over it:\n  \"Goes To: <address>\". It covers links, image-map areas, a form's submit button and links in ads and other frames, on\n  websites and Oracle's own pages; then the status line goes back to what it said. On Standard, Native and Lite.\n- New: find in page in Oracle. Ctrl+F (or F3, Ctrl+G, or \u2630 \u203a Find in page\u2026) opens a bar under the bookmarks bar: type\n  and the matches are highlighted, \"3 of 17\" says where you are, Enter (or \u25bc, F3) goes to the next and Shift+Enter (or \u25b2,\n  Shift+F3) to the previous one, round at the ends; Match case; Escape or \u2715 closes it. Ctrl+F inside a website opens\n  Oracle's bar, as in Chrome, even on sites that use Ctrl+F themselves. Text selected on the page fills the box, and the\n  bar belongs to its tab. On websites, Oracle's own pages and files, on Standard, Native and Lite.\n- Fixed: after a click into a website in Oracle, Ctrl+F and Ctrl+L gave the bar or the address box the look of having the\n  keyboard while what was typed still went to the website.\n- New: Oracle's Downloads page shows a progress bar for a file that is arriving (how much so far and how fast, and with\n  a known size the percentage and the time left) and one being checked. Each download has Remove beside Open: a file in\n  Downloads goes to the Trash with it; otherwise the notification \"File removed from list: Not in Downloads. Ask Blitzen\n  if you need help finding this file.\" has a \"\u26a1 Ask Blitzen\" button. Blitzen reads the download's record, the Trash and\n  where files of that name are, and says whether the file was moved, put in the Trash, deleted or never downloaded.\n- New: reopen a closed tab in Oracle. Ctrl+Shift+T (also inside a website) or \u2630 \u203a Reopen closed tab brings back the last\n  tab you closed, at its place and with its back and forward pages; again, the one before. \u2630 \u203a Recently closed\u2026 lists the\n  last 25; a closed window is one entry that brings its tabs back in a window of their own. The list is kept in memory\n  only, never written anywhere: it is forgotten when Oracle's last window closes and when you sign out. Private and Tor\n  windows keep their own list, which goes with the window. On Standard, Native and Lite.\n- Fixed: Make a Rudolf OS USB stick on an older Rudolf OS stick said \"/dev/sdb is in use (mounted at\n  /run/rudolf/stkinfo-sdb/i). Unmount it first.\" Settings' look at what such a stick holds left its image mounted\n  when the stick had no third partition. It now always unmounts, never looks while a stick is being made, and Make the\n  stick unmounts a look left behind by 1.6.0.\n- Fixed: Make a Rudolf OS USB stick with \"The latest from the update address\" stopped at once with \"use POST\", and so did\n  Settings \u203a System update \u203a Check this address: the page asked rudolfd's address check the wrong way (a GET where it\n  takes only a POST). Its test's stand-in now refuses a GET as rudolfd does.\n- New: the start-up's time from power-on. System health \u203a Last starts \u203a \"Where the time went\" now begins with the time\n  before Linux starts: the computer's firmware, the boot loader and the kernel's unpacking (from the firmware's own\n  record, ACPI's FPDT, and the processor's counter), and ends with the whole from power-on to the desktop's answer. The\n  health log's \"the desktop was ready\" line adds the time from power-on, and counts an installed computer's handover.\n- New: Emu plays Nintendo 64 and Sega Dreamcast games, in native emulators that run on the graphics card, full\n  screen: Rosalie's Mupen GUI (Mupen64Plus) and Flycast, free emulators downloaded once from Flathub the first time a\n  game of theirs is played. Add games takes .z64, .n64 and .v64 games, and Dreamcast discs as .cdi or .chd files or as\n  a .gdi with its track files (each such disc gets a folder of its own); the library has Nintendo 64 and Dreamcast\n  tabs. A plugged-in controller works in both (they set it up themselves); Super+Esc comes back to the desktop.\n- New: Emu plays Sega Genesis / Mega Drive and PlayStation games, in Emu itself: ClownMDEmu and PCSX-ReARMed as\n  WebAssembly (in Emu's player package, 1.1.0, from the Rudolf OS Library). Add games takes Genesis games (.md, .gen,\n  .smd, and a .bin that says SEGA in its header) and PlayStation discs as a .cue with its track files (a folder of its\n  own, as a Dreamcast .gdi), a .chd (a GD-ROM .chd stays a Dreamcast disc) or a .pbp; a PlayStation BIOS file\n  (scph*.bin) goes in PS1/BIOS and is used when it is there (without one, the PlayStation's own built-in replacement\n  starts most games). A disc is read from the library as the game asks for it, not loaded whole. The library has\n  Genesis and PlayStation tabs. Battery memory and the PlayStation's memory card 1 save by themselves; save states\n  as for the other consoles.\n- New: each console in Emu has its own buttons on the keyboard, a controller and the touchscreen: the Genesis's A B C,\n  X Y Z and Mode; the PlayStation's \u2715 \u25cb \u25a1 \u25b3, L1 L2 R1 R2, L3 R3 and the DualShock's two analog sticks (a controller's\n  sticks, or J I K L for the left one). Controls lists each console's own.\n- New: two-step sign-in with an authenticator app (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 17, the codes; security keys that also protect your files come later). User Settings \u203a Sign-in options \u203a Authenticator app: set it up with the setup key in an app such as Google or Microsoft Authenticator, Aegis or 2FAS; then signing in and unlocking ask for the app's 6-digit code after the password, on Standard and on the native desktop. A code works once; after 5 wrong attempts there is a wait (30 seconds, doubling up to 15 minutes). Security Settings \u203a Two-step sign-in: an administrator can require it for administrators or for every account.\n- New: each other's builds (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 16). Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Update address: Check this address says whether it answers, whether its releases are complete, and whose key signs them: updates install only from this computer's key; another person's build can still go on a USB stick. Only an administrator can change the address. Get beta versions switches the address that is set between its /stable and /beta. Make a USB stick can put the update address's latest release on a stick, checked first (another person's build only after a warning). Recovery mode is always the official Rudolf OS: it is kept at the newest official stable release, and every stick gets it.\n- Planned (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 15): one title bar for apps that draw their own (Discord, Steam, Claude, Chromium-based browsers) on Native: the app's own setting for the system title bar where it has one; otherwise its own bar alone, with moving, resizing and maximizing from it; Rudolf's instead by choice for apps whose bar loses nothing when cut.\n- Tests: the kernel tuning measured in QEMU (kernel-tuning.yml, nothing published): test kernels and base layers started in turn on one machine; the start-up's steps, wake-up latency and the page's input-to-frame time under load, memory with tabs, a game and a call at once on 3.3 GB, the power settings' fallback, the start on each kind of virtual hardware, and two builds of the kernel compared.\n- Tests: the checks that test the newest beta's own Chromium on GitHub's runners run again (the Oracle file check, the\n  Tor window keys, Oracle on Native and Standard with the beta's Chromium, Blitzen knows, App links, Dark pages video):\n  the beta's Chromium carries its own glibc at /opt/chromium/glibc, so it is now linked there before it starts\n  (iso/qt/host/betachrome.sh); before, it stopped with \"required file not found\" (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 21).\n- Faster start-up: the kernel is packed with zstd instead of xz (0.6 s less before it starts, in QEMU; about twice that on an installed computer, which starts two kernels), and the wired network cards' drivers are modules loaded after the start.\n- New: Settings \u203a System health \u203a Last starts \u203a Where the time went: each step of the start-up and how long it took. The health log's \"the desktop was ready N s\" and Last starts now give the moment the desktop first answered (they were up to 4 s and 10 s late).\n- New: Settings \u203a System health \u203a Power and battery \u203a Processor energy preference, for AMD processors (amd_pstate): Tuned saves power on battery (balance_power) and gives a full-screen game full speed (performance); the kernel's own (passive) for comparison. As in 1.6.0 stays the default until it is measured on a laptop.\n- Changed: the kernel's build stops when a feature Rudolf OS relies on is missing (75 options, each with who needs it: Flatpak's sandboxes, the Debian container and Wine, the desktop, security, reaching the system disk, power); FUSE, which the x86 kernel lacked, is added (Flatpak's document portal, AppImages).\n- Fixed: on the Lite desktop, a game's \"Save to a file\" (Ember Reach's, for one) or any file picker in a game or on a\n  website no longer freezes the computer. WebKit asked with a file dialog that went through Rudolf OS's own portal,\n  which draws its dialog in the desktop page, while the desktop's window was held for that dialog: nothing could be\n  clicked or typed, Task Manager opened behind it, and only the power button got out (the Vivobook, 2026-11-17). A\n  save from the desktop page now goes to Downloads, as a website's download does, and a file picker (Load from a file,\n  a site's upload in Oracle) is the desktop's own Open dialog. Should the Lite desktop itself ever ask the portal for a\n  file dialog again, the portal answers \"cancelled\" at once instead.\n- Fixed: smart cards were never seen (1.6.0). OpenSC's card module could not start, because pcsc-lite's client library\n  (libpcsclite.so.1, which OpenSC loads itself) was missing from the system. So Settings \u203a Smart cards always said no\n  reader was plugged in, and Oracle never offered a card's certificates. It is in the system now. Found by the new\n  virtual smart card test (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 21).\n- Tests: a virtual PIV smart card with a test CA made by the test (iso/qt/host/vpiv.py). On the host it uses vsmartcard's\n  virtual reader, OpenSC, the smartcard script and NSS. In QEMU it uses the newest beta's own reader stack, through\n  QEMU's USB CCID reader: the reader plugged in, Settings \u203a Smart cards, Oracle signing in to a test site with Chromium's\n  certificate picker and PIN box, a Tor window without the card, the screen locked when the card is taken out\n  (smartcard-qemu-check.yml; nothing is published).\n- New: User Settings \u203a Sign-in options \u203a Authenticator app shows the setup link as a QR code beside the setup key, so\n  a phone's authenticator app adds the account with its camera (an encoder of Rudolf OS's own; dark on white with its\n  margin in every theme). On Standard, Native and Lite.\n- New: security keys that protect your files (User Settings \u203a Sign-in options \u203a Security keys). Rudolf OS reads FIDO2\n  keys itself on Standard, Native and Lite: after your password, touch one of your keys. Add two (one as a spare) and\n  \"Require a security key\": then a copy of your disk opens nothing without your password and one of your keys together.\n  Old security keys checked by the sign-in screen only are moved over when you add your keys again. What to do if keys\n  are lost: docs/security-keys.md.\n- Security: Standard's websites (and Native's Oracle) in the shared browser profile, with \"Keep my websites encrypted\n  with my account\" off, are now encrypted with a key kept in the computer's security chip (TPM 2.0) and tied to its\n  Secure Boot, in place of the browser's built-in key: the drive taken out of the computer, or another system started on\n  it, cannot read the sign-ins, saved site passwords and cards. Without a TPM, as before. Turning Secure Boot off or on\n  makes a new key once: websites ask you to sign in again. Settings \u203a Oracle \u203a Website data says which way it is kept.\n- Lite: the desktop's menus and windows now open over Oracle's tabs instead of behind their pages. While something covers\n  part of a tab, a still picture of the page is shown there (taken again every 2 seconds); the page itself goes on running\n  and is back as soon as nothing covers it.\n- Lite: with the graphics card set to \"Use it for games and Linux apps\" (the default), Oracle's tabs are now drawn by the\n  graphics card while the desktop itself stays drawn by the processor, for smoother scrolling, games and videos in tabs.\n\n- New (docs/plan-1.6.1.md Part 19): NVIDIA's own graphics driver, by choice, for GeForce 16 and newer: Settings \u203a Display \u203a\n  Graphics driver, \"Open (NVK)\" (the default) or \"NVIDIA's driver\" (NVIDIA's open kernel modules and its OpenGL and Vulkan\n  libraries, a signed system extension from Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a System extensions; its modules signed for\n  Secure Boot like the kernel's own, checked by the kernel). If it cannot be used (a module refused, Xorg not starting, the\n  desktop not coming up), the computer goes back to the open driver by itself and says so. Recovery mode always uses the\n  open driver.\n- Several monitors, the system's part (plan Part 1): every monitor plugged in, up to three, is lit side by side, the main one\n  at the left, as Settings \u203a Display \u203a Displays arranged them for that set of monitors (kept per set); plugged in or out,\n  they are arranged again within a second. Linux apps and games open on the main monitor. (rudolf-display --arrange and\n  --monitors, screenctl arrange, rudolfd's screen/arrange; the software display still shows one monitor.)\n- Several monitors on the desktop (plan Part 1), Standard and Native alike: each monitor shows one of the three workspaces,\n  with the wallpaper and a taskbar of its own (the start button, the pinned apps, that workspace's windows, the workspaces\n  and the clock); the main monitor keeps the tray, the desktop's icons and the Start menu. A window moved to another\n  workspace moves to the monitor showing it, one dragged onto another monitor joins its workspace, and a maximised window\n  fills its own monitor. A workspace's button on a monitor's taskbar shows it there. A monitor unplugged: its windows stay\n  in their workspace and none is left off the screen. Settings \u203a Display \u203a Displays lists the displays and which\n  workspace each shows, and makes another the main one. The sign-in and lock screens cover every monitor.\n\n- Fixed (Part 19's follow-up): Settings \u203a Display \u203a Graphics driver offers NVIDIA's driver before its extension is\n  installed (the list of GeForce 16 and newer cards was not in the base yet), and only for Turing and newer cards; NVIDIA's\n  extension builds (glibc's librt.so.1 was missing).\n\n- Fixed (Part 19's follow-up): game mode puts everything back by itself after a game crashes, also when nothing asks\n  again (its watcher is a process of its own).\n\n- Fixed (Part 19's follow-up): a system extension with kernel modules or firmware (NVIDIA's driver) has them laid over\n  the system again; on Rudolf OS /lib is /usr/lib, and those were taken after /usr's own overlay, one overlay too many.\n\n## 1.6.0 (2026-11-20, release 2026112014)\nSummary: In progress.\n- Oracle's website data can be kept encrypted with your account: Settings \u203a Oracle \u203a \"Keep my websites encrypted with my account\". On, Oracle's sign-ins, site data, cache and saved site passwords go in a folder of the account's own, encrypted with the same key as its home folder and locked when it signs out. On by default on Native and Lite; off by default on Standard (Settings says plainly that off means not encrypted), where turning it on opens Oracle in a Chromium window of its own over the desktop, as Browse with Tor's windows are, using a little more memory while it is open. The choice is kept inside the account's encrypted folder, so it cannot be changed with the drive alone, and turning it off asks for the account's password. Only for accounts whose files are on the disk. The first time, the account's websites ask to sign in again, and their old unencrypted copy is removed. Tests: oraacct60_t.js 18-34, homeapi_t.sh 15a-15j.\n- Fixed: Settings \u203a Volume Controls was empty for an account whose settings were saved before 1.6.0 (they had only the first ten sound categories, which replaced the whole list at sign-in). Every sound category is now filled in with its default, and the account's own choices are kept. Test: iso/qt/shell2/sounds60_t.js.\n- Make a stick takes USB sticks and memory cards of 1.8 GB or more (was 2 GB), so a \"2 GB\" stick a little short of it can be used. The room the system actually needs is still checked before anything is erased.\n- The native desktop clears Oracle's websites as Chromium mode does: an account deleted in Settings has its websites cleared by Oracle (at once when Oracle is open, otherwise when it next starts), and signing out closes Oracle as it closes every other window, after saving its tabs for \"Reopen my tabs next time\" and clearing Guest's websites. Before, these happened only when done from Oracle's own page, and Oracle stayed open after signing out. Test: iso/qt/shell2/oraacct60_t.js 12\u201317.\n- A USB stick made from another build of Rudolf OS (its update address, or a computer running it) now starts the official recovery system in Recovery mode: Make a stick puts the official Recovery mode start-up file (recovery.efi) of the official recovery system's release on it, from that release's boot image (its checksum and Rudolf OS's Secure Boot signature checked; kept for the next stick), and adds the boot menu's Recovery mode entry when the build has none. The build's own start is left as it is. Offline with none kept, the stick keeps the build's own and says so. With Secure Boot on, the official file starts where the computer trusts Rudolf OS's key. Test: iso/qt/host/recefi60_t.sh.\n- Changed: the window transitions (LitePoint) are longer: opening 240 ms (corner to corner, was 200; side to side, was 220), minimizing and closing 240 ms (were 200), maximizing and restoring 200 ms (were 180); the start menu 200 ms opening (was 160) and 160 ms folding back (was 140); the taskbar's pop-ups and right-click menus 160 ms (were 140 and 120); a window's taskbar button 120 ms (was 100).\n- Changed: Oracle's websites (sign-ins and site data) belong to an account's ID, not its name, so a deleted account made again with the same name starts with none; Guest's are kept in memory only and forgotten when Guest signs out; a deleted account's are forgotten. Everyone signs in to websites again once (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 21), and is told so.\n- New: Oracle \u203a \u2630 \u203a Reopen my tabs next time: Oracle opens the tabs you had open when you last closed it.\n- New: smart cards, such as the DoD Common Access Card (CAC) and PIV cards (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 20). Card readers work, and Oracle can sign in to websites with the card: it shows the card's certificates, then asks for the card's PIN (Rudolf OS never sees it). Settings \u203a Security Settings \u203a Smart cards shows the reader, the card and when its certificates expire; an administrator can install the DoD's certificates (downloaded from the DoD and checked against the DoD's root certificates first); and \"Lock the screen when my card is taken out\". Never in Tor windows. Linux programs (Debian) can use the card too.\n- New: File Explorer \u203a a drive \u203a Copy to another drive\u2026 copies files and folders from one drive straight onto\n  another (a USB stick, say), folders and all, with no limit on size or number, and nothing on the first drive\n  changed. \"Only personal files\" leaves out Windows, programs, AppData and system files, for rescuing an old\n  computer's documents, pictures and videos. When the drive fills up, plug in the next one and copy again: it carries\n  on with the rest.\n- New for developers (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 19): docs/DEVLINK.md, the development link's whole protocol, so any agent (Claude, another, or your own) can run commands on a Rudolf OS computer through its private repository; its reference client iso/systools/devlink.py (Python's standard library only: run, send, wait, screenshot, get, put a local file); docs/custom-builds.md 7: a development link for your own build.\n- Oracle's loading line (docs/plan-1.6.0.md 17c): LitePoint's point of light moves left to right along the bottom of the bookmarks bar as a page loads, lighting the line behind it; it runs to the end and fades when the page is loaded, and fades where it stopped when a load fails. LitePoint's colour and brightness; Reduce motion: a plain line that fills.\n- New: the Rudolf sounds (docs/plan-1.6.0.md 17d): a start-up chime and a shut-down chime of Rudolf OS's own, and a\n  matching sound for each part of the desktop, as a new \"Rudolf\" style in every category of Settings \u203a Volume Controls.\n  Nine new categories, each with Classic, Retro, Candy, Minimal and Rudolf: signing out and locking, an update ready,\n  warnings (the questions before something that cannot be undone), the battery low (once at 10 %), a device connected\n  and removed, the start menu, the Trash emptied and a screenshot. \"Use one style everywhere\" picks one style for all\n  18, the nearest each older category has. The native desktop plays the same samples as Standard. The sounds you have\n  chosen stay as they are.\n- Blitzen as a developer's agent (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 19), with Developer mode on: Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Blitzen \u203a Developer (your fork and your development link, their tokens in the account's keyring) and Blitzen Developer: your workflow runs and a failed job's log (\"Ask Blitzen why it failed\"); a change to a file written by Blitzen, shown as a diff, pushed to a new branch with a pull request only after you Allow it (never your default branch, never merged); workflows started and commands sent to your test computer (a command, a file, a screenshot) only after Allow; a shell block in a chat answer can go to the test computer. And the `devlink` command on Rudolf OS (devlink.py's command line), to drive another computer's development link.\n- Fixed: on a slow computer the Lite (and Native) desktop could be given up as \"no answer in 90 s\" while it was still starting (QEMU's ARM64 check: Lite answered at 82-95 s, then fell back to Standard). A desktop still busy starting (more than a fifth of a processor in the last 10 s) is now given until 300 s; one that is idle and silent is still given up at 90 s.\n- For your own builds (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 19): a build knows the repository it comes from (/etc/rudolf/origin, written by its workflow): a fork's computers take Get beta versions, Blitzen's model, Debian's base, Make a stick's boot files and the release downloads from the fork, file system reports on it, and the development link's put takes its releases; Settings \u203a About names it. The recovery system and Return to official Rudolf OS stay official.\n- Planned (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 19): developers' own devlinks, builds and agents: docs/DEVLINK.md and a reference client (iso/systools/devlink.py) for any agent; a fork's build points at the fork (/etc/rudolf/origin); setting up a devlink for your own build; Blitzen as a developer's agent on their own repository and devlink, each push and command allowed first.\n- Blitzen looks things up before it answers (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 18): in Rudolf OS's own source and documents (the running release's, fetched once while the computer is idle, on mains power or a battery at 50 % or more and not on a metered connection; every system release now carries its source, in its signed list), in the system's logs (passwords, tokens and keys left out) and in notes you ask it to keep (\"Remember that \u2026\"); each answer lists what it used, a source file opening at its line. \"Look it up first\" can be turned off in the chat. \"Search the web\" (off, and off again after each question) sends only the question (DuckDuckGo, as Oracle searches; through Tor if asked) and reads the first pages, numbered and opened in Oracle. Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Blitzen: what it knows: \"Learn from what I do on this computer\" (off at first, per account: the apps and files opened, by name, the settings changed, the errors met; kept on this computer only), Memory, and \"See what Blitzen knows\" to forget an item, a day or everything. It does not retrain its model.\n- Fixed (2026-11-18, the owner's Vivobook, Standard): saving from Oracle (a Gmail attachment) froze the desktop: Oracle's tabs are the desktop browser's own, so the Save dialog it asked for was drawn in the desktop's page while Chromium held that window for it; nothing answered, and Task Manager came up behind it. rudolf-portal now saves what the desktop's own browser asks to save straight to Downloads (under its name, another if taken) and says so in a notification; an Open it asks for is cancelled (never drawn over itself), as the Lite desktop's already were. portal60_t.sh 6a-6c.\n- Fixed (2026-11-17, the owner's Vivobook): Discord (and other Flatpak apps that start their own processes through Flatpak's portal, as Electron apps do) showed no window when run as the account's own Linux user: the account's session bus was started with no environment, so Flatpak's portal, which the bus starts on demand, had no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and could not find the running app (\"Could not find requesting pid\"). The bus now starts with the session's settings (rudolfd-users.inc; keyring60_t.sh 4b).\n- Window transitions \"Splitting\u2013Merging LitePoint\" (the owner's design, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 17): points of light draw a window's frame as it opens (corner to corner 200 ms; side to side 220 ms for a window another window opened), grow it to maximize (180 ms), drop it into its taskbar button to minimize (200 ms; backward to restore), gather it to restore (180 ms) and fold it away to close (200 ms), meeting in a red spark; the same on Standard (desktop/litepoint60.js, in place of the fade) and Native (the same scenes in iso/sys/litepoint.h; X shapes and small windows of its own, no compositor; the window resized once). Display Properties \u203a Colours and theme: \"The window transitions (LitePoint)\" in the accent colour (Normal, Negative, Complementary; white when not ticked) and their light from Off to full. None with Reduce motion. Tests: iso/qt/shell2/litepoint60_t.js (the page and the native scenes compared), native59_t.sh 28b\u201345b. 1.6.0b is the public beta; the finished release drops the b.\n- LitePoint for the start menu (160 ms opening from the start button, 140 ms folding back into it), the taskbar's pop-ups (140 ms, from above the button clicked), right-click menus (120 ms, from their corner at the pointer) and a window's taskbar button when switching to it (100 ms, its outline): moving away from the taskbar's side (up from a bottom taskbar, down from a top one, sideways from one at the side), the menus where they were (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 17b). Minimizing clears toward the taskbar's side too.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop a new desktop icon (New folder, New text document, a file saved to the Desktop folder, a tick in Desktop icons\u2026) could take minutes to appear: when several windows gave the desktop's list at the same moment, all but one were refused (\"could not save\"), and the account's own copy was not kept. Each is kept now; the log says when the icons came after signing in.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W and Ctrl+N in Oracle's website tabs did nothing when the desktop was busy for a moment (Ctrl was let go before the desktop passed the key on); they now work as on Standard.\n- Fixed: File Explorer's Drives list shows another Rudolf OS stick (its data, read-only, as \"Rudolf OS data (another Rudolf OS, read-only)\"), and no longer lists the system's own internal devices (dm-0, dm-1, ...) as drives.\n- Fixed: a new USB stick (Make a USB stick) or a new installation (Install here) no longer stops at the Native desktop's sign-in screen with no account: until Rudolf OS is set up, it starts on Standard, whose setup wizard makes the first account, and Native from the next start.\n- Fixed: Recovery mode, nomodeset and rudolf.nogpu=1 start without the graphics card's driver again: udev's drivers-by-modalias rule (#465) no longer loads it at start-up.\n- Fixed: the built-in microphones of AMD laptops whose microphones are on the AMD audio processor (the ASUS Vivobook Go E1404FA and others) work again; since udev replaced mdev in 1.6.0, drivers for devices that appear after start-up (here the processor's microphone devices) were no longer loaded, so Settings \u203a Sound listed only the headset jack's microphone and nothing was heard.\n- New: every release promoted to stable is also published as an install release (the .iso, the boot image, their checksums, README.TXT, the source, and a new INSTALL.TXT with the steps to install), marked Latest on the Releases page; betas no longer take the Latest mark.\n- Fixed: betas again come with their boot image (boot.iso: Make a stick, Put back the official start-up files, Rufus sticks); since 2026-11-17 the release workflow no longer found the disc release it is made from among the newest 50 releases, so 2026111707 to 2026111712 had none.\n- Changed: the boot image's menu (an installed drive's start-up files after Get new boot files or a repair, and a stick made from boot.iso) says \"Start Rudolf OS\" again, not \"Start Rudolf OS (boot files: the system installed on this drive)\"; a stick with nothing installed still says so as it starts. The firmware's boot list is unchanged (\"Rudolf OS\").\n- Fixed: on computers whose firmware does not start a drive named only by itself (the ASUS Vivobook's AMI firmware), Rudolf OS's boot entry now names the drive, its start-up files and \\EFI\\BOOT\\BOOTX64.EFI, as the firmware's own setup does; before, beta 2026111711 could write the drive alone, and the computer opened its setup screen at the next start.\n- Security: what rudolfd does as root for drives (mounting, unmounting and ejecting a USB drive or SD card, a stick's\n  information, Install, Make a stick, Replace the boot files, Keep files on this drive) is now done by a small helper\n  that checks every name and file again itself (a disc image must really be in your home folder, yours or the signed-in\n  account's), run by rudolf-rootd, which only rudolfd can ask. Nothing changes in how the desktop works; it is the first\n  step towards rudolfd no longer running as root.\n- Security: system updates (checking, downloading, rolling back, going back to stable, Return to official, Developer\n  mode, the update settings) go through a helper of their own the same way, which checks again what rudolfd is asked.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop an app's window appears once, at its own size, with its app in it (opening as on Standard), instead of first showing Rudolf's sign-in screen or the desktop page in a large window before shrinking to the app; an app that is slow to start appears after 2.5 s as a plain window of its colour until it is ready.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop a right-click menu replaced by another (a second right-click elsewhere, on the desktop, an icon or the taskbar), or the start menu, a tray pop-up or the indicator replaced by another, no longer leaves a strip of the first one on the screen; a right-click on the taskbar closes the start menu, as on Standard.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the desktop's icons appear at once at sign-in (each account's own from its last session, then as they are now), and a new folder or text document made from the desktop's menu, a file saved to the Desktop folder or deleted from it shows on the desktop within half a second, as in File Explorer (it took seconds, or until another window opened).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop a background, colour or other look chosen in Settings \u203a Display Properties shows on the whole desktop at once, before Apply, and goes back on Revert or when Settings is closed without applying, as on Standard; the screen saver's Preview is full screen.\n- Fixed: about 1 keyring creation or unlock in 256 failed for Linux apps (the app told \"dismissed\", gnome-keyring-daemon logging \"Cannot import an invalid key\"): the desktop's side of the secret exchange now keeps the shared value at 192 bytes, leading zero bytes included, as gnome-keyring-daemon does.\n- Fixed: a start no longer rewrites the drive's start-up files by itself when they are not the official ones (it rewrote the whole area in front of your files, and on one ASUS laptop the computer then found nothing to start); it is said in System health instead.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop a file dragged from a Linux app (Debian's file manager, a Flatpak app, an AppImage) onto the desktop is copied into the Desktop folder, as on Standard (where a drop on a folder's icon or the Trash also goes to the Desktop folder, and text or the taskbar takes none); it did nothing. The file is read for the desktop by the account's own Linux user, only from the account's own files and the drives (Flatpak's document portal is mapped to the real file), never through a link; the original stays where it was.\n- Tests: the native desktop takes X drops from a stand-in Linux app (the desktop, a folder's icon, the Trash, a taskbar button, text, another account's file, a link out, a folder), compared with what Standard's page keeps for the same drops; rudolfd's reading of dropped files on the host (xdrop161_t.sh, drop161_t.sh).\n- Fixed: a computer whose start-up files were rewritten (by 1.6.0's automatic repair, or Get new boot files) could open its firmware's setup screen instead of starting: the firmware's entries for the drive (Rudolf OS's own, and one made in the firmware's setup) named where the old start-up files were, and the new ones are elsewhere. The \"Rudolf OS\" entry now follows the start-up files at every start, also from a USB stick, so starting once from a stick puts such a computer right. A new or corrected entry is written under its own number and read back before it replaces the old one, which is kept as \"Rudolf OS (previous)\" until a start proves the new one. Firmware settings are changed in place (never deleted first), read back, and each change is said in System health (they were refused there before, so the repair's own reports never showed). A drive whose ID Linux leaves out is named with the ID the firmware gives it.\n- Fixed: new start-up files are put into the drive's EFI system partition one file at a time (each written beside the old one, checked, then renamed; the old ones kept until the next start from the drive), never over the drive's whole start-up area, its partition table or its signature; the firmware's entry is checked before any restart, and if anything does not check the old files are put back. This is how Get new boot files, Replace the boot files only, Return to official and the start-up's staged write work now; new boot files staged by an older version for the start-up are removed unwritten. A drive whose EFI system partition has no room (made by Rudolf OS 1.4) is left as it is, and it says so.\n- New: Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Start-up of this drive: \"Check this drive's start-up\u2026\" (its partitions, its start-up files and their signatures, the firmware's entry for it, put right when needed; it says what it found and changed) and \"Put back the official start-up files\u2026\" (only on mains power or above 50 % battery). Both need an administrator's password; Rudolf OS no longer changes the start-up files by itself.\n- Fixed: the self-test's \"listeners\" check failed on 1.6.0 with \"something listens that the list of listeners does not name: unix /run/udev/control\" (udev's control socket, since 1.6.0 uses udev).\n- Fixed: System health no longer says \"the desktop's memory group is missing or empty\" when the computer restarts, and the desktop's page and API stay closed to other programs while the desktop is stopped for a restart or started again (an empty group turned the check off).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the desktop's background work runs once, whether or not any app window is open, as on Standard: an alarm or a Clock timer rings once (it did not ring with no window open, and rang once per open window), also after the Clock's window is closed; a Calendar reminder, a call's or a message's notice and Bluetooth's and Thunderbolt's questions show once; update checks, the Debian and Linux apps' updates and the session's save run once. App windows no longer each run the other apps' background work.\n- Changed: Oracle's start page now reads \"A Chromium-based browser for Linux\" under its name.\n- Fixed: Oracle in Rudolf's dark theme: no white strip under the page (between the page and the status bar); an open start page now follows the dark theme turning on or off by itself at dusk and dawn, not only from Settings.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop a Linux app's requests to the desktop now appear at once, above every window, whether or not any other window is open, and only once, as on Standard: the keyring's password prompts (signing in to Sober, the Roblox client, spun for ever: its \"new keyring\" password prompt never appeared, as nothing on Native collected it with no app window open), file Open and Save dialogs, links (in Oracle) and notifications (the desktop's own); with app windows open each of them drew the request inside itself (behind the app, or twice). The native desktop now collects them itself and shows each once. A file dialog whose app quit or was force-quit closes by itself instead of waiting, so an app opened again after a force-quit asks again at once. Task Manager's list of running apps (and its Force quit, which ends an app with all its background processes) was always empty, as its request was refused; it lists them again, so an app stuck half-closed can be ended and opened again.\n- Fixed: Linux apps that sign in through a web page work: \"Continue with Google\" in Claude's desktop app (and Discord, Steam, VS Code, Slack, Spotify) opens the page in Oracle instead of doing nothing, and after signing in the site's link back to the app (claude://\u2026) goes to that app after Oracle asks \"Open Claude?\". Linux programs in Debian (and AppImages run from it) had no way to open a web page: their xdg-open, xdg-email, x-www-browser, sensible-browser, $BROWSER and GIO's default for web links now hand the link to the desktop through the Rudolf portal, as Flatpak apps' do, so web pages open in Oracle, mail in Mail and files in their app; and Oracle (Chromium on Standard and in Native's Oracle window, WebKit on Lite) gives a link of an app's own scheme to the Linux app whose launcher registers it, on Standard and Native alike. Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Links shows which app opens each kind of link and lets you choose another, or none; links no app registers stay refused. Also, since 1.5.9 the questions an account's own programs ask the desktop (their portal runs as the account's Linux user) could not be left for it and were lost without a word: Flatpak apps' links and file dialogs did nothing while an account was signed in; they reach the desktop again, each readable by its own user only.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop Oracle's title bar and taskbar button show the page's title (\"Downloads - Oracle\", a site's name), as on Standard; with Rudolf's Chromium they said \"Oracle\" whatever the page, and a Tor window's title had no \"(Tor)\".\n- Fixed: in Oracle's website tabs other than the first, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W and Oracle's other keys did nothing and the tab showed the address instead of the page's name (both desktops).\n- Changed: Standard stays the default desktop for new installations in 1.6.0, and Native is not offered after an update (the owner, 2026-11-17: Native becomes the default in 1.6.1, once Discord and Steam work on it on the Vivobook); Native can be chosen in Settings \u203a System health \u203a Desktop engine. An update keeps the desktop the computer had.\n- Tests: Oracle's window on the Native desktop is compared with Standard's, part by part (title bar, tabs, toolbar, address box, bookmarks bar, the Home page, a website, the menu, downloads, dark and light mode, the accent colour) and step by step (new and closed tabs, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+N, Alt+F4, back and forward, full screen), with Rudolf's own Chromium too.\n- Tests: Browse with Tor's keys, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+N and Ctrl+Q in a website in the Tor window, are checked as in Oracle's other windows (the tab closes, a new tab, a new Tor window, Q does nothing) on the standard and the native desktop, with a test of the screen and keyboard service's own key handling for each kind of window (the desktop, the Tor window, Oracle's and Linux apps' windows, where the keys are unchanged).\n- Fixed: Return to official Rudolf OS clears the custom build's system once the official one has started (the start-up dropped the return's mark when it tried the official system, so the custom build stayed on the drive). Tested in QEMU on the real release with its own boot files: the official recovery's start-up files and recovery system put back at the next starts after being changed or deleted, Return to official (and the official rudolf.efi written again), Developer mode on a computer that never ran the official build (the official recovery system downloaded), and with Secure Boot on: a custom build refused, Return to official and Recovery mode.\n- Changed: the repair of the start-up files waits for the network (a start whose Wi-Fi connects late no longer puts it off for a day), and a disc build stops if its EFI image would not fit an El Torito entry (32 MiB; it is 27 MiB, and the build warns from 30 MiB).\n- New: Oracle has its own icon and start page. The icon (Stonehenge in white on navy, in a white circle) is used by Oracle's app everywhere, on Standard and Native, in every icon style and accent colour; the network's globe is unchanged. The start page shows Stonehenge under an arc: a softened navy page with the logo in white in dark mode (Rudolf's dark theme or Oracle's Dark pages, which no longer invert it), and a white page with the logo in navy in light mode. The artwork was made by Rudolf OS's owner (with GPT, after Stonehenge, an ancient monument) and traced to vector shapes.\n- Fixed: System health now shows what the start-up did with the signed system extensions (one left out because its file changed, its signature is not the release key's, it was built for another base or the last start with it never reached the desktop; a start script stopped after its time limit): those lines went to a health log in memory that nothing reads, because the extensions were laid over before the data partition was marked as in use. Tested in QEMU on the real release with test extensions (in use after a restart, broken ones left out, an update of the base with and without the extension's build, Roll back system, Recovery mode).\n- New: Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Roll back to last stable version\u2026, shown when Get beta versions is on or when this computer runs a build the update server does not list as a published stable release (a beta, a test build, a developer's build). It says which version it goes back to and what that version does not use, asks an administrator's name and password, starts the stable release from the other system slot when it is still there (nothing to download) or downloads it as updates are, turns Get beta versions off, and restarts; on a developer's build it is Return to official Rudolf OS. Each release list now says how the release keeps your accounts and files, and going back to a release that could not read this computer's data is refused, saying why, with nothing changed (1.5.9 reads 1.6.0's data; 1.5.8 and older do not). Fixed with it: Return to official Rudolf OS on a computer without an official recovery system stopped at \"another update is running\".\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop quick settings' switches are saved where the native desktop and every window read them, at once, so the taskbar's switches always agree with the windows (High contrast or Do not disturb could show off on the taskbar while the windows had them on); and a pop-up (quick settings, the calendar, the volume, a menu) now always catches the pointer once it is on the screen, so a click anywhere else closes it, as on Standard, also on a slower computer.\n- Fixed: 3D games and other Linux apps that draw with OpenGL work again (Unvanquished showed only \"Couldn't load a renderer.\", SuperTuxKart installed but did not start): since 1.5.9 the display server looked for the graphics drivers in a folder that does not exist, so it offered OpenGL to no program, whoever ran it (2D apps and the desktop itself were not affected). Settings \u203a System health \u203a Run the self-test now says when the display server offers no OpenGL to Linux apps.\n- Fixed: sleep on AMD laptops (the Vivobook Go's Ryzen 3 7320U): the kernel has AMD's power management controller driver (AMD_PMC, and AMD_PMF), which their modern standby needs to really sleep; and the hardware check's sleep step now passes only when the computer really slept (the kernel's own count and clocks, from rudolfd), saying why not otherwise, instead of when ten seconds had passed.\n- Fixed: Task Manager's \"Check this computer\u2026\" on the Native desktop: the sound step's tones are played once the app window's sound output is open, and \"Play again\" reuses it (a tone started at once could be lost); the microphone step listens for 3 s from the microphone's first sound (WebKit's takes a moment to open it); the drawing step really draws its moving picture (its two shaders gave one value different precisions, so it never linked, on Standard as on Native, and only the dark background was shown while frames were counted) and fails when nothing is drawn.\n- Fixed: the system report on the Native desktop describes the computer, not the window it was made in: \"Pages drawn with\" names the graphics chip WebKit draws with (from EGL where GTK has no OpenGL; it said \"Apple GPU\"), \"Desktop size\" is the screen's (it was Task Manager's window, 860x546), and \"Desktop\" says native.\n- Fixed: \"Report a problem\u2026 \u203a Save to a file\" saves into the signed-in account's own Documents (it went to C:\\DOCUMENTS, the old shared folder File Explorer no longer shows, so the file could not be found), says the full path and offers \"Open the folder\"; on Native the file reaches the disk at once and File Explorer's own window opens there.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the health log's \"the desktop was ready N s after start-up\" counted the time the sign-in screen waited for a password (197 s on the Vivobook), as the pages there load only after signing in; the native desktop now notes it when its sign-in screen shows, with when it started and how long its screen took.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the keyboard step of Task Manager's \"Check this computer\u2026\" saw every key again (19 did not answer on the Vivobook: F1 opened Help in front of the check, Super the start menu, and the keys pressed after them went there): while the step (or Settings \u203a Keyboard's \"Press keys\u2026\") is in front, the native desktop leaves every key to it. Super is no longer kept from the apps: a lone Super still opens the start menu, and an app sees the key as on Standard (MetaLeft, Meta); the apps' windows name it as Chromium does. Only Rudolf OS's exact shortcuts are taken from the apps, so arrows, F-keys, Ctrl and Alt reach games and editors as on Standard.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the desktop's sounds play as on Standard, with the account's Volume Controls: a window opening and closing, the taskbar's buttons, the start menu's rows, menus' rows, quick settings and the pop-ups' buttons, dialogs' buttons, notifications and the sounds inside apps, each once (only the start-up, sign-in, error and shut-down chimes played before).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the Library's apps (Ember Reach), Windows programs (Starport GE) and Linux apps (Discord, Steam, Claude) open from the start menu, the taskbar's pins and the desktop's icons as on Standard (a program rudolfd runs leaves no empty window), and the Linux and Windows programs show their own icons there; the start menu's search is Standard's (apps, Settings' pages, files, sums), so Packages is found again; Desktop icons\u2026 lists every app at once (Ember Reach was missing until \"Restore the standard icons\") and a tick shows or hides the icon on the desktop within a second (it took minutes, or never happened).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop an app's window no longer shows the sign-in screen for a moment before the app: the window signs in with the native session without drawing it (it still appears when a window really has to ask, such as the page opened by hand).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the display settings (title bars, the taskbar, colours and the accent, light or dark, the background, fonts and sizes, and the rest) change the native desktop and every open app window at once, as on Standard; a window that had missed a change, or Oracle's page, can no longer put the older settings back.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop the System monitor and Weather widgets kept \"CPU: \u2013\", \"Memory: \u2013\", \"Windows: 0\" and \"Loading\u2026\" for good after the native desktop started again or at an unlucky moment of signing in; they now always show live values (the monitor counts every open window, Oracle's too), or the weather's error.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop notices no longer pop up again: \"Rudolf OS was updated\u2026\" shows once per account and release (from the desktop itself, not from each app window as it opened), and \"Real browser tabs are ready\" is no longer offered there (Oracle on Native already has real tabs; its \"restart the desktop\" would have started Standard's browser).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop, signing in now opens the encrypted folder (Debian, Linux and Windows programs, Linux apps, Downloads) as Standard's sign-in does, so the Terminal opens Linux (Debian) again instead of only Command Prompt (C:) tabs; Ctrl+Alt+T in an app's window opens the Terminal's own window, and the Start button's and the desktop's \"Command Prompt\" open the Command Prompt as on Standard.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop sound from the app windows (the Media Player, games, videos) no longer stops for good once a page muted or turned down a sound: each window's sounds are mixed by it, as Chromium does, so nothing one page does is kept for the others; and the desktop's own sounds play there too: the start-up chime at the sign-in screen, the sign-in and unlock sound, the error at a wrong password and the shut-down chime, with your Volume Controls settings.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, pressing a quick settings switch could restart the whole desktop (the page doing it offered Standard's \"Real browser tabs are ready\" and restarted the browser); the magnifier no longer risks ending the desktop when a window closes while it draws.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, a new window now fades in and grows as on the standard desktop; the windows in Super+Tab's overview go on moving (a video keeps playing there); what is behind a notification (its shadow and corners) is shown as it is now, not as it was when the notification came, and a notification no longer sits 15 px off after another one goes.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, a new window now fades in and grows as on the standard desktop; the windows in Super+Tab's overview go on moving (a video keeps playing there); what is behind a notification (its shadow and corners) is shown as it is now, not as it was when the notification came, and a notification no longer sits 15 px off after another one goes; an app's window on a smaller screen (1280\u00d7800) is no longer shorter than on the standard desktop; drop-down lists show their arrow in dark mode.\n- Fixed: Oracle showed a blank white page for its Welcome and README bookmarks, an .HTML file opened from File Explorer and now and then its own about: pages (Rudolf's Chromium 158 sometimes started a sandboxed page's own process as if the page were not shown); such pages are drawn again, still in their sandbox. A phone number (tel: link) clicked in a page on the drive now asks how to call it, as in Oracle's real tabs.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, quick settings' switches now work as on Standard with app windows open: Night light and High contrast colour the whole screen (taskbar, desktop, every window and Linux apps), the magnifier zooms the whole screen round the pointer, the on-screen keyboard is one keyboard above every window that types into the window in front (it showed a keyboard inside each window), touch mode makes the title bars and the tray's buttons larger, High contrast and Touch mode are kept (they were lost, so the other windows never followed), Do not disturb holds every window's pop-ups, the screen reader says that it is on, and Settings comes to the front if it is open.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, the power button shows the power dialog at once, above every window, and only once (it was left waiting until a page took it: Oracle drew it inside its own window minutes later); the dialog has \"Restart into Windows\" as on Standard, and Escape cancels it.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop, the volume and brightness keys show Rudolf's indicator whichever window is in front (also when the computer's firmware changes the brightness itself), Ctrl+Alt+Del and Ctrl+Shift+Esc bring up Task Manager above every window, and Standard's other global keys work there too (the screen reader, the magnifier's zoom, a screenshot, clipboard history, the emoji panel, the next keyboard layout); the taskbar has the brightness button again.\n- Fixed: on the native desktop the windows' title bars and frames now look and work exactly as on the standard desktop with every setting, not only the defaults: the title follows Display Properties' font size (the bar grows with large sizes), Wireframe title bars use the window's own text colour (they were black on black in dark mode), windows not in use are dark grey in dark mode in every style, the touch mode's larger title bar and buttons, high contrast's white edge and colour vision filters, \ud83d\udccc for Always on top, \u25a1 (not \u2750) on a maximised window, which keeps its round corners and the standard desktop's size, and Oracle's window is dark in dark mode too; the display scaling no longer blurs edges; a right-click on a title bar opens the window's menu and a middle-click sends the window behind the others; resizing follows the standard desktop's sizes. Fixed: after the native desktop ended (given up, or started again), Oracle's Chromium kept running and held the standard desktop's profile, so the standard desktop then opened in plain Chromium windows with grey title bars of Chromium's own; it is now closed first. Settings \u203a System health \u203a Last starts names Native.\n- New: Messages, for text messages through a cellular modem: conversations, read and send (long messages and any language, the parts counted as you type), delivery reports (Sent, Delivered), and a notice for each new message, also on the lock screen with the sender only (an administrator can allow the text). Messages are kept in your account's folder, encrypted when \"Encrypt my data\" is on, and taken off the modem.\n- New: the taskbar's network icon shows mobile data's signal as bars, with the operator and the network type (5G, LTE, 3G), on both desktops; the network menu lists mobile data in use or kept in reserve. Settings \u203a Mobile network shows the signal's details (RSRP, RSRQ, SINR, in words too) for placing the computer or an antenna. System health notes modem events (the SIM taken out, the PIN blocked, no network, the module restarting), and the self-test's modem line checks the driver, the SIM, the network and the data connection.\n- New: Settings \u203a Mobile network shows the mobile data used this month on each SIM (counted on this computer, kept across restarts), from the day of the month you choose, with a monthly limit: a notice at 80 % and, if you choose, mobile data turned off at 100 % until the next month (Connect goes on anyway). Mobile data is metered by itself: updates are never downloaded by themselves on it, and Settings offers them with their size (\"Download over mobile data: up to 42 MB\").\n- New: mobile data through a cellular modem: Connect and Disconnect in Settings \u203a Mobile network and in quick settings (\ud83d\udcf6 Mobile data), connected by itself on the SIM's home network (abroad only when roaming is allowed). A cable comes first, then Wi-Fi, then mobile data: it stays connected but unused while Wi-Fi works and carries the traffic within seconds when Wi-Fi drops, with a notice, and Wi-Fi takes over again when it is back. The firewall covers the cellular connection as every other and never treats it as the local network. IPv6-only networks (T-Mobile's) reach IPv4 sites through a CLAT (464XLAT).\n- New: Settings \u203a Mobile network, for a cellular modem's SIM card: unlock it with its PIN (an administrator's password; the tries left are shown), or with its PUK and a new PIN after three wrong PINs (with the warning that ten wrong PUKs block the SIM for good); the operator's settings (APN) found by themselves from the SIM, with Rudolf's own corrections for operators that need the APN's capitals exactly (US Mobile's \"PWG\"), or typed in exactly as given; mobile data when roaming off unless turned on; the network type (5G, 4G, 3G) where the modem lets it be chosen. Quick settings has Airplane mode: Wi-Fi, Bluetooth and the cellular modem off together, and back as they were.\n- Cellular modems (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 1): Settings \u203a Network \u203a Cellular modems offers \"Turn on this laptop's cellular module\" for laptop modules whose radio starts locked (the FCC lock: Lenovo's and Dell's Fibocom, Quectel and Sierra cards), saying what it does; an administrator's name and password turn it on, and it is kept. Modules that Linux supports only partly (Intel's XMM7360 in some ThinkPads) are named there with what is known about them, instead of failing silently. ModemManager comes with QMI, MBIM, QRTR and AT support and the tools its unlock scripts need. Fixed: the modem service started ModemManager again for each of a modem's ports and never stopped it when the modem was unplugged.\n- The native desktop's dialogs (Open and Save, Print, Allow access?, Close anyway?, a game's download) now appear over the whole desktop, dimmed and centred, as in the standard desktop, instead of inside the app's window where their buttons could be cut off.\n- Fixed on the native desktop: a video in full screen showed a black screen; Task Manager listed only its own window (it now lists every window, with Switch to and End task).\n- The native desktop's apps now draw sliders, colour boxes, time and date fields, radio buttons and progress bars as the standard desktop does, show the chevron on every drop-down list and 12-hour times with AM and PM.\n- The native desktop shows Glacier Lake's northern lights and Spirit Falls' statue, petals and sky lanterns, as the standard desktop does.\n- Fixed on the native desktop: right-click menus inside apps were cut off at the window's edge (File Explorer's last rows could not be reached); Settings \u203a System health \u203a Log video problems lost its lines whenever another app window opened; apps showed no scrollbars, so text wrapped differently; plain text boxes, and title bars in dark mode, looked different.\n- Fixed on the native desktop: File Explorer's \"Folders on this device\" (import and export a folder) was missing and storage showed 0 B; Settings \u203a About showed \"Apple GPU\" as the graphics; a window's own icon (Flash Player's) was replaced by its app's.\n- New: rudolf-fetch in the Terminal: Rudolf's logo (the nose in red) with this computer's details beside it: Rudolf OS's version, the kernel, the desktop engine, the update channel, the processor, memory, disk and uptime.\n- Fixed: Get in App Store \u203a Rudolf OS Library failed for an app the Library had just updated (Ember Reach 0.4.0): the computer's list still named the older version, so the download was refused as not matching. Rudolf OS now checks the list again and gets the new version.\n- New: beta versions are opt-in: Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Get beta versions (an administrator's name and password to turn on). Turned off, the next update brings the latest stable release, even from a newer beta.\n- The native desktop shows the Phone screen and Blitzen exactly as the standard desktop does: apps' text, drop-down lists and check boxes in its windows are drawn as in the standard desktop.\n- Fixed: when memory ran out, the memory guard could close the desktop's own page (it then stopped responding and the browser was restarted, back to the sign-in screen). The desktop now says which page process is its own until that works, the guard checks that it is still that process, and while it is not known no page of the desktop's browser is closed.\n- New: printers plugged in by USB: the print dialog lists them as \"USB: name\" and Settings \u203a Devices shows each one and its kind. Printers that print over USB with IPP (most sold since about 2015) and PostScript printers need the new USB printers system extension; printers that read PDF print straight away; others are named as needing a driver Rudolf OS does not have.\n- New: signed system extensions (Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a System extensions): parts such as printer support added to Rudolf OS without a new release, laid over the system read-only at each start and never changing it; only Rudolf OS's own signed ones, installed by an administrator, left out if one would replace a part of Rudolf OS or keeps it from starting, with Start once without extensions.\n- The native desktop is the desktop a new installation starts with (it uses less memory and starts faster); a computer that is updated keeps its desktop engine, and Settings offers the native desktop once. Settings \u203a System health \u203a Desktop engine still has Standard and Lite, and Standard is used if Native does not start.\n- The native desktop has the screen saver and its lock, as the standard desktop (not while sound plays, a call or a full-screen app is on, or an app keeps the screen on).\n- The native desktop has the window keys now, as the standard desktop: Alt+Tab's switcher, Super+Tab's overview of all windows, Super+D, Super+Up and Down, Alt+F4, Super+E, R, I and L, Ctrl+Esc and Super for the start menu, F1, and your own keys from Settings \u203a Keyboard.\n- Linux apps can keep the screen on or the computer awake: a game, a video or a download in a Linux app now holds off the screen saver, the lock, the screen turning off and sleep while it asks.\n- Devices are now set up by udev, as on other Linux distributions, so Linux apps, Steam and the modem service find hardware the usual way; mdev stays as a fallback if udev cannot start.\n- The keyring's own questions are now asked by Rudolf OS: when an app creates a new keyring or needs one unlocked, the desktop asks for its password in Rudolf's own prompt, naming the app, instead of the request failing.\n- Settings \u203a Privacy \u203a Saved passwords for apps: see which apps keep sign-ins in this account's keyring and remove them; Flatpak apps can now keep theirs too (the Secret portal).\n- New: App Store \u203a Rudolf OS Library says when its list of apps was last checked (and when the Library published it), with a Refresh button at the end of its first line: the list was kept until it stopped working, so a working list days old never showed new apps or updates (Ember Reach on the Vivobook, 2026-11-14)\n- A keyring for Linux apps: apps such as the Claude app, VS Code and other Electron apps now keep their sign-ins in the account's own keyring (the Secret Service), unlocked by signing in, instead of warning that GNOME Keyring is not set up or asking again after every restart.\n- Settings \u203a System health \u203a Log video problems works on the native desktop too (its video and sound warnings, and Oracle's video and sound messages there), and the native desktop's log is now kept across a restart and included in the system report.\n- Planned: what 1.5.9 had left, moved here on 2026-11-15 (docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 15): the native desktop's comparison with Standard, the official recovery's repair at each start, the Vivobook's call and feature checks, QEMU security checks, Lite's tab fixes, the hotspot, ARM64's test channel, Waydroid and the first phone.\n- Planned: a keyring for Linux apps (the Secret Service), unlocked by the account's sign-in, so apps such as the Claude app keep their sign-ins without a plain file or a second password.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 4): workspaces as on Standard: the taskbar's 1 2 3 buttons, Ctrl+Alt+Left and Right (with Shift the window goes too), a window's Move to workspace N, the taskbar listing the shown workspace's windows, \"Workspace N\" said in a toast (compared with Chromium: new same_t.sh cases ws2, ws2-dark, ws2-left; the same steps on both engines: new test iso/qt/native/wsbeh_t.js). Fixed: a notification with no text under its title is as tall as the page's.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 3's last part): the taskbar at the left or the right of the screen, as on Standard: a column as wide as its widest part, the start button, the pinned apps, the open windows one under the other and the tray at the bottom; the desktop's icons, windows, the start menu and the pop-ups placed beside it.\n- Changed: the plan: signed system extensions (Part 13): drivers, firmware and tools added to one computer as signed read-only layers over the unchanged base, instead of a conventional package manager; USB printers (Part 8) are their first use.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 3's last part, first half): the network's pop-up (the adapters, the one in use, Network Settings\u2026); a notification shows in the screen's corner as on Standard (sliding in, gone after its time, a click doing its own action or opening the notifications' centre, \u26a1 Why?), not inside the window of the app that sent it, and is in the centre and counted on the bell at once; the taskbar's \ud83c\udf99\ufe0f while an app uses the microphone, muting it for everything with a click (\ud83d\udd07); the bell shows \ud83d\udd15 with Do not disturb and counts the notifications come since signing in; Meta+N and Meta+A; a setting changed in one app's window reaches the others. Fixed: a dismissed notification could come back for a moment; the shell's dark pop-ups and menus followed the dark theme's switch alone, not the light or dark mode; Settings asked for at a page by another app's window opens at that page.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 3): quick settings (\u2630: its switches, the network, Lock, Settings, Power) and the notifications' centre (\ud83d\udd14: Do not disturb, Clear all, each notification with its time, \u2715 and Blitzen's Why? for a failure), drawn as the desktop page draws them on Standard; the battery's button opens Settings' System health.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 3, second part): the calendar under the taskbar's clock (the month's weeks, today, the Calendar's events and the next four, the months before and after, Open Calendar, a day double-clicked opening the Calendar there) and the volume's pop-up (its slider, Mute, Desktop sounds\u2026), drawn as the desktop page draws them on Standard.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 3, first part): the taskbar's right-click menus as the page's, row by row: the taskbar's own (Show desktop, Restore all windows, Cascade windows, Close all windows, Taskbar settings), the start button's, a pinned app's (with each app's own rows: Oracle's New private tab), a window's button's (read from the window's page: Restore, Minimize, Maximize, Always on top, Center on screen, Pin or Unpin, Print / Save as PDF\u2026, Close, Force quit), the sound's, the network's and the clock's (compared with Chromium: new same_t.sh cases tbm-*; the same steps on both engines: new test iso/qt/native/tbbeh_t.js). Fixed: the shell's menus are placed as the page's (above the pointer when they would not fit below); Open Command Prompt opens the page's Command Prompt (terminal).\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 2): the desktop's widgets (the clock, the calendar, the sticky note, the system monitor and the weather) drawn by the shell exactly as the page draws them (compared with Chromium: new same_t.sh cases widgets, widgets-ask, widgets-top, widgets-over, widget-picker), each where the account left it: dragged by its header, removed with its \u2715, added from the desktop's menu (Add a widget\u2026, the page's picker), the note typed in as the page's textarea (its keys, menu, undo and copy and paste), the weather from Open-Meteo (a click opens Weather), each with the page's timers; saved as the page saves them (bd95widgets, kept by rudolfd until an app's page takes it). The same steps on both engines: new test iso/qt/native/wgbeh_t.js. App windows' pages no longer run the widgets' timers.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 1, second part, drag and drop): a desktop icon follows the pointer as it is dragged; a file dropped on an app's window opens in that app in a window of its own (as the page's __dropFileOnWindow), a file or a shortcut of your own dropped on the Trash goes there; an Explorer window's files dragged onto another window (another page) are dropped there as on Standard (moved between Explorer windows, opened in an app's window). Fixed: on Native each window's page now sees the files the other windows changed (a page that saved tells the others, which read them again), so a window no longer shows or saves back old files. Fixed: an app's window on Native showed its page at the size GTK first asked for (larger than the window, its right and bottom cut off) and was not drawn again where another window uncovered it (the shell's window manager replaced GDK's own X events for its windows); the shell no longer crashes when a window closes while its frame is still there; chosen desktop icons stay chosen when the desktop's list comes again.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 1, second part): the Desktop folder's files and folders as icons in the page's places; the page's right-click menus (the desktop's, a file's, the Trash's, an app's) and its Rename and Open with dialogs drawn by the shell as the page draws them (compared with Chromium: new same_t.sh cases desk-files, desk-menu, file-menu, file-rename, file-openwith); New folder, New text document, Rename (F2), Move to Trash (Delete), Open with, Properties and Empty trash done by the page itself in a hidden one-app window (#app=do-desk, #app=do-fs). Fixed: an app's window or a hidden page no longer squeezes the desktop's icon places into its own size (its grid is the screen's).\n- Fixed: the native desktop (Native engine) now looks as Standard does, measured part by part against the desktop page in Chromium (new test iso/qt/native/same_t.sh, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, \"The rule\"): the taskbar in its five styles, its buttons' styles and the accent colour's modes (the page's sizes, font, corners, gloss, edges and shadow; the network, sound and battery as the page draws them; the workspaces' buttons; the notifications' count), the desktop's icons (the page's font, wrapping, picture and text shadows), the start menu (the page's own list, with its search box, headings and greyed rows), the windows' frames and title bars in their five styles and colour modes (the page's sizes, round corners, shadow and resize handles; an app's window opens at the page's size and place), and the sign-in and lock screens. Meta+L locks the screen; Start \u203a Shut Down... shows the page's Power dialog.\n- Fixed: with Oracle's Dark pages on, part of a video could be drawn as a negative: on YouTube, while the mouse was on the progress bar, the video from the player's left edge to the pointer. A layer over a video (a picture, a canvas, or anything with its own background picture, with a blur behind it) inverted the video under it once more, and so did a video inside such a layer; each is now turned back only once. Also fixed: a dark page whose colour is set on the page itself rather than on its body was turned light.\n- New: the native desktop (Native engine, docs/plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, item 1, first part): the desktop's icons, as the page shows them for the account (its shortcuts, pictures and places), on the page's grid at the icon size and display scaling: a double-click opens, dragging moves an icon to the nearest free place (kept by rudolfd), Arrange icons by name, Refresh, and the icon's and the desktop's menus. Files on the desktop come next.\n\n## 1.5.9 (2026-11-15)\nSummary: A native desktop to try (Native, test), Browse with Tor in Oracle, protections against a directed attack, developer mode with custom builds and an official recovery, more memory for games, calls and microphone tests, and Save and Open dialogs with document tabs (docs/plan-1.5.9.md)\n- Fixed: Browse with Tor on Standard: clicking the Tor window's bookmarks bar, address box or buttons no longer jumps back to the desktop, and the Tor window is signed in as the desktop is instead of showing the sign-in screen.\n- Fixed: the self-test run over the read-only development link failed its listeners check when a Linux app was open (it cannot look into other users' programs there); it now says so instead.\n- New: Browse with Tor in Oracle: an onion button at the right end of the bookmarks bar sends that window's tabs through Tor, in a private Tor window of its own (Rudolf's Chromium through Tor: sites do not see the computer's address, and no site's name or WebRTC address leaves outside Tor); pressing it again brings the tabs back to a normal window. The window says plainly that this is not Tor Browser's anonymity and links Tor Browser (plan 16.2).\n- New: WordPro's hyperlinks as in other word processors: link to a web address, a place in the document (a heading or a bookmark), a file on this computer or an e-mail address with a subject; the text to show and a ScreenTip; bookmarks; a link's right-click menu (Open, Edit, Copy address, Remove); typed web and e-mail addresses becoming links; and a list of every link with Go to, Edit, Remove and Remove all.\n- New: Tor in the system (for Oracle's Browse with Tor, plan 16.1): it runs only while a Tor window is open, as a user of its own, listening only on this computer (127.0.0.1:9050), with nothing kept on the disk; System health notes when it starts, is ready, stops or fails.\n- Fixed: the Save As box of Notepad, WordLite, WordPro and Paint (and the Run box) ignored typing: its Enter handler cancelled every other key.\n- New: Notepad, WordLite and WordPro save and open through a dialog like the File Explorer: folders, a name, and the file type from a list (Notepad: text, Markdown, web page, Lua, CSV, JSON, batch; WordLite and WordPro: rich document, web page, text). Notepad and WordLite keep several documents in tabs under their buttons (New, Open, Ctrl+T, Ctrl+W, Ctrl+Tab), a file opened from elsewhere joins the open window as a tab, and unsaved tabs are asked about.\n- Fixed: another Rudolf OS stick plugged in (an older one, for example) never showed in the File Explorer: every disk with a Rudolf OS partition was hidden, not only the one Rudolf OS runs from. It now shows; its data partition opens read-only and its start-up parts stay hidden.\n- New: Settings \u203a Volume Controls \u203a Microphones has a list of the microphones (the one chosen becomes every app's, a call's too) and a Test button: an equalizer and a level meter show what the chosen microphone hears.\n- Fixed: Oracle on Native (test): a link opened in a new window, Ctrl+N or a web page's file from another app opens an Oracle window of its own, as on Standard, not a window inside Oracle's; closing Oracle while a download is arriving no longer shows Chromium's \"Download is in progress\" question: the window goes, the download is still put in Downloads and told, then Chromium closes; files saved in Oracle and in an app window that is open are seen by each other at once (before, one could overwrite the other); text copied in Oracle stays on the clipboard after Oracle closes; the volume keys' indicator appears at the top of the screen, not inside a window.\n- Fixed: calls heard nothing on laptops whose built-in microphones are on another sound chip than the speakers (the Vivobook's AMD microphones): the headset jack's microphone was made the default even with nothing plugged in. It is now the default only when a headset is plugged in.\n- New: Oracle's bookmarks bar ends with a \"+\" that adds a bookmark by its address, from the browsing history (newest first, searchable), or as a file on this computer (a document or a PDF).\n- Fixed: choosing a file in the desktop's own file picker (Open a presentation or a spreadsheet, and others) answered as if nothing had been chosen.\n- Changed: the plan: Browse with Tor in Oracle (Part 16): a button on the bookmarks bar sends that window through Tor, which runs only while it is used.\n- Fixed: a USB tethering link or cable without internet took over from a working Wi-Fi, cutting the computer off (found with a MiFi without a data plan on the same network range as the home router). A new wired connection is now used only once the internet answers through it, and is tried again every 30 seconds; until then the Wi-Fi keeps the route and its DNS, and the network menu and Settings say \"No internet\" for it.\n- New: Recovery mode in the boot menu now starts its own start-up file, recovery.efi, signed with the Rudolf OS key. It starts only the official recovery system kept on this computer (system/r), after checking that its release list is signed by the official key (never by Developer mode's extra key) and that every file has the size and SHA-256 that list says. A changed or wrongly signed recovery system is refused, with the file and the reason shown, and nothing else is started in its place.\n- Fixed: the self-test's listeners check failed while a Linux app ran (the Claude app's own sockets inside Debian, Chromium's newer socket folder), and its Debian check failed when run over the read-only development link; a Linux app's own sockets inside its container are now said as such, and the Debian check says when it cannot look.\n- New: the self-test says which system runs (the Official Rudolf OS, or a Custom build in Developer mode with the key that signed it), and every development link result names the system that answered.\n- Fixed: the development link could not be switched on with the official builds. It needed Developer mode, which can only be turned on by trusting a developer's key. It now has its own switch, \"Allow the development link on this computer\" (an administrator's password; unticking it ends the link), and Developer mode moved from Update and recovery to Settings \u203a Security, just above the development link.\n- Changed (security): the display needs authorization. X no longer lets every program on the computer watch the screen, read the keys and type into the desktop: it starts with a new cookie only root can read, and only root, the desktop's user and the signed-in account's own Linux user may use it (taken back at sign-out). Debian's Linux programs and Wine now run as the signed-in account's own Linux user too, with the account's folder as their home (Wine's programs and their Start menu entries are the account's), and Library packages started from the desktop as well; another account's programs cannot reach them (plan 15.3, second step).\n- Changed (security): each account is its own Linux user (uid 1001 and up), made and removed with the account. Its files belong to it alone, and the Linux apps and Flatpak apps it starts run as it, with its own session bus and desktop portal, so one account's apps cannot read another's files; signing out stops all of them. Existing accounts' files are given to their users at the first start (ownership only, logged; what cannot be moved is named in System health). The desktop's page and API now also check the desktop's own user. Debian and Wine follow in the next step.\n- New (security): the development link can only look at the computer (read files and logs, take screenshots) unless \"Allow changes to this computer\" is ticked in Settings \u203a Security \u203a Development link, which needs an administrator's password and is unticked again at every start. Read-only is enforced by the system: its commands run as an unprivileged user in a read-only view of the files, without the link's token; bringing files and restarting are refused. The banner says which.\n- Changed (security): fewer and harder doors. A list of everything that listens on the computer (TCP ports and Unix sockets), with who may use each and how it checks; the self-test fails when something listens that the list does not name. The system programs are built with the stack protector, FORTIFY_SOURCE=3, PIE, full RELRO and control-flow protection, and each release checks the flags on the built programs. rudolfd's request parsing and JSON are fuzzed before each release; the fuzzing found and fixed a read past the end of a JSON string ending in a broken escape, and a request with a negative or second Content-Length is now refused.\n- New (security): a daily check compares the Chromium and the kernel of the latest release with the newest ones Ubuntu has published (the chromium snap, Ubuntu's mainline builds of the release's kernel series) and opens one issue \"Security update available: \u2026\" when either is newer, never a second while one is open; it builds and releases nothing (it replaces the Chromium check). Settings \u203a About shows the release's Chromium and its date.\n- Changed (security): the development link is off unless it is being used. It can be switched on only in Developer mode, by an administrator whose password rudolfd now checks itself; Ask first is on again after every restart; the link is off after a restart unless \"Keep on after restarting\" is ticked (off by default), and turns itself off after 4 hours without a command, with a notification. System health shows whether it is on, since when, who switched it on and its last command.\n- Fixed (security): WordPro's cleaning of documents and pasted text could be got round (a slash for a space before onerror, an SVG animation, javascript: links), so a crafted document could run script in the desktop. It now uses the browser's own parser (safeHtml) and removes everything that can run. A test opens 19 hostile files in every app; a new check (htmlsinks, run with deadcheck) catches new places that make HTML from data; and the page now forbids plug-in content and outside <base> addresses.\n- Fixed (security): rudolfd gave the desktop's page, with its API token and the page's stored data, to any program on the computer, so a Linux app, a Wine program or a Flatpak could use the system API, which runs as root. Now the page and the API answer only the desktop's own processes (its memory group) and root; others are refused and System health says so (Security). The kernel also gets Yama, so programs cannot read the browser's memory either. The self-test checks both.\n- Changed: the plan: a security part (Part 15): only the desktop gets the page and its API token (programs on the computer can read it today), files opened in apps kept apart from the rest of the desktop, a Linux user per account (moved from 1.6.0), the devlink off unless used, fewer and hardened listening services, and a daily check for Chromium and kernel security updates.\n- Changed: the plan: on the native desktop, Oracle will run on Chromium with Rudolf's tab shell, as on Standard (real tabs, the same sign-ins and private tabs), started only while Oracle is open (plan Part 12, step 8).\n- New: Display Properties says \"Note: Dynamic effects are disabled on power-saver mode.\" under Dynamic Backgrounds, only while power saver (or battery saver) is on, when Spirit Falls' petals and lanterns and Glacier Lake's aurora stand still.\n- Changed: Rudolf OS's calendars show weeks from Sunday to Saturday (they began on Monday): the Calendar app, the calendar under the taskbar's clock and the calendar widget.\n- New: private browsing in Oracle: New private tab in Oracle's \u2630 menu, Ctrl+Shift+N, and when you right-click Oracle's icon (on the taskbar, in the Start menu, on the desktop, or an open Oracle window's taskbar button). A private window is marked \ud83d\udd76\ufe0f Private in dark violet; its pages are not kept in History, its cookies and site data are kept in memory only (with Standard and Lite) and forgotten when the last private window closes; pop-ups from it stay private. Help \u203a Privacy explains what is kept (bookmarks, downloads) and who can still see what you visit.\n- Changed: the plans: on the native desktop Rudolf OS must appear and work exactly as it does on Standard (Chromium): every part of the native shell, and later every native app, is compared with Standard's in screenshots and behaviour, and each difference is a bug (plan-1.6.0.md Part 12, plan-2.0.md).\n- Fixed: Native (test)'s six bugs seen on the Vivobook (plan 12.3b step 7): a picture of your own shows from the first start (rudolfd makes the shell's copy from the page's database mirror when no page has given it yet); the screen shows the arrow, not X's bare cross; the taskbar follows Display Properties' display scaling and draws the apps' pictures as the page does (not emoji), with the version on the start button; drop-downs in app windows are drawn as in Lite (the page now knows Native's app windows are WebKit); the development link's red banner is drawn once by the shell at the top of the screen (windows kept below it, End now, and it asks about commands itself), no longer inside every app window, where it hid Settings' search box.\n- Changed: the plans: Rudolf OS goes native (decided from the native engine's measurements on the Vivobook: 854 MB used at idle against Standard's 1,110 MB). Native (test)'s bugs seen on the Vivobook are fixed in 1.5.9 (plan Part 12, step 7); the native shell gets everything the web desktop's shell has and becomes the default in 1.6.0 (plan-1.6.0.md Part 12); the apps are rewritten natively, one by one, for 2.0 (docs/plan-2.0.md).\n- New: Settings \u203a System health \u203a Desktop engine \u203a Native (test): the native desktop engine's prototype (plan Part 12), with Lite's safeguards: on trial until its sign-in screen appears, and given up for Standard (said in System health and Settings) after a start that does not get there; never in Recovery mode. Ctrl+Alt+Backspace turns it off too.\n- New: the native desktop engine's prototype is a window manager too (plan Part 12): every window gets a title bar in the title bar styles and colours, with minimise, maximise and close, moving and resizing, focus on click, full screen for games and videos, dialogs centred over their window, and the open windows on the taskbar.\n- New: rudolf-native, the native desktop engine's prototype (plan Part 12): the wallpaper, the taskbar in its styles and accent colours, the pinned apps, the tray, the clock, the start menu and the sign-in and lock screens drawn natively (GTK), each app in a WebKit window of its own, all sharing one WebKit process. Not offered in Settings yet.\n- New: rudolfd answers the native desktop engine's questions (plan Part 12): the accounts for its sign-in screen (never a hash), the sign-in itself, checked as the desktop page checks it and opening the account's encrypted folder, and the desktop's settings, pinned apps and start menu; the desktop page gives it the start menu's list and a copy of your own background picture. Written down in docs/api.md.\n- New: the desktop page's one-app mode (for the native desktop engine being prototyped, plan Part 12): index.html#app=NAME starts one app alone, filling the window, signed in from the native session; closing the app writes its files and closes the page.\n- New: Display Properties \u203a Taskbar \u203a Buttons: the taskbar's buttons (start, pinned apps, open windows, workspaces, the tray) in a style of their own (Standard, Wireframe, Monochrome, Candy, Retro), in the accent colour as Normal, Negative or Complementary when \"Match the theme colours\" is ticked, otherwise in each style's own colours.\n- Fixed: plugging in an Android phone with USB debugging or file transfer on no longer says Rudolf OS has no driver for it: apps use those directly, without a driver.\n- New: the PDF Viewer prints (\ud83d\udda8\ufe0f Print, or Ctrl+P): the PDF itself, on its own paper size, to a network printer (IPP Everywhere / AirPrint) or saved as a copy, through the same print dialog as WordPro.\n- Fixed: the self-test said the desktop's browser was not in the desktop's memory group when it was.\n- Fixed: the start-up chime plays when the sign-in screen appears, not only after the first key or click there (on installed Rudolf OS; the browser version still needs a key or click first, as browsers require).\n- Fixed: the sign-in page after a start-up showed a teal background instead of the desktop's picture: it now remembers which background the desktop has (your picture, a drawn background, or the colours) rather than how it looked at that moment, and a drawn background shows its light or dark picture for the time of day.\n- New: Settings \u203a Date, time and language \u203a Share my language with apps (on by default): Library apps such as games are told Rudolf OS's language, when they start and whenever it changes, so they can show their own menus in it. Off, apps are not told which language you use.\n- Fixed: the screen saver no longer stays off for good after something went full screen in a window that was then minimised or hidden (that also kept game mode on), or after a saver's picture was taken away; why the saver waits (something full screen, sound playing, a microphone in use) is written to the page log.\n- New: the Feather Sky static background: Rudolf's logo in a sky of swirling white feathers (a night-blue version in dark mode).\n- New: two static backgrounds: Carbon Fibre (Rudolf's logo in brushed metal on carbon fibre, for work and gaming) and Moonlit Stag (Rudolf on a boulder above a mountain lake, at night in dark mode and at dawn in light mode).\n- Changed: Display Properties no longer has the colour presets (Sunset, Ocean, Classic Teal and the others, there since 1.1); Background style and Color 1 / Color 2 still make simple colour and pattern backgrounds. The drawn backgrounds are now in two rows: Dynamic Backgrounds, the ones that move (Spirit Falls, Glacier Lake), and Static Backgrounds (Alpine Peak, Obsidian Waves, Gilded Crest, Teal Prism, Slate Panels). Both still have a light and a dark version.\n- New: Display Properties \u203a Title bars: the windows' title bars in the taskbar's styles (Standard, Wireframe, Monochrome, Candy, Retro), in the accent colour as Normal, Negative or Complementary. The window you are using has the colour; the others stay grey.\n- New: Search in Settings: a search box at the top of Settings. As you type, a drop-down lists the pages, sections and settings that still match, each with where it is underneath in smaller text (Settings \u203a Network Settings \u203a Wi-Fi). Choose one (click, or the arrow keys and Enter) to open its page with that setting highlighted.\n- New: game mode puts Oracle's background tabs to sleep to free memory for the game. A sleeping tab keeps its address and shows \ud83d\udca4; selecting it loads it again. Tabs playing sound or holding typed text stay awake. Blitzen's model is unloaded too (unless Blitzen is in front), and system, Linux app and Debian updates wait until the game ends. Settings \u203a System health \u203a Memory can turn it off.\n- New: when memory is getting low, Rudolf OS warns before it has to close a page, also inside a full-screen game (\"Memory is almost full: save your progress if you can\"), and game mode puts more tabs to sleep.\n- Changed: the desktop page is about 2 MB smaller (5.5 to 3.5 MB): the dynamic backgrounds' pictures, the fonts and the Super Nintendo player are now separate files, and SpreadsheetPro and PresentPro load the first time they are opened.\n- Changed: with compressed memory on, idle memory is moved into it sooner (swappiness 180), so a game that suddenly needs more finds room; the memory warning now comes whenever free memory is low.\n- Changed: the graphics driver keeps at most 64 MB of spare memory for reuse (it could grow to half the memory over a long session).\n- New: Task Manager \u203a Performance shows where the memory goes: programs and pages, the file cache, the kernel, graphics, the SSD's buffer, compressed memory, and what Linux cannot itemise while the system is protected.\n- New: Task Manager \u203a Performance has Clear cache, which frees the memory holding recently used files at once; game mode does the same when a game starts, so the game finds free memory as it loads.\n- New: when a game starts, apps running in the background with no window (such as Discord) are offered to be quit, in a notice inside the game with a button; nothing is quit unless you press it.\n- Fixed: Wi-Fi dropped for about 45 seconds when it tried a radio of the router that never answers (the Vivobook's router's 2.4 GHz radio). Such a radio is now left out reliably, and remembered for a day, restarts included.\n- New: Settings \u203a Network \u203a Wi-Fi \u203a Radios of this network: each radio of the router (2.4, 5 and 6 GHz under one name) with its channel, signal and state, and a switch to stop this computer using one; it also says which bands this computer's Wi-Fi has.\n- New: \"Why does the Wi-Fi drop?\" (Settings \u203a Network \u203a Wi-Fi, or asking Blitzen about a Wi-Fi problem): Blitzen reads the Wi-Fi's log, the kernel's Wi-Fi messages and the radios, and recommends only the Wi-Fi settings that exist.\n- Improved: Blitzen's Wi-Fi answer now rests on findings worked out from the logs (which radio did not answer, how strong each radio is) and names the setting to change, instead of retelling earlier versions from the documentation.\n- Fixed: the Wi-Fi tool that reads the Wi-Fi chip's bands (iw) is now in the system, so \"This computer's Wi-Fi radios\" and the radios' bands show on real computers (without it the list was empty).\n- New: \"This computer's Wi-Fi radios\" (Settings \u203a Network \u203a Wi-Fi), a drop-down menu with a check by each of the computer's own Wi-Fi radios (2.4 / 5 / 6 GHz) that is on. Only an administrator can change it, with their password, and Rudolf OS never changes it by itself.\n- Security: Library web apps run in a sandbox of their own (no access to the desktop, its storage or rudolfd), talking to it only through the game kit's messages; an app from an outside developer gets only what it asks for and you allow when you first open it (saves, game controllers, full screen). Needed before apps from outside developers can be in the Library.\n- New: an official recovery system on every installed computer: the newest official release that started successfully is kept (without using extra room), and Recovery mode starts it. Only releases signed with the official key ever become the recovery system.\n- New: Developer mode (Settings \u203a Update and recovery, administrators): install your own Rudolf OS builds, signed with your publisher key, from your own address, after a strong warning, a typed confirmation and the password. Settings \u203a About says whether the running system is the Official Rudolf OS or a Custom build, and with which key.\n- New: Return to official Rudolf OS (Settings \u203a Update and recovery, and offered in Recovery mode): Developer mode off, the official Rudolf OS at the next restart, keeping your accounts, files and apps or erasing everything; the custom build is removed once the official one has started.\n- New: User Settings \u203a Developer: make a publisher key for your own games and builds. Its private half is kept only in a backup file protected by a passphrase; the account keeps the public half and its fingerprint.\n- New: docs/custom-builds.md, how to make your own Rudolf OS (keys, update address, Library) and go back to the official one; its commands are checked in CI.\n- New: packages from outside developers, through a chain of trust: the Rudolf OS Library certifies a developer (their key and the names they may use); their apps install only as desktop apps in the sandbox, under those names; the Library can revoke a developer or a package, which is then disabled on every computer at its next check.\n- New: video calls: with the Lite engine, Google Meet, Zoom's web client, Teams and other call sites open in Chromium's window (Ubuntu's WebKitGTK has no WebRTC); test calls on both engines in CI.\n- New: \"In a call\" in the taskbar (\ud83c\udf99\ufe0f while an app uses the microphone; one click mutes it for everything); a call keeps the screen saver, the lock and sleep away; changing the sound output moves the call too, and a headset plugged in takes over.\n- New: cellular modems are recognised (ModemManager, started only while a modem is plugged in): Settings \u203a Network shows each one with its SIM, operator and signal. Connecting with a SIM comes in 1.6.0.\n- New: Share this connection (Settings \u203a Network, administrators): the computer's Wi-Fi becomes a hotspot (WPA2/WPA3) for other devices, sharing a cable, a phone's USB tethering or a modem (or the Wi-Fi itself on chips that can do both). Off by default and after a restart.\n- New: Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Calls to phones: what Google Voice, Zoom Phone and Teams Phone need to call phone numbers; a phone number clicked on a web page asks how to call it (Google Voice, Teams, the phone on the desk, or copy it), once if you like.\n- Changed: Chromium comes only from Ubuntu's open-source Chromium (the snap). When it cannot be fetched, the release keeps the Chromium it has, instead of taking another kind of browser; it never goes back to an older version.\n- Fixed: when a system file does not match its release, the start-up and Task Manager say so plainly, with its size, instead of a low-level error.\n- New: on the Native desktop (test), Oracle runs in Rudolf OS's own Chromium with real browser tabs, as on Standard: websites open as real pages, sign-ins are kept in the same place as on Standard, and one Chromium serves every Oracle window, started with the first and closed with the last. Its windows are framed by the native desktop like every other app; without Chromium, Oracle opens as before and says so once (plan 12.3c).\n- New: on the Native desktop (test), an app opened from Oracle (a downloaded file, a link to another app) opens in a window of its own, as from any other app (plan 12.3c, step 5).\n- New: on the Native desktop (test), Oracle's notifications show in the screen's corner and in the notifications' centre like every other app's, and clicking one does what it says (plan 12.3c, step 5).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop (test), Ctrl+W in a website in Oracle closed Oracle's whole window, and Ctrl+Q closed every Oracle window. Oracle's keys now work as on Standard: Ctrl+W closes the tab, Ctrl+T opens one, Ctrl+Shift+N opens a private window of its own, and Ctrl+Q and Alt+F4 do what they do on Standard (plan 12.3c, step 5).\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop (test), the desktop's own full screen in Oracle's window (a video or a game shown full screen by the desktop) filled only Oracle's window. It now fills the screen, as on Standard.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop (test), Open with in Oracle or in another app's window opened the chosen app inside that window. It now opens the file in a window of its own, as on Standard.\n- Fixed: on the Native desktop (test), a website's download could be taken by two windows at once, one saying it was saved and the other that it stopped. Oracle files every download, as the desktop does on Standard.\n\n## 1.5.8 (2026-11-12)\nSummary: Diagnosing a computer that does not start, full rescue sticks, Oracle's tabs in the Lite desktop, mobile data, Android phones and tablets, and new dynamic backgrounds (docs/plan-1.5.8.md)\n- Fixed: when the memory guard ends a web page, the desktop now leaves full screen at once and says so, also when only a frame inside a page was ended (a game on a website), which left the screen full screen on a broken frame before.\n- Fixed: a page closed to free memory while full screen (a game in an Oracle tab) left the screen full screen and black, and Ctrl+Alt+Del's Task Manager opened behind it. A tab that ends now leaves full screen, and Task Manager and the shutdown dialog leave full screen first. Settings pages also no longer react to changes on a page shown before them.\n- Fixed: the moving parts of the dynamic backgrounds (northern lights, petals, lanterns) kept running behind games and over a picture of your own, which made Friday Night Funkin' choppy. They now run only when they can be seen, stop in power saver, and use half the frames on battery. Choosing a dynamic background now puts it on the screen even when a picture was set. Fixed: System health showed memory 1,024 times too large (\"1201.3 GB of 3415.5 GB\"). The battery's time left now follows the power used over the last few minutes, not one moment's reading.\n- New: Make a Rudolf OS USB stick makes a full rescue stick, or none. It checks the stick's size before erasing anything, keeps the system it copies a second time as the stick's rescue system (no extra room; updates on the stick never change it), and starts that rescue system when no updated system on the stick can. A stick that could not be finished is cleared instead of being left half-made. Settings \u203a Storage says what each plugged-in Rudolf OS stick holds, and a boot-files-only image says so in its boot menu.\n- New: games from the Rudolf OS Library, starting with Fault Lines (a logic puzzle: clear the ground without opening a fault; three fields and a Daily one). It is in the Start menu and App Store \u203a Games, and downloads its small package (16 KB) the first time. Best times are kept with your account, and the game pauses when its window is minimised or the computer locks.\n- New: Night Flight, a bullet-hell shooter from the Rudolf OS Library: Rudolf flies up through a winter night firing lasers from his glowing nose, charges a piercing beam, grazes bullets and saves corrupted friends. Stages 1 and 2 with their bosses (more to come), Story, Practice and Score Attack, three difficulties, and your last flight to watch again. Keyboard, controller or touch; in the Start menu and App Store \u203a Games.\n- New: Frost Barricade, for older players (cartoon horror and combat), from the Rudolf OS Library: hold a barricade against corrupted elves for twenty-five nights, through fog, blizzards and a full moon, with a weather radio,, with nine winter weapons, days of repairs, searching and training, and survivors who guard, repair or scout. In the Start menu and App Store \u203a Games.\n- New: Logic Pack, puzzles from the Rudolf OS Library, made fresh every time with exactly one answer: Sudoku, Picture Grid (nonograms: fill the cells the numbers describe to find a picture) and Cages (each number once per row and column; cages add, take away, multiply or divide to their targets). Easy, Medium, Hard and a Daily puzzle of each kind, the same for everyone. Notes, undo, and hints that point out a wrong entry or do the next step and say why. The puzzle you are on and your best times are kept with your account. In the Start menu and App Store \u203a Games.\n- New: Block Drop, a falling-blocks puzzle from the Rudolf OS Library: fill rows to clear them, and watch for prisms, which burst every block of their colour so the blocks above tumble down and new rows clear in a chain. Classic gets faster as you go, Relaxed keeps one speed, and the Daily game is three minutes on the same blocks for everyone. Each colour has its own mark, so colour is never the only clue. Keyboard, controller, mouse or touch (swipe to move, tap to turn, flick down to drop); in the Start menu and App Store \u203a Games.\n- Changed: Frost Barricade is now **25 Days**: a grittier look, an angular Rudolf like Rudolf OS's portrait, Rudolf moving up and down behind a longer barricade, shots that fly past the crosshair, unlimited ammunition, faster elves, and survivors who miss until trained and panic when the barricade is nearly gone.\n- New: with the Lite desktop, Oracle's tabs are real browser tabs again (WebKit pages): websites open inside Oracle, signing in works, and only sites that need Chromium (Netflix, Spotify, Disney+ and other protected video and music) open in Chromium's browser window. Oracle's menu has \"Open in Chromium\" for any page.\n- New: Oracle's menu \u203a Sign-in with FedCM: Chromium's own way for \"Sign in with Google\" buttons, off by default (on some computers Google's sign-in through it stopped on a blank page). It applies to the browser window and to Oracle's tabs on the Standard desktop.\n- New: mobile data. A hotspot's or a phone's USB tethering, a cellular modem or a USB network adapter plugged in at any time gets connected by itself (\"\ud83d\udcf1 Phone connected\"). Hotspots and phones that use RNDIS, NCM, MBIM or EEM, iPhones' tethering, and QMI modems are supported.\n- New: metered connections. Cellular modems, tethered phones and networks that say so (Android hotspots) are metered by themselves, and any network can be set in Settings \u203a Network \u203a Metered connection. On a metered connection updates are found but not downloaded by themselves, and Linux apps and Debian are not updated in the background; Settings shows the data used this month.\n- New: opening an Android app (.apk) shows what it is (its name, version, the Android it needs and what it asks for) without running it, and offers what works now: the app on your phone through Phone screen, its web version, or the App Store.\n- New: Phone screen shows an Android phone's screen in a window, used with this computer's mouse and keyboard (scrcpy, from Linux). It needs USB debugging turned on by the phone's owner; Rudolf OS never changes anything on the phone.\n- New: tablet mode, by itself on a touch-only screen or in Settings \u203a Display: each window fills the screen, the taskbar becomes a dock, swipe up from the bottom for Start and in from the right for notifications.\n- New: the browser version of Rudolf OS can be added to a phone's or tablet's home screen, and opens without a connection.\n- New: System health \u203a Last starts shows how each of the last four starts went, from logs kept on the disk (they outlasted no restart before).\n- New: holding Ctrl+Alt+Backspace for a second starts the desktop again with the standard engine, when the desktop shows nothing or keeps restarting.\n- Fixed: a sound chip that did not answer at start-up (no sound after forced power-offs) is reset once by itself; the self-test says when it is still silent.\n- Fixed: a big web game loading (Friday Night Funkin' on Newgrounds, at a song's loading screen) was ended by the memory guard after a moment's low memory, while most of the swap was still free and nothing was stalled. A page is now ended for \"thrashing\" only when the system really stalls, the swap is nearly full, or under 4% of the memory is left.\n- Fixed: YouTube's full screen in Oracle went straight back to the normal size. Chromium let pages in Oracle's tabs go full screen only if Rudolf OS's tab shell allowed it for websites, and it allowed it only for itself, so Chromium cancelled every request at once. Websites now go full screen the real way (Chromium's own \"press Esc\" notice), including sites with strict security such as YouTube, where Oracle's stand-in could not run.\n- Fixed: a website opened in Oracle in the first moments after the desktop started stayed in the old kind of tab (no page title, no Ctrl+T, no full screen); it moves to a real tab as soon as they are ready.\n- Fixed: signing out (or the end of a child's time) closed Oracle's windows but left their pages running, hidden.\n- New: Display Properties \u203a Lock and sign-in screen: the background (the desktop's, a picture of your own, or a colour), blur, darkening, clock size, position, and whether the account's picture and name show. The sign-in page now has a clock too. It sits with the other background settings, right after the colours.\n- New: Glacier Lake, a dynamic background (light and dark): a great cliff of ragged buttresses, a wall of snowy peaks cut by gullies with clouds drifting through them and a forested slope, rocky shores of gravel, boulders and driftwood, and a glowing ice cavern at the foot of the peaks, all mirrored in a rippled turquoise lake; at night a full moon lays a glittering path across the water, its image broken by the ripples, and the northern lights move across the sky and the lake in the accent colour (Normal, Negative or Complementary; Display Properties \u203a Dynamic Backgrounds). New: Spirit Falls, a dynamic background (light and dark): a waterfall behind a vermilion arched bridge where spirit lights drift, lanterns, karst towers in the morning fog, bamboo, palms and a cherry tree in full bloom whose petals blow across on the breeze, over a pool with lily pads and lotus flowers in its middle and right, and fallen petals drifting on its left. In a clearing under the palms stands an old stone statue, mossy, cracked and grown over with kudzu, its carvings glowing faintly: one of the twelve animals of the Chinese zodiac, a panda, an elephant, a leopard or a deer, chosen by your accent colour (the glow takes the accent colour, its negative or its complement; Display Properties). At night paper lanterns rise from behind the cliffs and drift across the sky, in the accent colour by the same choice of rule. Fixed: the dynamic background pictures spilled out of their frames; they are now four to a row. Fixed: on the Lite desktop, Settings pages scrolled sideways and on past their end.\n- New: a \"Shutting down\u2026\" / \"Restarting\u2026\" screen that covers everything at once; the shutdown tone now plays to the end before the system's safe shutdown turns the power off.\n- Fixed: the screen saver did not start after its time. Chromium tells the desktop the pointer moved whenever something under a resting pointer changes (the clock, a widget, a page), and the desktop took that as someone using the computer. Only real movement counts now, and the system's own count of input decides.\n- Changed: how the Lite desktop draws follows Settings \u203a Display \u203a Graphics card. By default (\"Use it for games and Linux apps\") the processor draws, on several cores, and the graphics card puts the picture together and moves it: smooth, without the graphics card's large memory use (some 400 MB at rest on a 4 GB laptop). \"Use it for games and the desktop\" has the graphics card draw everything; without a graphics card the processor does it all, with lighter effects (no shadows or blurs). The Lite desktop's page also frees memory it can spare from about 250 MB, instead of from about 1 GB.\n- Added: Settings \u203a System health \u203a Memory \u203a Log video problems. For the Lite desktop, it writes the video and sound system's warnings and errors to a log that the system report includes (off by default; from the next restart).\n- Fixed: with the Lite desktop, YouTube said \"Your browser can't play this video\": WebKit's streaming video needs GStreamer's Opus and VP9 parsers, which were missing. They are included now, with playback speed and subtitles.\n- Fixed: with the Lite desktop, YouTube stopped with \"Something went wrong\" after a minute or so: it had switched to a format Lite could not play (MP4 with H.264 and AAC, or MP3 sounds). Lite now has FFmpeg's decoders for them, as Chromium does.\n- New: orchestral sound cues. Startup's Classic chime and Soft bells, Login success's Classic and Bright ding, Saves & notifications' Classic ding and Gentle bell, and Shutdown's Classic chime and Slow fade now play the melody on bells or a horn over a string chord in bass, tenor and alto, in a small hall, a little longer than before. The earlier sounds stay in Settings \u203a Sound as \"\u2026 (legacy)\".\n- Fixed: changes to Oracle's tabs on the Standard desktop never reached installed computers after 1.5.1 (Chromium updates the tabs' app only to a higher version, and its version had stayed the same). Every release with changes to them now raises it, so YouTube's full-screen fix and the earlier fixes to Oracle's tabs arrive with the next update.\n- Fixed: even with a higher version, Chromium (157) never replaces a tabs' app it installed from its file (\"App is already installed\"), so the computers kept the tabs of 2026-11-01. At start-up the session now compares Chromium's copy with the system's and, when they differ, replaces it once (rudolf-shellfix, through Chromium's remote-control pipe; no network port). Websites in Oracle's tabs ask to sign in again that once, and a notification says so.\n- Fixed: the Lite desktop never drew with the GPU: GTK checks OpenGL through GLX, and the system had no Mesa GLX driver, so WebKit turned its hardware acceleration off on every computer (\"No available configurations for the given pixel format\"). Mesa's GLX (about 400 KB) is now in the system; the self-test checks it (lite-gpu), and the WebKit check fails if acceleration is turned off again.\n- Changed: the development link no longer waits ten minutes in silence when Ask first is on and the desktop is not running: it answers at once that nobody can allow the command.\n- Changed: the start-up message about boot files names the drive it is about; Help has a page on starting from a USB stick.\n\n## 1.5.7 (2026-11-11)\nSummary: Blitzen diagnostics, and what the 1.5.6 tests taught (docs/plan-1.5.7.md)\n- Fixed: the desktop's errors were never written to the system's error log (/run/rudolf/page.log): rudolfd answered the desktop's report as if it were a request to read the log. System health, the system report and the devlink now see them.\n- Changed: the Event Viewer's events are kept in your account's folder too (.rudolf/events.log, with your files on the disk), so they outlast the desktop's storage; events from before signing in are written once it is open.\n- Changed: Rudolf OS's service log (rudolfd) is kept on the disk for this start and the 3 before it (/persist/var/log/rudolfd.log, .1 to .3), instead of being lost at each restart.\n- New: ask Blitzen why something went wrong. Error notifications, problems in System health and errors in the Event Viewer have a \"\u26a1 Why?\" button, and System health has \"What's wrong with this computer?\". Blitzen first shows what it will read (the matching log lines and parts of Rudolf OS's documents) and you can leave anything out; it then explains what happened, the likely cause with the log line it comes from, and what you can do. It changes nothing and runs nothing, and it all stays on this computer.\n- New: \"Draft a report\" under Blitzen's explanation opens Report a problem filled in with it and the log lines it read, for you to check and send yourself.\n- New: files that Rudolf OS deletes on its own, not because you deleted them (a file gone from the desktop's view of your folder some other way, or large files cleared from the Trash's store), are kept for 7 days in \"Recovered automatically\" at the bottom of the Trash, with Restore and Delete now. System health says what went there and why. Files you delete yourself go to the Trash as before.\n- New: Settings \u203a System health \u203a \"Run the self-test\" checks, without changing anything, that Linux apps and Debian's programs see your folders, Downloads, the camera, the battery limit, the compressed memory, the desktop's reserved memory, the logs and the Wi-Fi, and says what failed (with \"\u26a1 Why?\"). It is also the command rudolf-selftest.\n- Changed: a download the browser held back, or one that stopped arriving, is offered again: its notification downloads it again when clicked, and Downloads (in Oracle) has \"Download again\" beside every failed download.\n- Fixed: unfinished downloads left behind by the browser stayed in the downloads folder for ever (one from November 4 on a test computer). One that has not grown for a day is now removed, and System health says so.\n- Changed: Battery care remembers which limits this battery does not keep (some laptops keep only 60, 80 or 100 %) and marks them in the list (\"this battery keeps 80 % instead\").\n- Changed: clearer error messages. The ones you could meet (\"write failed (drive full?)\", \"rpk failed\", \"no DHCP answer on this network\" and others) now say what to do, and clicking the notification opens the Help page about it. \"Set up Linux first\" now points to Terminal, which has had the Linux tab since 1.5.6.\n- New: Task Manager's Apps lists every app and program that runs, also with no window open (Discord in the tray, Steam half-closed, Windows programs in Wine, Linux programs in Debian), with the memory each uses and \"End\": it is asked to quit, and stopped if it has not after 5 seconds.\n- New: when memory runs out and Rudolf OS closes an app with no window open, a notification says so at once (\"Discord was closed to free memory\").\n- New: starting a game while little memory is free and apps run in the background asks first: \"Discord is running in the background and uses 640 MB. Close it for the game?\"\n- New: Settings \u203a System health \u203a Memory: the memory in use, the compressed memory (Automatic, Smaller or Off, applied at the next restart), what was closed to free memory in the last 7 days, and whether apps with no window open may be closed when memory runs out.\n- Fixed: what the desktop gets from the internet at start-up no longer fails once and waits for the next turn when the Wi-Fi has no address yet. The update check runs once the computer is online (and a failed check is tried again in 5 minutes, not an hour); the Library's list is not kept empty after an offline try; downloading Flathub's app list or Blitzen while offline waits for the internet and then starts by itself; and a page Oracle could not open offline opens by itself once online.\n- Changed: the self-test also checks what Linux apps and games need: /dev/fd and /dev/stdin, stdout and stderr (Steam's interface), game controllers, the Linux apps (also in the encrypted folder), Debian with Wine, the windows on screen, and that the compressed memory has the size set in Memory.\n- New: opening an .AppImage, .bin or .run file (Linux programs that need no installing) shows what it is, with Run and \"Keep it in the Start menu\". It runs in Linux (Debian) from where it is.\n- New: Linux programs in Debian can use the camera and game controllers (also Steam Input's virtual controllers), including ones plugged in after Linux started. Cameras now belong to the desktop user's own group, which Debian's user shares.\n- New (test): System Settings \u203a Desktop engine: Standard (Chromium) or Lite (WebKit). Lite runs the desktop and its apps in WebKit, a lighter web engine that took about a third of Chromium's memory in a trial, and opens websites in Chromium's own window. The change applies when the computer restarts; if Lite does not start, Standard takes over for that start. Lite's engine (WebKitGTK) is part of Rudolf OS itself, so it works with or without Linux (Debian) and with \"Encrypt my data\"; videos in H.264 or AAC play only with Standard for now.\n- Changed: a USB stick made in Settings \u203a Storage and backup is a rescue stick for this computer. Recovery mode on it starts this computer's own Rudolf OS with safe settings (and the stick's own when this computer has none that can start); a normal start runs the stick's own Rudolf OS. The stick gets the boot files of the system it copies, downloaded when the drive's own are older (on a test laptop, a stick with older boot files could not start Recovery mode at all). Each release now carries its boot files (boot.iso) for this. Help \u203a Starting from a USB stick explains it, with the boot-menu keys and Fast Boot.\n- New: Force quit, on an app's right-click menu below Close (a window's title bar and taskbar button, a Linux or Windows program's taskbar button, the Start menu for a Linux app that runs) and in Task Manager's Apps: it ends the app with all its background processes at once, without asking it to quit, for apps that leave something running after they are closed (Steam's helpers, a tray app). For the desktop's own apps it closes all their windows at once.\n- New: Task Manager's Processes has End process tree (as in Windows XP's Task Manager): it ends a process and every process it started, and theirs, such as all of Steam's helper processes at once. Processes Rudolf OS needs are left running. End process, End process tree and End now are also on a process's right-click menu.\n- Changed: a selected or highlighted item in desktop windows (Settings' pages, File Explorer's files and folders, Task Manager's processes, the network list, menus and others) looks as in the Start menu: the accent colour with white writing, or black writing on a light accent colour. Before, some lists drew a light bar under the dark theme's light writing, and the text could not be read. Hovering over an item tints it with the accent colour and keeps the writing as it was.\n- Fixed: coming back to a computer whose screen had locked could show the lock screen twice (on an HP desktop), and the screen saver or the lock could start again just after waking or unlocking. Each idle check now waits at most 4 seconds for the system, one runs at a time, and one that saw you come back while it waited does nothing; waking from sleep counts as you being back; nothing locks by itself for a minute after unlocking (Lock and Windows+L still do). Updates and other background work also wait while the screen is locked, as they were meant to.\n- Changed: on the Lite desktop the screen saver draws at most 15 frames a second at a lower resolution, leaving the processor to your apps.\n- Fixed: \"Sign in with Google\" on websites (claude.ai and others) stopped on a blank accounts.google.com window in the browser window (and could in Oracle's real tabs). Chromium's FedCM sign-in is turned off there, so Google uses its usual sign-in window, as in Firefox.\n- Changed: with the Lite desktop, the notice when Oracle opens a website in the browser window says why (every website opens there with Lite), instead of speaking of signing in.\n- Changed: Task Manager is in the desktop's right-click menu, and Ctrl+Alt+Del opens it too (as Ctrl+Shift+Esc does), also from a game or an app in front: the desktop comes forward with it.\n- Changed: Task Manager opened with Ctrl+Alt+Del or Ctrl+Shift+Esc comes up above every window and stays there (Always on top), and while it is open the desktop's processes get the processor and the disk first, so a program that is using up the computer can still be found and ended. Closing it puts everything back. Opened from the menus it is an ordinary window.\n- Fixed: a window opened later no longer comes up over the windows set to Always on top.\n- Fixed: with the Lite desktop engine, dropdown menus (for example in System Settings) looked blank in the dark theme: WebKit drew them white with light text. They now look as they do with Standard.\n- Fixed: with the Lite desktop engine, a website opened from Oracle could open its browser window behind the desktop, so nothing seemed to happen; Oracle's \"Show it again\" brought it up.\n- Fixed: with the Lite desktop engine, the desktop restarted every few seconds with \"Real browser tabs are ready\": it offered Chromium's one-time restart into real tabs, which Lite does not use. Lite no longer offers it.\n- Fixed: the Lite desktop engine (WebKit) closed as it started on real computers: a graphics library it loads while running (OpenGL ES, libGLESv2) was missing from Rudolf OS. The self-test now checks for it, and the release check runs Lite from the system itself, so a missing library is caught before a release.\n- Fixed: a desktop engine that cannot start no longer leaves the computer stuck. Lite (WebKit) that closes 3 times in a row as it starts, or that has not reached the sign-in screen when the computer is switched off, is turned off for good: Standard is used from the next moment on, and System Settings \u203a Desktop engine says when and why. Choosing Lite again tries it once more. (On a test laptop, Lite closed as it started every few seconds and the computer never reached the sign-in screen.) The same goes for Oracle's real tabs (the shell): if it keeps closing, or the desktop asks for it again and again, the desktop page is used by itself until the next start.\n- Fixed: a system update that cannot start because part of it is missing now goes back to the previous system by itself, instead of stopping at \"this drive has boot files only\".\n- Changed: Recovery mode starts this computer's installed system with safe settings (the standard desktop engine, no shell, safe graphics) and changes nothing. Before, it started the drive's own system, which most installed computers do not have (\"this drive has boot files only\"), or a USB stick's older system with this computer's newer files. Recovery mode works this way with boot files from 1.5.7 on (Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a boot files).\n- Fixed: a Rudolf OS started in Recovery mode, or running from a USB stick or a disc on a computer where Rudolf OS is installed, no longer updates the installed system (Settings says why). On a test laptop, a rescue stick put an update in place that needed the stick, and the laptop could not start without it.\n- Fixed: an update never counts on a USB stick or disc staying plugged in: a part it shares with one is copied onto the computer's own drive, and an update is only made ready to start once every part of it is there.\n- Under the hood: a review of what rudolfd keeps in memory between requests (each request is a process of its own, so a value kept in one was lost in the next, as with Downloads in 1.5.6): every such value is now listed with why it is safe, and a test fails when a new one is added without that review.\n- Under the hood: Blitzen can read what this computer recorded (the health log, the service log, the desktop's errors, the last task's log), with lines that carry a password, token or key left out, and Rudolf OS's own documents (its plans, this list of changes and the Help pages), searched on this computer. Nothing is sent anywhere; the diagnostics that use them come next.\n\n## 1.5.6 (2026-11-10)\nSummary: your files on the disk (docs/plan-1.5.6.md)\n- Under the hood: Rudolf OS can keep each account's files as real files on the disk, encrypted with that account's own key and unreadable while it is signed out.\n- New (beta): accounts keep their files on the disk. Saving writes only the files that changed, large files have no size limit, and Linux apps see the same Documents, Pictures, Music and Videos.\n- New (beta): an account from before 1.5.6 moves its files to the disk once, in the background after signing in. Everything is read back and compared before the switch; a move that does not arrive whole changes nothing and is tried again at the next sign-in. The old copy is kept for 7 days, so going back to 1.5.5 still finds your files.\n- New (beta): with your files on the disk, the Trash still gives back large files (they wait in your folder's own Trash until it is emptied), backups carry your home, a backup restored on another computer fills your folder there, and administrators can open, change and close other accounts' files as before.\n- Changed: Power and battery is in Settings \u203a System health, below the summary, instead of System Settings. The battery on the taskbar, User Settings' link and the Settings search go there.\n- Changed: Battery care stops charging at 70 %, 80 %, 90 % or 100 % (it was 60 %, 80 % or 100 %). A limit of 60 % set before is kept as 70 %, the nearest choice, not raised to 100 %. If the battery reports a different limit than the one chosen (some batteries take only certain limits), Power and battery says so.\n- Fixed: Bluetooth's page could fail when another Settings page was opened before it had loaded, and the encryption page when Rudolf OS gave no list of moved folders.\n- Fixed: Oracle's Downloads page still pointed at C:\\DOWNLOADS, which 1.5.5 replaced with each account's own Downloads: \"Open\" gave 404 Not found and \"Open Downloads folder\" gave Folder not found. Both go to your Downloads now, and a file kept on the disk opened from Oracle opens the way File Explorer opens it. Small files Oracle downloads itself go straight into your Downloads.\n- Changed: opening a Debian package (.deb, such as Discord's) says Rudolf OS does not install packages from a file yet, and offers the App Store, where most such apps (Discord among them) are.\n- Changed: with your files on the disk, Linux apps' Downloads is your own Downloads, like Documents and Pictures. Nothing is copied back and forth any more. Copies the earlier 1.5.6 beta had made of your downloads in the Downloads folder shared by Linux apps are removed at sign-in (only exact copies), and files Linux apps saved there move into your Downloads when the computer has one account. Website downloads still arrive in a separate folder first, where they are checked. (Fixed in the second beta: with \"Encrypt my data\" on, that separate folder was not set up, because the encrypted folder is still locked at start-up, so website downloads could not be saved. Fixed again in the third beta: rudolfd forgot the separate folder after each request, so with your Downloads shared with Linux apps it looked for new downloads in the wrong place.)\n- Fixed: waking the computer from sleep restarted the browser (closing every window), because the desktop's \"I'm alive\" signal was as old as the sleep. The desktop now gets 90 seconds after waking to answer.\n- Fixed: with your files on the disk, the old two-way copying between your Downloads and Linux apps' Downloads could still run when your Downloads could not be shared with Linux apps directly, and it deleted files it thought an app had deleted (on the Vivobook, DISCORD.DEB and an installer). It never runs for files on the disk now.\n- Fixed: a Linux app put on the desktop (Discord from the App Store, say) showed the penguin instead of its own icon. It now shows the app's icon, as the Start menu and the taskbar do. (An app installed from a Linux package, such as Discord's .deb, showed its icon at full size, over the other icons; it is icon-sized now.)\n- Fixed: no app could use the camera on an installed Rudolf OS (the Vivobook's built-in webcam, a USB webcam): the camera's device belonged to the system only. Cameras now belong to the video group the desktop is in.\n- Fixed: programs in Linux (Debian), Wine among them, did not see your Documents, Pictures, Music, Videos and Downloads as they are since 1.5.6, and with \"Encrypt my data\" on they could not open Downloads at all. They now see the same folders as the rest of Rudolf OS, also after switching accounts.\n- Fixed (third 1.5.6 beta): the installation wizard said a package in Downloads was \"not a package in your folders\": it gave the file's place in your account's folder, which Linux does not use. rudolfd now turns it into the name Linux sees.\n- New: installing apps from files. Opening a Linux package (.deb, such as Discord's) shows what it is and installs it into Linux (Debian) with what it needs, with a progress bar; its app is then in the Start menu, and the wizard offers to open it or put it on the desktop. An iPhone or iPad app (.ipa) is explained: it cannot run outside Apple's devices. AppImages, .bin installers and Android apps follow.\n- Changed: the Command Prompt and the Linux terminal are one app, Terminal, with the Command Prompt's icon. Each is a tab: \uff0b opens either. Terminal opens on Linux when Linux is set up, otherwise on the Command Prompt (also in a web browser). Lua programs, .BAT files and rpk open a Command Prompt tab. Its desktop icon keeps its place.\n- Changed: the Developer Console is out of the Start menu unless Settings \u203a Apps \u203a \"Show developer tools in the Start menu\" is on. It is always in Task Manager and in search.\n- Changed: installing a Linux package also adds what it recommends once the package itself is in (Steam's package adds Valve's own source, whose 32-bit libraries can only be found then).\n- New: Windows programs (.exe, .msi) open in the installation wizard: an installer runs with Wine and the programs it installs are offered (Open, Put on the desktop) as they appear; another program runs, or can be installed anyway. Without Wine (or Linux), the wizard sets them up first.\n- Fixed: the Install button on Flathub's website (in Oracle) gave \"This site can't be reached\". Its link, and a downloaded .flatpakref file, now open that app's page in the App Store.\n- Fixed: /dev/fd, /dev/stdin, /dev/stdout and /dev/stderr were missing. Steam (from the App Store) failed to start its interface because of it, and scripts using them failed too.\n- Fixed: Alt+F4 with the desktop in front closed the desktop's whole window, back to the sign-in screen. It now closes the window in front, as everywhere else.\n- Changed: when memory runs out and no web page can be closed, an app with no window open (Discord in the tray, say) is closed instead, never the app or game in front. System health says which.\n- Changed: the compressed memory (zram) is as large as the computer's memory instead of half of it, so it can hold twice as much (on the Vivobook, Steam, Discord and a game filled the old one).\n- Changed: while an app or game is in front and memory ran short, the desktop gets 5 minutes instead of 2 to answer before its browser is restarted (closing every window).\n- Fixed: the Weather widget said \"Weather needs an internet connection\" for 15 minutes after start-up when the desktop was ready before the Wi-Fi. It now tries again after 20 seconds, then 1, 2 and 5 minutes, and at once when the computer is online.\n- Fixed: a prompt that needs you (the development link's \"Allow this command?\", \"Allow a remote connection?\") waited behind a full-screen game or app where it could not be reached. It now comes to the front, and the app comes back once you answer.\n- Fixed: an installer's window could stay hidden behind the desktop (Starport's installer in Wine: its wizard belongs to another window). A window's dialogs now stay above it with the keyboard, and a dialog whose owner is hidden is shown as a window of its own. The installation wizard has \"Show the installer\" and \"Stop the installer\".\n- Fixed: Chromium's own shortcuts could close, reload or cover the desktop: F5 and Ctrl+R reloaded it (every window lost), Ctrl+H, Ctrl+J, F1 and others put Chromium's pages over it, Shift+Esc opened Chromium's task manager. None of them acts on the desktop now; the desktop's own shortcuts still work. In Oracle's tabs Chromium's pages never open from a website, and Ctrl+Shift+R and Shift+F5 reload the tab.\n- New: File Explorer sorts in every view (Sort by Name, Date modified, Type or Size, with the direction next to it), and remembers each folder's order for your account. Downloads opens newest first. View \u203a \"Folders first\" can be switched off, and \"Use this order for all folders\" sets your default.\n- New: favourite files. \"Add to favourites\" works for files too; they are listed in the sidebar below your favourite folders and open in their app. A favourite follows its file when you rename or move it in File Explorer, and stays greyed when the file is gone, with \"Remove from favourites\".\n- New: Settings \u203a Storage and backup says what fills the computer's disk, largest first: your files, other accounts' files, Downloads, the Trash, Linux apps, Steam's games, Linux (Debian), Emu's games, Blitzen's model, the system and everything else, each with what you can do about it (Open, Manage, Open Steam). \"The disk is almost full\" opens it.\n- New: the Trash shows its size (on the desktop and in its window, large files at their size on the disk), and Settings \u203a Storage and backup can delete items for good once they are older than 1, 14, 30 or 60 days (Never by default; each account its own), with one notification a day at most.\n- New: File Explorer's \"Everywhere\" search looks in the folder you are in first, then the rest of C:, then plugged-in drives (by name, at most 3 folder levels and 2,000 files each), with filters for the type (documents, pictures, music, videos, archives, programs) and when it changed. Results show where each one is and appear while it looks; it can be stopped, and shows 1,000 at a time with \"Show more\".\n- New: Rename\u2026 with several files selected renames them together: a pattern (\"HOLIDAY ###\" gives HOLIDAY 001, 002\u2026, each keeping its extension) or find and replace, with every new name shown first. Names that would clash are shown in red and nothing changes until they are fixed; one Undo puts all the names back.\n- New: Find duplicates (a folder's menu in File Explorer, and Settings \u203a Storage and backup) finds identical files (the same size and SHA-256, also for files kept on the disk), suggests keeping the newest of each, and moves the others to the Trash only when you ask.\n- Fixed: most accounts' files did not move to the disk on 1.5.6 (\"Your files stay where they were\" at every sign-in): account ids with capital letters were refused. Found by the new QEMU test of the move (t156).\n- Fixed: the move of an account's files to the disk stopped at its large downloads when the account's id had capital letters (their downloads folder is in lower case), and a move that did not finish left the account's folder unlocked after sign-out. Found by QEMU t156.\n- Fixed: when Linux (Debian) was started after signing in, the account's folders stayed in use after sign-out, so the names of its folders and files could still be listed (their contents could not be read). Found by QEMU t156.\n- Fixed: restoring a backup after the account's folder on the disk was removed did not bring the files back: the large files, which backups do not hold, stopped the move. They are now left out.\n- Fixed: closing Steam or Discord could leave the screen white for over a minute and then go back to the sign-in screen: the desktop's memory had been pushed into the compressed swap behind them. The desktop now keeps its memory (a memory group the system frees last), a reserve of free memory is kept for the graphics driver, and the watchdog waits up to 5 minutes while the desktop is still reading its memory back instead of restarting it after 1 minute. And closing a game no longer restarts the desktop the moment it comes back: behind a game it is not judged at all, and back in front it has a minute to answer.\n- Fixed: the power button signed you out before asking what to do, so Cancel left you at the sign-in screen. It now asks first: Cancel goes back to the desktop as it was, Shut down and Restart sign out once chosen, and Sleep keeps you signed in behind the lock screen. The same for Start \u203a Shut down.\n- Fixed: the Wi-Fi dropped every open connection whenever it moved between the router's 2.4 and 5 GHz radios. A short drop now keeps the network address (and renews it on the same network); the Wi-Fi looks for a better radio only when the signal is weak; Realtek rtw88 Wi-Fi chips (RTL8821CE and others) no longer use PCIe link power saving, a known cause of drops. When a change of radio fails and the Wi-Fi sets the network aside (on the Vivobook for 5.5 minutes), Rudolf OS takes it back within about 25 seconds. A radio of the router that does not answer is left out for the rest of the session (on the Vivobook the 2.4 GHz one, tried twice in 25 minutes, which disconnected a game), and allowed again if the Wi-Fi stays off the network for more than 10 seconds.\n- Fixed: Linux apps' icons (Discord, Steam) could stay a penguin for the whole session after one failed request; the icon is now asked for again. With Encrypt my data, Linux apps also went missing from the desktop and the Start menu after a restart (their list was asked for before sign-in opened the encrypted folder): the list is now kept and asked for again after sign-in, and their pictures drawn at once. The same for Debian's apps and Windows programs (Starport GE).\n- New: Settings \u203a Display Properties: 16 accent colours (8 before), and the accent colour on the taskbar and on the desktop icons, each with its own tick box and a choice of Normal, Negative or Complementary. The 16 colours stay on one row, a little smaller when the window needs it.\n- New: Settings \u203a Display Properties \u203a Taskbar \u203a Style: Standard, Wireframe (outlines on a see-through bar), Monochrome (greys), Candy (glossy, round buttons) or Retro (raised and sunken edges on silver); each in the accent colour when that is ticked for the taskbar.\n- Changed: the Start button and the \"Rudolf OS 1.5.6\" button beside it are one button: the logo with the name and the whole version, which opens the Start menu (Settings \u203a About stays in Settings and the Start menu). On a taskbar at the left or the right the version is under the logo.\n- New: every app in the Start menu has the same right-click menu: Open, Pin to taskbar, Add to (or Remove from) desktop and Properties, with Uninstall\u2026 for Linux apps from Flathub. Before, Linux apps and Windows programs had none and Emu only Open. Properties shows the app's details: what kind of app it is, its address (which File Explorer opens, to look at), its size on disk, the file types it uses, its program file, version, publisher, ID, and its pinned and desktop state.\n\n## 1.5.5 (2026-11-08)\nSummary: each account has its own Downloads, choose the app shortcuts on your desktop, and the Trash's tooltip says what is in it\n- Changed: each account has its own Downloads again (C:\\USERS\\<name>\\DOWNLOADS), encrypted with its home folder; large downloads are kept on the disk in the account's own folder. From 1.2.7 to 1.5.4 every account shared C:\\DOWNLOADS. At the first start of 1.5.5 that folder goes to its owner when the computer has one account; with several, it stays readable by all as C:\\SHARED DOWNLOADS (BEFORE 1.5.5) for an administrator to sort out. Nothing is deleted.\n- Changed: a download that finishes while nobody is signed in waits, and goes to whoever signs in next. The guest's downloads are deleted when the guest signs out.\n- New: app shortcuts can be taken off the desktop: right-click \u203a Remove from desktop, the Delete key, dropping the icon on the Trash, or File Explorer's Desktop folder. The app stays in the Start menu.\n- New: right-click an app in the Start menu \u203a Add to desktop (or Remove from desktop). Apps without a desktop icon of their own get one. Before, Add to desktop only said \"Already on the desktop\".\n- New: Desktop icons\u2026 (in the desktop's right-click menu and in File Explorer's Desktop folder) lists every app with a tick for those on the desktop, and Restore the standard icons. Each account keeps its own choice.\n- New: File Explorer \u203a right-click \u203a Copy to desktop.\n- Changed: My Computer and Programs cannot be dropped on the Trash (hide them from their menu instead). Desktop icons\u2026 lists the desktop's own icons by name, My Computer among them, and the notification after removing an icon puts it back with a click.\n- Fixed: the Trash's tooltip kept saying \"The Trash is empty\" after something was put in it.\n- Fixed: the built-in microphones of AMD laptops (1.5.4) were found but not offered to apps: their sound card did not open with ALSA's use case files, so it is opened with the sound server's own settings instead (found on the ASUS Vivobook Go).\n- Fixed: after a restart, Downloads was only looked after once something was downloaded, so downloads from before 1.5.5 did not move into your own Downloads, and lost entries were not found again (found on the ASUS Vivobook Go).\n- Fixed: some downloads (for example Discord's and Steam's .deb files) stopped with \"the site may offer it over an insecure connection\": the browser was holding them for a confirmation it cannot ask for. It no longer does (Rudolf OS checks every file itself), and the message says what happened.\n- Fixed: Downloads no longer lists downloads as \"Downloading\u2026\" for ever (from before a restart, or cancelled by the browser), and a saved file is no longer listed with another download's address.\n- Fixed: a download whose entry in Downloads was lost (for example one that finished while nobody was signed in) is listed again by itself, after the antivirus has checked it.\n\n## 1.5.4 (2026-11-08)\nSummary: Bluetooth on computers with an Intel, MediaTek, Qualcomm or Realtek chip, sound on laptops with an Intel sound processor or speaker amplifiers, the built-in microphones of AMD laptops, Windows games through Steam's Proton, and Thunderbolt and USB4 docks and displays\n- New: Bluetooth works with Intel chips (the Bluetooth half of Intel Wi-Fi cards: 8260, 8265, 9260, 9560, AX200, AX201, AX203, AX210, AX211, AX411, BE200, BE201 and newer), MediaTek MT7921, MT7922 and MT7925, and Qualcomm chips on USB, as well as Realtek (1.5.1). Not yet tried on a computer with these chips: if Bluetooth does not appear, Settings \u203a Devices \u203a Not supported yet and Report a problem say what the computer has.\n- New: Bluetooth built into the board on a serial line (UART), found in inexpensive laptops and tablets (Realtek RTL8723BS and RTL8723CS, Broadcom, Intel, Qualcomm).\n- New: the built-in microphones of AMD laptops whose microphones are wired to the AMD audio processor (ACP): Ryzen 4000 and 5000 (Renoir, Cezanne), 6000 and 7x20/7x30 (Rembrandt, Mendocino: the ASUS Vivobook Go among them), and 7040/8040 (Phoenix, Hawk Point), where the laptop is known to the kernel or its firmware says so.\n- Changed: Settings \u203a Sound offers only sound cards that play as outputs, and lists the microphones it found (\"Built-in digital microphones (AMD audio processor)\").\n- New: sound on laptops whose speakers and microphones go through Intel's sound processor (SOF): 11th to 13th generation Core (Tiger Lake, Alder Lake, Raptor Lake; Elkhart Lake) and Core Ultra (Meteor Lake, Arrow Lake, Lunar Lake, Panther Lake), including SoundWire laptops. Settings \u203a Sound names it (\"Intel sound processor (SOF)\"). Not yet tried on such a laptop.\n- New: the speaker amplifiers of many ASUS, Lenovo, HP and Dell laptops (Cirrus CS35L41, CS35L54 and CS35L56; TI TAS2563 and TAS2781), without which the speakers stay silent or very quiet while headphones work.\n- New: a sound card that appears after start-up (Intel's sound processor once its firmware runs, a USB headset or speaker) is ready to use by itself, and one that is unplugged goes away from the list. Sound processors and USB sound cards are set up with ALSA's use case files, as on other systems; the HD Audio of most computers (the Vivobook's among them) is set up as before.\n- New: Windows games through Steam's Proton. Installing Steam from App Store \u203a Games also installs the 32-bit graphics drivers Proton needs (a Steam installed earlier gets them the next time it is opened), and Rudolf OS gives games what Proton expects: NT synchronization (ntsync), more memory maps and more open files.\n- New: Steam's card in App Store \u203a Games says whether this computer's graphics have the Vulkan that Proton needs, and why not when they do not (drawing with the processor, Intel graphics before Broadwell, older NVIDIA cards, old Radeon cards, virtual machines); that games whose anti-cheat allows Linux work and others (Valorant, Fortnite, Call of Duty) do not, with cloud gaming for some of those; and how much space is free for games.\n- New: Steam can keep a game library on a USB drive or a second disk (Steam \u203a Settings \u203a Storage).\n- New: game controllers work in Steam (Steam Input) and in Linux games: Xbox, PlayStation 4 and 5, Switch and Steam controllers, by USB and by Bluetooth. Keyboards and mice stay out of apps' reach.\n- Fixed: PlayStation 4 and 5 controllers ran as plain HID devices (no light bar, touchpad, motion or rumble): their driver was left out of the system without a word. It is now checked when Rudolf OS is built.\n- Changed: notifications wait in the notification centre while a Linux app's window is full screen (a Steam game, Big Picture, a Windows game in Wine), as for full-screen pages.\n- New: Thunderbolt and USB4 docks, displays and devices that use PCIe over the cable (external drives and cards), and the Aquantia network chip of many docks. Where the computer protects its memory from devices on the cable (DMA protection), they are allowed by themselves; otherwise a new device asks once, an administrator allows it, and it is allowed by itself from then on. Settings \u203a Devices lists them, with Forget.\n- New: USB-C ports are managed by Rudolf OS (UCSI), and displays work through USB-C (DisplayPort alternate mode) where the port supports it.\n- New (preview): the first part of built-in cameras on newer Intel laptops (Intel's camera processor IPU6 and IPU7, the camera's power and light, and the common sensors). Apps cannot use these cameras yet: that needs a later update. Until then Settings \u203a Devices \u203a Not supported yet lists the camera. USB cameras work as before.\n- New: Settings \u203a Security \u203a Health log notes each package installed, updated or removed from the Rudolf OS Library, and each one refused (for example one signed by the Library that tries to bring a program).\n\n## 1.5.3 (2026-11-07)\nSummary: USB Wi-Fi adapters with Ralink and MediaTek RT2800 chips, a choice of Wi-Fi adapter, and a list of the devices that have no driver yet\n- New: USB Wi-Fi adapters with Ralink and MediaTek RT2800 chips work (RT2770, RT2870, RT3070, RT3071, RT3072, RT3370, RT3572, RT3573, RT5370, RT5372, RT5572: most inexpensive Wi-Fi dongles), and the same chips as cards inside older laptops (RT2860, RT3090, RT3290, RT5390 and others).\n- New: Settings \u203a Network \u203a Wi-Fi: with a USB Wi-Fi adapter next to the built-in one, choose which one to use. Rudolf OS remembers it, uses it whenever it is plugged in, and goes back to the built-in Wi-Fi when it is unplugged.\n- Fixed: the RT2800 driver was asked for but left out of the system without a word. Every Wi-Fi driver is now checked when Rudolf OS is built, so none can go missing again.\n- New: Settings \u203a Devices \u203a Not supported yet: the parts of this computer that Rudolf OS has no driver for yet, built in or plugged in (for example a Wi-Fi card, a sound processor, a camera or a fingerprint reader), or that every device has one. Report a problem includes the list, and the health log notes it the first time Rudolf OS starts on a computer.\n- New: Settings \u203a Devices shows each USB device's speed and the port it is in (\"USB 2.0, 480 Mbit/s, in a USB 3 port (10 Gbit/s)\"). A USB device that does not say what it is (most Wi-Fi dongles) is named by what it calls itself.\n- Fixed: Make a Rudolf OS USB stick could report a stick as ready that would not start (\"this drive has boot files only\"): made from an installed Rudolf OS, the stick needs that system copied onto it, and once it was not. The stick is now checked before it is called ready: its system must be complete and read back the same as this computer's, or it says what went wrong.\n- Fixed: Install here from a Rudolf OS USB stick made by Make a Rudolf OS USB stick (or from an installed Rudolf OS) installed only the boot files, and the computer stopped at \"this drive has boot files only\". It now copies the running system too, and checks it before saying Rudolf OS is installed.\n- Fixed: Shut down, Restart and Sleep no longer cut off a job that would be left half done: making a USB stick, installing, writing boot files, setting up a drive for your files, a system update or encrypting your files. Rudolf OS says what it is still doing; \"Restart anyway\" (and the like) is there if you really mean it. A restart in the middle of Make a Rudolf OS USB stick had twice left a stick without its system.\n\n## 1.5.2 (2026-11-06)\nSummary: the Rudolf OS Library: Quake and Emu download when you first need them, and every update is smaller; Game Boy games in Emu's library; Windows games get the keyboard and full screen\n- New: App Store \u203a Rudolf OS Library, a catalog of apps made for Rudolf OS that not every computer needs. Each shows its size, description and licence, with Get, Open, Update and Remove. The Library has its own signing key: it can add apps, but it can never change Rudolf OS itself.\n- Changed: Quake's free game data and Emu's Nintendo DS player moved into the Library. They download (21 MB and 0.4 MB) the first time you ask for them, so every desktop update is about 12 MB smaller. SNES games play without a download, and Quake data you already have on drive C: stays.\n- Changed: a computer that already played DS games gets Emu's DS player back by itself after the update, once.\n- New: Emu's \"Add games\" takes Game Boy, Game Boy Color and Game Boy Advance games (.gb, .gbc, .gba, or in a ZIP file). They go to Games \u203a GB, show under Game Boy / GBA in Emu's library, and play in the Game Boy player.\n- Fixed: a ZIP file whose game had a space in its name (most downloaded games) was skipped as \"no SNES or DS game inside\".\n- Fixed: Windows games (Wine) got only some keys: the arrow keys worked, letters did not. A game in front now gets the keyboard the way Windows games expect (and gets it back when you click it after using the taskbar).\n- Changed: a game that goes full screen now covers the whole screen, taskbar too, as Roblox does: whether it asks for full screen or makes its borderless window the size of the screen (as Windows games in Wine do, Starport GE among them). Switching the game to windowed brings the taskbar back. Super+Esc goes to the desktop, Super+Tab to the next window.\n\n## 1.5.1 (2026-11-03)\nSummary: Bluetooth (controllers, keyboards, mice, headphones), and Xbox Cloud Gaming and GeForce NOW in the Start menu\n- New: Bluetooth (Settings \u203a Bluetooth), on computers with a Realtek Bluetooth chip or a USB Bluetooth adapter with one (Intel and MediaTek chips follow in an update). Pair controllers (Xbox, PlayStation, Switch Pro), keyboards, mice and headphones: put the device in pairing mode, choose Search for devices, then Pair. No code to type. Paired devices reconnect by themselves; headphones become the sound output when they connect. Notifications say when a device connects, goes away, or its battery runs low.\n- New: Xbox Cloud Gaming and GeForce NOW in the Start menu and the App Store's Games tab. They open in Oracle; controllers work in them.\n- New: game mode. While a game or video is full screen, notifications wait in the notification centre instead of popping up, and one message afterwards says how many came.\n- Fixed: playing with only a controller did not count as using the computer, so the screen could lock or the computer sleep in the middle of a game.\n- New: controllers are listed in Settings \u203a Devices.\n- Fixed: Bluetooth pairings were not kept: a device showed as paired, but after a restart it had to be paired again, and a phone asking to compare codes never got an answer. A phone's code now appears on screen to confirm (Yes / No), a device that asks to pair is asked about, a keyboard that needs a code shows it, and Settings says so when a pairing did not finish.\n- Fixed: phones and other computers could not find this computer over Bluetooth. Settings \u203a Bluetooth has Let this computer be found (for a few minutes), and Search for devices makes it visible too.\n- Fixed: Wi-Fi dropped every few minutes on laptops with a Realtek Wi-Fi chip (such as the RTL8821CE) when the router has both a 5 GHz and a 2.4 GHz radio. The chip's deep power saving is now off.\n\n## 1.5.0 (2026-11-02)\nSummary: security: a firewall, encrypted data, administrators can open account files, a health log, and remote access from any browser\n- New: a firewall. Other computers can no longer connect to programs on this one (Linux and Windows programs included); everything this computer reaches out to works as before. Security Settings shows how many connections it blocked. The old \"Firewall\" setting for web pages is now called Website blocking; it works as before.\n- New: Encrypt my data (Security Settings). Debian, Linux and Windows programs, Linux apps and files kept in Downloads move into a folder the system encrypts. Signing in with your password unlocks it; Guest does not. A recovery key, shown once, opens it if every password is forgotten.\n- New: administrators can open the other accounts' files (in C:\\USERS) and reset an account's password without its files being lost. It is on for every account and can be turned off per account (User Settings \u203a Files and administrators). Each account's welcome file says who can open its files.\n- New: System health (Settings) records what happened on this computer: start-ups and how long they took, crashes, pages closed to free memory, updates that fell back, failed sign-ins, firewall blocks and remote sessions. It stays on this computer; Report a problem includes its latest events after you read the report.\n- New: Remote access (Settings). Use this computer from a phone, tablet or another computer on the same network, in any web browser, with nothing to install. A pairing code is asked once per browser, then an account and its password. Someone at the computer allows each connection, unless an administrator lets that account connect unattended. A red bar shows who is connected, with End.\n\n## 1.4.9 (2026-11-02)\nSummary: Windows programs in a click: install Wine from Settings, and installed programs appear in the Start menu\n- New: Install Wine from Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Linux, or straight from a Windows program that cannot open yet. No Terminal commands are needed: it downloads about 1 GB and says when it is done.\n- New: Windows programs installed with Wine (games and their launchers) appear in the Start menu by themselves, with their own icons.\n- Fixed: programs that expect to start in their own folder (game launchers and updaters) are started there.\n- Fixed: Windows programs whose paths have backslashes (all of them) started with a wrong path from Wine's own menu entries.\n\n## 1.4.8 (2026-11-02)\nSummary: Windows programs open with Wine, and Linux apps appear in the Start menu straight away\n- New: with Wine installed in Debian (Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Linux), Windows programs (.exe, .msi, .bat) open with it: double-click the file, or use Open with. Large downloads kept on the disk open the same way. Without Wine, opening a Windows program says how to get it.\n- Fixed: a Linux app installed outside the Linux Terminal only appeared in the Start menu after signing in again or opening the Terminal. The Start menu now checks for new Linux apps each time it opens.\n- Fixed: Linux apps that only open files (such as Wine's program loader) were never offered in Open with.\n- Changed: the L in Rudolf now stands for Linux: Rudolf OS is the Runtime Driver Open Linux Framework (it was Lua). The sign-in screen, About Rudolf OS, VER and the Prompt say so, and so does C:\\README.TXT, also on computers set up by an older version.\n- Fixed: when a page filled the memory faster than Rudolf OS could move it to the compressed swap, the desktop could stall for up to half a minute before the page was closed. The page is now closed as soon as the system reports it is stalling with little memory left.\n\n## 1.4.7 (2026-11-01)\nSummary: a page that uses up the memory no longer freezes the desktop: Rudolf OS closes that page and says why\n- New: compressed swap in memory. Rudolf OS keeps memory it is not using right now compressed in memory, so a computer holds about half as much again before it runs out (a 3.4 GB laptop holds about 5 GB). Task Manager shows how much is stored this way.\n- New: when memory runs out anyway, Rudolf OS closes the web page using the most memory, and only that page. Oracle shows \"This page was closed because it used too much memory\" in its tab, with a Reload button, and a notification names the page. Before, the whole desktop could stop responding for a minute and then start again with every window and tab closed.\n- Changed: if memory runs out, the desktop and what draws it are the last things the system closes.\n- Fixed: Display Properties' Font list offered fonts Rudolf OS does not have. Tahoma, Verdana, Trebuchet MS, Georgia, Comic Sans MS and Lucida Console all looked the same. Every choice is now a real font, named with the font it stands in for (\"Gelasio (like Georgia)\"), and each one looks different. Three of them (Gelasio, Fira Sans, Comic Neue) are new in Rudolf OS. A font chosen in an older version keeps the look it had.\n- Fixed: Weather and its desktop widget showed Berlin until a city was searched for, and the widget never changed afterwards. They now show the city of your time zone (System Settings), or ask for your city when the time zone does not say. The widget follows the city chosen in Weather at once, and updates every 15 minutes; clicking it opens Weather.\n- New: Weather shows \u00b0F and mph for places in the United States, with a \u00b0C/\u00b0F switch, and a city name with several matches (Springfield) lists them to choose from.\n- Fixed: with \"Use it for games and the desktop\" (Display Properties \u203a Graphics), playing a large game in Oracle (Friday Night Funkin' on Newgrounds) could freeze a laptop with little memory and bring back the sign-in screen.\n\n## 1.4.6 (2026-11-01)\nSummary: Notey in SpreadsheetPro and PresentPro as in WordPro, with help for each app; dark pages in Oracle, and its menu easier to read\n- New: Notey in SpreadsheetPro and PresentPro is the same Notey as in WordPro. He moves in held poses, a few frames a second (not a constant smooth bob), with his expressions, tricks and reactions. He can be dragged anywhere, and falls asleep when nothing happens. Hiding him in one app hides him in all three.\n- New: in SpreadsheetPro, Notey checks a spreadsheet and fixes what he finds:\n  - errors, explained, or shown as a blank instead;\n  - numbers stored as text;\n  - a formula unlike the ones around it;\n  - a total that misses the last rows;\n  - columns too narrow for their numbers;\n  - long tables whose headings scroll away.\n  He also explains the formula in any cell in words, finds the function you describe (\"how do I work out an average?\"), offers a total under a column of numbers or a chart for a table, and reminds you to save.\n- New: in PresentPro, Notey checks a presentation:\n  - slides without a title;\n  - slides with too much text (he can split them in two);\n  - text too small or too faint to read;\n  - stretched pictures;\n  - empty slides;\n  - too many fonts;\n  - missing speaker notes.\n  He offers a theme and transitions as the deck grows, opens the themes, and rehearses the talk.\n- New: dark pages in Oracle (\u2630 \u203a Dark pages). Oracle's bar, tabs and menu turn dark, and so do the pages. Pages that are dark already are left as they are; pictures and videos keep their colours.\n- Fixed: Oracle's \u2630 menu had light grey text on a white background when the desktop used the dark theme.\n\n## 1.4.5 (2026-11-01)\nSummary: two new apps with WordPro: SpreadsheetPro, in the place of Excel, and PresentPro, in the place of PowerPoint; Shut down and Restart finish within seconds\n- New: SpreadsheetPro replaces the small Spreadsheet app. It has WordPro's menus and toolbar, 702 columns by 100,000 rows, several sheets, 167 functions (with a description as you type each one), number formats, fonts, colours, borders, merged cells, conditional formatting with colour scales and data bars, sorting, filters, data validation, find and replace, comments, named ranges, charts (column, bar, line, area, pie, doughnut and scatter) that follow the data, the fill handle for series, frozen panes, printing and PDF with page setup, and templates (a budget, an invoice, a timetable, a grade book, a loan calculator). It opens and saves Excel .xlsx files, CSV files, and the old Spreadsheet's files.\n- New: PresentPro replaces the Presentations app. Slides with eight layouts, twelve themes and a master (footer, date, slide numbers, logo); text, shapes, pictures (cropped, with a border and brightness), tables, charts and icons that can be moved, resized, rotated, aligned, grouped and layered, with guides that snap; speaker notes; sections and the slide sorter; transitions and animations. Presenting works with the keyboard and with a presenter remote, has a presenter view (P) with the notes, the next slide and the time, a pen and a laser pointer, black and white screens, rehearsed timings and a show that loops. It opens and saves PowerPoint .pptx files, makes PDFs of the slides, handouts and notes pages, pictures of the slides, and has templates (a talk, a lesson, a report, a photo album, a pitch). The old Presentations files open as before.\n- New: the three apps work together. A range copied in SpreadsheetPro pastes as a table in WordPro and PresentPro, and a chart copies as a picture or with its data. WordPro's Insert menu has Spreadsheet table and Chart, and File \u203a Send to PresentPro makes slides from a document's headings; PresentPro's File \u203a Send to WordPro makes a handout. While there are unsaved changes, SpreadsheetPro and PresentPro keep a copy every minute and offer it after a crash or a power cut. The Start menu has an Office group with all three.\n- Fixed: Shut down and Restart could take half a minute, and stayed at \"Please wait\u2026\" for good if a step of the desktop never finished. Every app is now asked to close at once and given 5 seconds at most, the desktop's own steps 3 seconds, and the data partition is made read-only before the power goes off. About \u203a For developers shows how long the last shutdowns took. The power button set to Shut down now does so.\n- Changed: the desktop's right-click menu says \"Open settings\u2026\" instead of \"Change background\u2026\".\n- Removed: the Cyberdeck test in About \u203a For developers.\n\n## 1.4.4 (2026-11-01)\nSummary: the volume and brightness keys work; the clock sets itself from the internet; the taskbar shows the battery, the real network and one time; Power and battery settings; eight screen savers; a tidier Settings\n- New: the laptop's volume, mute, microphone and brightness keys work (on the ASUS Vivobook Go: F1 to F5), in the desktop, in games and in Linux apps alike, with an indicator showing the new level. The taskbar's volume is now the computer's own volume, which every app follows; a brightness icon sits next to it, and Settings \u203a Volume Controls has System volume.\n- New: System Settings \u203a Power and battery: power mode (Power saver, Balanced, Performance), battery saver (on now, or by itself at 10, 20 or 30 %), turning the screen off and sleeping after a time without input, what closing the lid and pressing the power button do, and battery care (stop charging at 60 or 80 %), each for on battery and plugged in where it applies. The settings hold from the start, signed in or not.\n- New: the battery on the taskbar, next to the workspace buttons, with its level, charging, and the time left or to full. A device's battery that reports -1 % is no longer listed.\n- New: Display Properties \u203a Screen saver: on or off, start after 1 to 60 minutes, eight styles (Starfield, Clock, Rudolf, Code rain, Mystify, Bubbles, Aurora, Fireworks) with a preview, the time over it, and the password when it ends. It is drawn by the processor, and it no longer starts while a game or a Linux app is being used.\n- Fixed: the clock could go back to 2016 after a restart (when the computer's own clock lost its time), and then no secure connection worked: no update check, no development link. Rudolf OS now never starts earlier than its release date, and sets the clock from the internet by itself at every start, when the network comes up and every 12 hours, keeping the result in the computer's clock.\n- Fixed: the calendar, the lock screen and the widgets showed another time than the taskbar (the browser's own time zone instead of the one set in System Settings). They all show the same time and date now.\n- Fixed: the network icon said \"Ethernet \u2014 connected\" on a laptop on Wi-Fi. It shows the real connection now: Wi-Fi with the network's name and signal, or the cable with its speed.\n- Fixed: the pointer speed in Mouse and touchpad was forgotten at every restart.\n- Changed: Settings is tidier. Mouse and touchpad has every pointer setting (one pointer speed, also for games); the language and the internet clock are in System Settings \u203a Date, time and language; web search and downloads are on the Oracle page; Accessibility is grouped as Vision, Hearing and Voice control; the Cyberdeck test and updating the desktop page from a file are in About \u203a For developers. Quick settings keeps its switches; volume, brightness and the battery have their own taskbar icons.\n- Removed: unused code and programs, and two Accessibility settings that did nothing (natural scrolling and horizontal scroll speed; natural scrolling is in Mouse and touchpad).\n- Changed (1.4.4 update): the taskbar has one network icon. Its menu now also shows the Wi-Fi networks in range and joins one, as the separate Wi-Fi icon did; that icon is gone.\n- Fixed (1.4.4 update): on ASUS laptops F1 to F5 needed Fn for the volume and brightness (the keyboard started with Fn lock on). They now work on their own, as in Windows; Fn with them gives F1 to F5, and Fn+Esc switches.\n- New (1.4.4 update): a picture of your own as the desktop background. Display Properties \u203a Picture: choose one from your files or from this computer, and how it fills the screen (fill, fit, stretch, centre or tile). It is kept with your settings, so it stays after a restart even if the file is moved or deleted.\n\n## 1.4.3 (2026-10-30)\nSummary: games and apps that draw with OpenGL through X11, such as Oolite, start again\n- Fixed: apps and games that draw with OpenGL in an X11 window (Oolite among them, and many games made with SDL) stopped as soon as they started, because the display server offered no OpenGL to them (\"GLX is not supported\"). It now does, with the computer's own graphics driver.\n\n## 1.4.2 (2026-10-30)\nSummary: Google sign-in works in Oracle again; fixes 1.4.1 starting without its drivers (no Wi-Fi, slow screen) when an older Rudolf OS USB stick with its own files started the computer; the Rudolf OS boot entry works on more computers\n- Fixed: on a computer started through an older Rudolf OS USB stick that keeps its own files, the 1.4.1 update could start with the stick's older system instead of its own. Its drivers were then refused (each release signs its own), so there was no Wi-Fi and the screen was drawn without the graphics card, slowly. The update now starts only with its own system, from whichever drive holds it.\n- Fixed: Google refused to sign in from Oracle (\"This browser or app may not be secure\"), because Oracle's tabs said they were controlled by automation software. They no longer do.\n- Fixed: on some computers (the ASUS Vivobook Go among them) the firmware skipped the \"Rudolf OS\" boot entry and started the drive through another entry, such as one made in its setup. Rudolf OS now takes the path of the entry that started it from the drive, so its own entry works on the next start.\n\n## 1.4.1 (2026-10-28)\nSummary: a computer where Rudolf OS is installed starts from its own drive, without a USB stick (after Get new boot files, or a new Install here)\n- Fixed: some computers (the ASUS Vivobook Go among them) could not start Rudolf OS from the drive it was installed on, only from a USB stick. Install here copied the disc image's partition table, which suits a stick but which a computer's firmware takes for an invalid table on an internal drive. Install here and Get new boot files (Settings \u203a Security \u203a Secure Boot) now give an internal drive a table the firmware accepts, a disk ID of its own, and a \"Rudolf OS\" entry first in the computer's boot order. An installed drive keeps its files; USB sticks are made as before.\n- Fixed: the development link ran every earlier command again when it was switched on with another data partition (for example on a USB stick). Commands that already have a result are not run again.\n\n## 1.4.0 (2026-10-27)\nSummary: the development link: let a developer you choose run tests on this computer through a private GitHub repository you own, while you watch and allow each command\n- New: Settings \u203a Security \u203a Development link. While it is on, a developer you choose (for example a Claude session) can run tests on this computer: run commands with full control, take screenshots, send files from it and bring files to it, and restart it. Commands and results go through a private GitHub repository you own; only people and sessions you give that repository to can use it.\n- It is off unless you switch it on, which asks for an administrator's password. It ends by itself after 1, 2, 4 or 8 hours, and stays on across a restart only if you tick that. A red banner across the top of the screen shows while it is on, with the time left and End now.\n- Ask first (on unless you untick it): every command waits on the screen, with its full text, until you press Allow or Refuse.\n- The GitHub token you paste is checked with GitHub (a private repository it can write to), kept where only the system can read it and never shown again; revoking it on GitHub ends all access. Files in home folders are not sent unless you allow it.\n- Settings shows a log of every command, its result and whether you allowed it (30 days). The system report says whether the link is on.\n- Use it on a test computer only, never on one with personal files.\n\n## 1.3.1 (2026-10-27)\nSummary: fixes 1.3.0 running an older Rudolf OS USB stick's system and files, instead of the computer's own, on computers started through such a stick\n- Fixed: on a computer where Rudolf OS is installed but which starts through an older Rudolf OS USB stick that keeps its own files (for example because the computer's firmware does not start its internal drive), the 1.3.0 update ran the stick's older system and files. The desktop then said, for example, \"Rudolf OS 1.1\", and Wi-Fi and settings were the stick's. The update now always runs from the drive that holds it, with that drive's files, whatever drive the computer started from. Nothing on either drive was changed or erased.\n\n## 1.3.0 (2026-10-26)\nSummary: App Store \u203a Games lists every game on Flathub, with search and genres; a USB stick uses its own files on a computer where Rudolf OS is installed; crashes are reported as crashes; the Linux password can be changed\n- Fixed: a Rudolf OS USB stick started on a computer where Rudolf OS is installed could run from the computer's own drive instead, with its files (on a laptop with an NVMe drive it usually did, because that drive is ready first). Rudolf OS now starts from the kind of drive the computer's firmware started (a USB stick or an internal drive), and uses that drive's files when it has its own; otherwise it works as before.\n- New: when a part of Rudolf OS crashes during a task (as the app list reader did in 1.2.8), the task says so, with Report a problem, instead of guessing (\"Is this computer connected to the internet?\"). The system report lists such crashes.\n- New: Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Linux \u203a Change the Linux password\u2026 sets a new password for Debian (the one sudo asks for). Before, it could only be chosen when Debian was set up.\n- Changed: updates rebuild every Rudolf OS program, so fixes to the package tool, the assistant and printing reach installed computers too.\n- New: App Store \u203a Games lists every game in Flathub's app list under \"All games on Flathub\", with a search box, genres (action, arcade, board, emulators, puzzles, role-playing, simulation, sports, strategy and more) and \"Verified only\". Installed games come first.\n- Fixed: Linux apps \u203a Games left out games that list only their genre (for example \"Action\") and not \"Game\".\n- Fixed: Command Prompt said \"Rudolf OS [Version 1.1]\" (when it opens and for VER), and Lua's sys.version() said \"Rudolf OS 1.0\", whatever the version. Both now give the version that is installed.\n\n## 1.2.9 (2026-10-26)\nSummary: the App Store's Linux app list downloads again; signing in to Roblox in Sober (and web pages inside other Linux apps) works\n- Fixed: Sober could not sign in to Roblox: its sign-in window stayed blank with \"No such interface org.freedesktop.portal.ProxyResolver\", then \"Something went wrong\". Linux apps from the App Store ask Rudolf OS which proxy to use for each address; Rudolf OS now answers (its own proxy from Settings \u203a Network, or none), and says whether the computer is online.\n- Fixed: App Store \u203a Linux apps \u203a Download the app list stopped with \"Segmentation fault\" and \"Is this computer connected to the internet?\". Flathub's catalogue has empty entries (such as an empty paragraph), and the program that reads it crashed on them.\n- Fixed: release updates now rebuild the desktop portal and the app catalogue reader for Linux apps too (before, only a full build did).\n\n## 1.2.8 (2026-10-26)\nSummary: the Linux terminal and App Store apps work on computers running an installed update; new boot files for an installed computer without erasing it (Secure Boot on computers installed from an older disc); webcams work, with a camera switch in Settings \u203a Privacy; the hardware check tests the keyboard, touchpad, camera, battery and screen\n- Fixed: the Linux terminal (Debian) could not be set up or started, with \"the Debian sandbox did not start: mounting /proc failed (Operation not permitted)\", on every computer running an installed update (since 1.2.3). The start-up options were mounted over /proc/cmdline there, and the kernel then refuses a sandbox its own /proc. Flatpak apps from the App Store were refused the same way. Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Linux \u203a Check now tests this too (\"A new /proc for sandboxes\").\n- New: Settings \u203a Security \u203a Secure Boot \u203a Get new boot files: a computer installed from a disc older than 1.2.2 can start with Secure Boot on without being reinstalled. Download the boot image (rudolf-os-X.Y.Z-boot.iso and its .sha256) from the Rudolf OS releases; Rudolf OS checks it, keeps a copy, and writes it at the next start in front of your files, which stay as they are. The installed system then starts from the installed update.\n- New: Install here \u203a Replace the boot files only: the same for another drive Rudolf OS is installed on, from a USB stick, for example to repair one whose boot files were damaged.\n- New: the disc releases have a boot image (rudolf-os-X.Y.Z-boot.iso, about 27 MB) next to the disc image.\n- New: webcams work (USB Video Class, built-in laptop cameras included), in the Camera app, in websites (which still ask first) and in Linux apps.\n- New: Settings \u203a Privacy \u203a Camera: one switch that turns the camera off for everything; its driver is not loaded until it is switched on again, also after a restart.\n- New: the hardware check (Task Manager \u203a Hardware) has five more steps: every key of the keyboard (as laid out in the chosen layout), the touchpad or mouse (moving, both buttons, scrolling), the camera, the battery's health (its capacity compared with new, and its charge cycles) and the screen (one colour at a time, to spot dead pixels).\n- Changed: a factory reset of a drive that has boot files only keeps its installed system (everything else is erased as before).\n\n## 1.2.7 (2026-10-26)\nSummary: a disc image downloaded in parts can be made into a USB stick without joining it by hand; making a stick over an older Rudolf OS stick works again; a check for Linux apps and the terminal; more USB Wi-Fi adapters work; a power button on the login screen; Luau and Pilot.lua checks are ready straight away; Files copies whole folders from a USB drive\n- New: Make a USB stick and Install here accept a Rudolf OS disc image downloaded from GitHub in parts (rudolf-os-X.Y.Z.iso.part00, part01, ...). The parts are joined in order, checked against the .sha256 downloaded with them, and then written; a missing, short or damaged part is named and nothing is written. The joined image stays in Downloads.\n- Fixed: Make a USB stick refused to erase a stick that held an older Rudolf OS (\"a file on it is still open\"). (1.2.6)\n- Fixed: Install here and Make a USB stick with a downloaded disc image stopped at once without a message, and wrote nothing. (1.2.4)\n- Changed: downloads are in one place, C:\\DOWNLOADS, which Files' \"Downloads\" now opens. Large files are kept on the disk and shown there.\n- Changed: the disc files of a Rudolf OS release (the .iso, its parts and .sha256) downloaded from the Rudolf OS releases on GitHub are no longer blocked as unknown or program files. The same names from anywhere else still are.\n- New: Settings \u203a Security \u203a Secure Boot, Make a USB stick and Install here say when this Rudolf OS started from boot files older than Secure Boot support (a disc before 1.2.2), and how to get newer ones: make a stick from a newer disc image.\n- New: Settings \u203a Apps \u203a Linux \u203a Check: what Linux apps and the terminal need from this computer (the data partition, temporary files, user namespaces, Flatpak's folders, the Debian sandbox), with what is wrong. It is also in the system report. When the Debian sandbox does not start, it now says which step failed and why.\n- Fixed: Flatpak apps could not be installed (\"open(O_TMPFILE): No such file or directory\") when Flatpak's repository had lost its tmp folder; it is made again at start-up. /var/tmp is made at start-up too.\n- New: more USB Wi-Fi adapters work: Atheros AR9271 and AR7010 (TP-Link TL-WN722N v1 and many others), MediaTek MT7601U, MT7610U and MT7612U (among them Alfa AWUS036ACM). Plug one in and it appears in Settings \u203a Network.\n- Changed: Blitzen and Code Editor get the Luau type checker ready in the background as soon as Luau or Pilot.lua is chosen, or a .LUAU file is opened, so the first check no longer waits for it to load (up to two minutes on a slower computer). Switching between Luau, Roblox and Pilot.lua scripts no longer loads it again.\n- New: the login screen has a power button (bottom right) to shut down, restart or put the computer to sleep without signing in.\n- New: Files \u203a a USB drive or memory card \u203a Copy into Rudolf OS copies whole folders, with everything in them, into Downloads. It shows how far it has got and can be cancelled; files over 256 MB and files that cannot be read are left out and listed; a folder that does not fit is refused before anything is copied.\n\n## 1.2.6 (2026-10-25)\nSummary: Check this disc no longer reports a good disc as damaged; Make a Rudolf OS USB stick works with a stick that is already formatted\n- Fixed: \"Check this disc or USB drive for errors\" (in the start menu) said every disc and stick was damaged, because of one file (boot/rboot.bin) that is changed while the disc image is written. A 1.2.6 disc or stick passes. The result now says how many files were checked and names the damaged ones (Settings \u203a Update and recovery shows the last result).\n- Fixed: on a computer with an update installed, the check ran twice (once before the update started, once after) and the first result was lost. It now runs once. A disc or stick made before 1.2.6 still runs its own check first and shows the false error there; the second check, from this update, gives the right answer and is the one Settings shows.\n- Fixed: Make a Rudolf OS USB stick and Install here refused a USB stick that Rudolf OS had opened by itself when it was plugged in (any stick formatted in the shop), with \"is in use. Unmount its partitions first\". Rudolf OS now closes the drive itself, and nothing on it is opened again while it is written. If a file on it is still open, the message says to close File Explorer or the app using it.\n- Changed: the erase question before making a stick names what is on the drive now (its name and format).\n\n## 1.2.5 (2026-10-23)\nSummary: make a Rudolf OS USB stick from Rudolf OS; check this computer's hardware; apps over Wi-Fi; a Games page; Restart into Windows; Blitzen writes Luau and Pilot.lua\n- New: Blitzen has three more languages. Lua is plain Lua 5.3, without the BlockLua rules. Luau is Roblox's Lua (types, continue, +=, string interpolation): Blitzen checks the types with Luau's own checker, then runs the program on this computer. Roblox game scripts are checked but not run, since they need Roblox. Pilot.lua is for Waste of Space microcontrollers: every part call is checked against Pilot.lua's definitions, then the program runs with stand-in parts that say what they were asked to do.\n- New: when Luau code has a type error, Blitzen sees the error and its line and fixes the program, as it already did for programs that fail when run.\n- New: Code Editor opens .LUAU files and runs them (Luau and Pilot.lua), and \"Ask Blitzen\" picks Luau or Pilot.lua for them.\n- New: Linux apps and Roblox (Sober) install over Wi-Fi, not only over a network cable. Before a download of more than 500 MB over Wi-Fi the App Store says how much it is and how much space is free, and asks.\n- New: Settings \u203a Network \u203a Saved networks: mark a network Metered (a phone hotspot, a capped plan) and the App Store asks before any download over 50 MB on it.\n- New: an app download that stops (the Wi-Fi drops, the router restarts) is tried again by itself, and Get continues where it stopped instead of starting over.\n- New: App Store \u203a Games: Roblox, Steam, Minecraft (Prism Launcher), RetroArch and the games built into Rudolf OS, each with its download size and whether it needs the graphics card. When Rudolf OS draws with the processor, the page says which games will be slow and why.\n- New: Settings \u203a Storage and backup \u203a Make a Rudolf OS USB stick: puts this Rudolf OS (with its installed updates), or a downloaded image, on a USB stick or memory card of 2 GB or more. The copy is read back and checked, the stick keeps files from its first start, and it can take your files and settings with it. No Windows, Rufus or joining of parts is needed any more.\n- New: Back up to a USB stick (Settings \u203a Storage and backup): the backup is written straight to a plugged-in drive, with a checksum, and read back. On a new Rudolf OS, the first-start screen has Restore from a backup on a USB stick, which finds it there and refuses a damaged one.\n- Changed: Install here reads the copy back and compares it before it finishes.\n- New: Task Manager \u203a Hardware \u203a Check this computer\u2026: a guided check of about a minute: sound (a tone: did you hear it?), microphone (a level meter), network (wired speed with the cable hint, Wi-Fi networks in range, a download from the update server), USB (plug in a stick), graphics (what draws the screen, frames per second) and, if you like, sleep for 10 seconds. The results go into the system report, so a problem report shows them (nothing personal is in it).\n- Fixed: joining a Wi-Fi network for the first time after starting could fail with \"out of range, or the password is wrong\" on slower computers, because Rudolf OS gave up after 20 seconds while it was still scanning. It now waits up to a minute.\n- New: Restart into Windows (in the Power dialog and in Settings \u203a Update and recovery) on a computer that also has Windows: the computer starts Windows Boot Manager once, then comes back to the Rudolf OS menu as before. It works with Secure Boot on.\n- New: Settings \u203a Update and recovery \u203a Boot menu: choose what the UEFI boot menu starts when nobody chooses (for example Start from memory) and how long it waits. The choice is kept in the computer's firmware, so the disc or stick is not changed.\n- Updated: Chromium 156 and Linux 6.18.58 (security fixes).\n\n## 1.2.4 (2026-10-19)\nSummary: a USB stick written from the image can keep your files; Secure Boot key enrolment works on more computers; clearer graphics and network status\n- New: a USB stick written with Rufus or Etcher can keep your files, settings and apps. After you sign in, Rudolf OS offers to use the stick's free space (Settings \u203a Storage and backup \u203a Keep files on this drive); restart once and everything you made so far moves onto it. App Store apps such as Roblox (Sober) need this.\n- Fixed: on some computers (ASUS among them) the blue Secure Boot screen refused rudolf.cer with \"Unsupported Format\". The boot files now carry their lowercase names. On older discs, Settings \u203a Security Settings \u203a Prepare Secure Boot always works.\n- New: \"Install here\u2026\" can install a newer disc image you downloaded (Install from), so an older stick can install a Secure Boot ready system. Only images signed by Rudolf OS are accepted.\n- New: Task Manager \u203a System and the system report say what draws the screen (the graphics card and its name, or the processor), what web pages are drawn with, and the NVIDIA GSP firmware state.\n- New: a wired network running slower than its chip (100 Mb/s on gigabit Ethernet) is pointed out, with a hint about the cable or router port.\n- New: AMD processors get AMD's latest microcode at every start (security fixes such as SRSO), without a BIOS update.\n- New: the disc download has join.ps1 for Windows: it joins the parts, checks them and names a missing or damaged part. JOIN-PARTS.TXT has PowerShell commands, and warns never to keep the image on the stick being written.\n- Changed: Settings \u203a Update and recovery says when an update has been kept after a successful start.\n- Changed: the README covers the UEFI boot menu, keeping files, installing from a downloaded image and what to do when Windows says the stick is not accessible.\n\n## 1.2.3 (2026-10-19)\nSummary: graphics acceleration for NVIDIA GeForce 20, 30 and 40 series cards, and fixes found on a real computer\n- New: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 20, 30 and 40 series (and GTX 16) cards draw with the graphics card instead of the processor. Rudolf OS now carries NVIDIA's GSP firmware and Mesa's NVK driver, which these cards need for any acceleration with the open driver. The first start after the update can take a few seconds longer while the card starts its firmware. If the card cannot use it, Rudolf OS draws with the processor as before.\n- New: the firmware for Realtek Ethernet chips (the r8169 driver) is included.\n- Fixed: Task Manager \u203a System \u203a Installed release and the system report now show the release that is running (version, serial, channel, slot).\n- Fixed: Task Manager says \"USB drive\" or \"SD card\" when Rudolf OS started from one; it said \"disc\" for a disc image written to a USB stick.\n- Fixed: saving a window as PDF no longer turns dashes, quotes and table columns into \"?\".\n- Fixed: the start-up options in Task Manager and the system report no longer list the same options twice.\n- Note: the update is larger than usual (about 75 MB) because of the NVIDIA firmware.\n\n## 1.2.2 (2026-10-18)\nSummary: Rudolf OS starts with Secure Boot on: enroll its key once, and the computer starts only signed, checked software\n- New: Secure Boot. UEFI computers no longer need Secure Boot turned off. The first time, a blue screen asks to enroll the Rudolf OS key (Enroll key from disk \u203a EFI \u203a rudolf \u203a rudolf.cer); after that Rudolf OS starts as before. Already running Rudolf OS? Settings \u203a Security Settings \u203a Prepare Secure Boot does it with a one-time password, then turn Secure Boot on in the firmware settings.\n- New: UEFI computers have a boot menu: Start, Recovery mode, Check this disc or USB drive, Start from memory, and Debug start (only with Secure Boot off).\n- Security: every system file is checked as it is read (dm-verity) against the signed release list, with Secure Boot on or off; a damaged or changed layer is caught.\n- Security: with Secure Boot on, the kernel is locked down: only signed drivers and signed updated kernels start, and nothing can write to kernel memory. An update whose files fail the check is dropped and the previous system starts.\n- New: Task Manager \u203a System and the system report show whether Secure Boot is on, the lockdown and whether the system layers were checked.\n- Note: to use Secure Boot, write the 1.2.2 disc image to the USB stick or disc; an older stick keeps working with Secure Boot off. Computers that started in BIOS (legacy) mode are not affected.\n\n## 1.2.1 (2026-10-18)\nSummary: download files of any size from websites, up to 4 GB by default; large files stay on the disk and open from C:\\DOWNLOADS\n- New: downloads are no longer limited to 1.5 MB. Files up to 1.5 MB go into C:\\DOWNLOADS as before; larger ones are kept on the disk (in the Downloads folder that Linux apps see) and C:\\DOWNLOADS shows them with \ud83d\udcbd and their real size.\n- New: videos, music, pictures and PDFs kept on the disk open in Rudolf OS and are read in pieces, so a large video starts at once. Text files up to 4 MB open read-only. Other kinds of large files can be opened in Linux apps.\n- New: Settings > Network > Downloads > Largest download (100 MB to 16 GB, 4 GB at first). about:downloads shows the progress of a download and what was checked.\n- New: Settings > Storage lists the downloads kept on the disk and how much space is free. Rudolf OS warns when the disk is over 90% full; running live, downloads are kept in memory until you shut down.\n- New: PDF, ZIP and other archives, more video and audio formats, fonts and subtitles are recognized file types.\n- Security: every download still goes through the file-type rules and the antivirus. For files over 8 MB the antivirus checks the first and last 4 MB, and about:downloads says so. Program files wait in a quarantine folder until an administrator approves them. Files kept on the disk are never run as pages of the desktop.\n- Changed: renaming or deleting a large download in C:\\DOWNLOADS does the same on the disk (deleting it keeps it in the Trash until the Trash is emptied). Large downloads cannot be copied elsewhere in C: (files up to 1.5 MB can). Backups contain a note of them, not the files.\n- Changed: Chromium's own Safe Browsing check of downloads is off; it needed a connection to Google and held downloads in place. Rudolf OS's antivirus checks them.\n\n## 1.2.0 (2026-10-18)\nSummary: Oracle's tabs are real browser tabs, so signing in to websites works inside Oracle; a new Task Manager shows the kernel, the services and the hardware; report a problem with the computer's status in one step\n- New: Oracle opens websites as real browser tabs. Signing in works inside Oracle for every site (Google, Microsoft, banks\u2026), pop-up sign-ins keep working, and each Rudolf OS account has its own cookies. The browser window for sign-in sites is no longer needed.\n- New: Rudolf OS moves to its new desktop host by itself once, shortly after starting (a few seconds; your files, ROMs, game saves and settings move along). If it cannot start, the desktop runs as before. Settings > Oracle sign-in > Real browser tabs turns it off.\n- New: downloads from websites go to C:\\DOWNLOADS through the firewall, file-type and antivirus checks, also files that need you to be signed in.\n- New: a website or a video can go fullscreen; Esc brings it back. Games can capture the mouse pointer.\n- Fixed: Ctrl+W, Ctrl+Q, Ctrl+T and Ctrl+N could close or quit the desktop or open a browser window over it. Ctrl+W now closes an Oracle tab, Ctrl+T opens one, and Ctrl+Q does nothing harmful. Linux apps get these keys unchanged.\n- New: Task Manager: Processes (every process grouped by what it is, with CPU and memory, and End process), Performance (CPU, memory and network), System (Rudolf OS, the desktop's host, the services with Restart, the kernel, its drivers and warnings), Hardware (processor, graphics, display, storage, network adapters, sound, input devices, battery). Apps lists Oracle's real tabs with the memory each page uses.\n- New: Report a problem (Task Manager, or Start): opens a ready-filled report on the Rudolf OS issue tracker on GitHub, with the state of the computer at that moment. It leaves out your name, files, web and network addresses and Wi-Fi names; you review it before sending, or save it to send later.\n- Security: ending a system process or restarting a service asks for an administrator's password; the processes Rudolf OS needs cannot be ended. Websites in Oracle keep their own protection against being shown inside other pages.\n- Fixed: with the newer Chromium that security updates bring, Rudolf OS's browser settings were not applied, so the system's own file dialog (which froze some PCs) could open again, and real tabs could not start. Both work again.\n- Known limits: a few websites forbid the script that makes fullscreen work and stay in their window; downloads are limited to 1.5 MB as before.\n\n## 1.1.2 (2026-10-13)\nSummary: signing in to websites works (Oracle moves sign-in pages to a real browser window); choose the screen resolution and frame rate; fixes for small screens and display scaling\n- Fixed: signing in to websites in Oracle did not last: the site accepted the password, then asked again. Oracle shows websites inside the desktop, where Chromium drops their sign-in cookies. Sites that need a sign-in now move to a real browser window (\ud83c\udf10 in the taskbar switches between it and the desktop).\n- New: Oracle notices when a page asks for a password and offers to open it in the browser window, or does it by itself when you send a sign-in or press \"Sign in with Google / Microsoft / Apple\u2026\". Nothing you type is read.\n- New: Settings > Oracle sign-in: your sign-in sites, and whether Oracle asks first. Google, Microsoft, Yahoo, banks and payment sites always open in the browser window.\n- Security: sign-in sites, banks and payment sites keep their own protection against being shown inside other pages.\n- New: Flash content plays in the browser window too (Ruffle).\n- New: Settings > Display > Resolution: Automatic, 1920\u00d71080, 1366\u00d7768, 1280\u00d7720 or 1024\u00d7576. On a screen of another shape the picture keeps its shape, with black bars, and is scaled smoothly; touchscreens still touch the right spot.\n- New: Settings > Display > Frame rate: 120, 90, 60 or 30 fps. 90 and 120 need a monitor that offers them and the graphics-card display; 30 saves power.\n- New: after changing the resolution or frame rate, \"Keep this display setting?\" \u2014 the previous setting comes back by itself after 15 seconds, or after 90 seconds if the screen shows nothing.\n- Fixed: with display scaling above 100%, the Start menu ran off the top of the screen, and windows, the Calculator and the file picker could be larger than small screens such as 1024\u00d7576.\n\n## 1.1.1 (2026-10-13)\nSummary: choosing files no longer freezes some PCs; laptop touchpads work; plugged-in devices are recognised and USB drives open in File Explorer\n- Fixed: choosing a file (Files > Upload, Open a file, From computer, Import) froze the whole screen on some PCs, so the computer had to be switched off. Rudolf OS now uses its own file picker, which lists Downloads, Documents, Pictures and every drive.\n- Fixed: the touchpads of most laptops did not work, because drivers they need were missing (Intel LPSS, the Intel and AMD GPIO controllers, ELAN, Synaptics and Elantech).\n- New: touchpad gestures: tap to click (two fingers for a right click, three for a middle click), two-finger scrolling up, down and sideways, and a right click in a clickpad's bottom-right corner. Taps are ignored while you type.\n- New: Settings > Mouse and touchpad: pointer speed, acceleration, tap to click, natural scrolling and ignoring the touchpad while typing.\n- New: plugged-in devices are recognised. A notification names each USB device (keyboard, mouse, game controller, camera, audio device, printer, Wi-Fi adapter and more) and says when Rudolf OS has no driver for it.\n- New: USB drives and SD cards open by themselves in File Explorer's Drives list; the notification opens them. \u23cf Eject makes a drive safe to unplug.\n- New: Settings > Devices lists the connected drives, USB devices, keyboards, mice and touchpads.\n- New: after an update, a notification tells you what changed, and \"What's new\" shows every version's changes.\n- Fixed: game controllers no longer move the mouse pointer.\n- Security: drives are mounted without device files, set-user-ID or programs run from them, and websites cannot open the system's file dialog.\n\n## 1.1 (2026-10-11)\nSummary: the first public release\n- Boots on real computers: Rudolf Boot (BIOS) and UEFI, from DVD or USB; install to disk with saved data; recovery mode, updates with rollback, factory reset.\n- Task Manager, App Store with sandboxed apps, Code Editor, Image and PDF viewers, Screenshot and screen recording, Calendar, Clock, Spreadsheet, Presentations, Contacts, Weather, Characters & Emoji, Event Viewer, Camera, Voice Recorder, Font Viewer, Developer Console.\n- Alt+Tab, workspaces, overview, Start search, notification centre, quick settings, calendar flyout, pinned and grouped taskbar buttons, desktop files and widgets, resize from any edge.\n- New File Explorer with tabs, split view, preview, thumbnails, search inside files, undo, ZIP, Open with and drives.\n- Encrypted home folders, stronger passwords, lock screen, administrator prompts, guest account and parental controls, backups.\n- Screen reader, magnifier, dark and high-contrast themes, scaling, keyboard and mouse assistance, input methods, Arabic and Hebrew.\n- Emu (Super Nintendo and Nintendo DS), Blitzen (offline coding assistant), Linux apps from Flathub and a Debian 13 terminal.\n";
